const dishRecipes = {
"afghan|Ashak": {"time":"About 1 hour 30 minutes","steps":["Knead the flour and water to a firm dough and rest it.","Chop the leek very fine. Squeeze out as much liquid as you can. Wet leek bursts the dumpling.","Roll the dough thin, cut rounds, fill with leek, and fold into half-moons. Pinch the edges hard.","Boil the ashak in plenty of water until they float and the dough is cooked, about 6 to 8 minutes. Lift them out gently.","Brown the minced beef. Serve the dumplings with the beef, the yogurt, and the tomato sauce, all three on the plate."]},
"afghan|Aush": {"time":"About 1 hour 15 minutes","steps":["Soak the kidney beans and chickpeas overnight. Boil them together until soft, about 40 minutes. Keep the cooking liquid.","Slice the onion. Heat the oil and fry half the onion until brown. Add the minced beef and half the salt and cook until the meat is brown and dry. Add the chopped tomato and cook to a thick sauce. This is the meat topping.","Crush the garlic with a little salt and stir it into the yogurt with half the dried mint. Keep this cold.","Cook the wheat noodles in the bean liquid, adding water if you need it, until just tender. The broth should be soupy, not a dry noodle dish.","Stir the beans and chickpeas back through the noodles. Taste the salt.","Ladle the noodles and beans into bowls. Spoon on the cold garlic yogurt, then the hot minced beef, and finish with the rest of the mint. Aush is eaten with all three in one spoon: noodle, yogurt and meat."]},
"afghan|Bolani": {"time":"About 1 hour","steps":["Mix the flour and water to a soft dough. Knead 5 minutes and rest 20 minutes.","Boil and mash the potato. Mix it with the chopped green onion and cilantro. The filling should be dry enough to spread.","Divide the dough and roll thin rounds. Spread filling on one half, fold over, and press the edges shut with a fork.","Cook on a hot, lightly oiled tawa until both sides are spotted and crisp, about 3 minutes a side.","Cut into wedges. Serve hot. The green onion should still taste fresh."]},
"afghan|Borani Banjan": {"time":"About 45 minutes","steps":["Slice the eggplant into rounds about 1 cm thick. Salt them and leave 15 minutes, then pat dry. This draws out the bitter water.","Crush the garlic with a pinch of the salt and stir it into the yogurt with the dried mint. This is the borani. It should be thick, not a drink.","Heat the oil and fry the eggplant until both sides are deep gold and the centres are soft. Drain. Do not skip the colour; pale eggplant tastes raw under the yogurt.","In the same oil, fry the sliced onion until gold. Add the chopped tomato, the rest of the salt and the chilli. Cook to a thick sauce, about 8 minutes.","Layer the eggplant with the tomato sauce in the pan. Add two spoons of water, cover, and simmer 8 minutes so the eggplant drinks the sauce.","Spread the garlic yogurt over the hot eggplant, or serve it in a cold spoon beside. Do not boil the yogurt on the eggplant or it splits. Borani banjan is eaten warm, the yogurt still cool."]},
"afghan|Do Piaza": {"time":"About 1 hour 20 minutes","steps":["Do piaza means two onions: one fried, one added later so it stays sweet. Slice all the onion, but keep the two halves apart. Cut the lamb into large pieces.","Heat the oil and fry the first half of the onion slowly until it is deep brown. This takes patience, about 15 minutes. Pale onion will not colour the gravy.","Add the crushed garlic and the lamb. Brown the meat. Add the salt and black pepper.","Add a cup of hot water, cover, and simmer until the lamb is nearly tender, about 40 minutes. The gravy should reduce, not stay thin.","Add the second half of the onion and the slit green chillies. Cook uncovered 15 minutes until this onion is soft and sweet but not brown, and the sauce clings to the meat.","Finish with the coriander. You should taste both onions: the dark fried one and the soft fresh one. That is the dish."]},
"afghan|Firni": {"time":"About 30 minutes, plus chilling","steps":["Whisk the rice flour with a cup of the cold milk until there are no lumps. A lump becomes a dumpling in the pot.","Heat the rest of the milk with the sugar and the cardamom pods, stirring so the sugar dissolves. When it steams, pour in the rice-flour milk, whisking.","Cook on medium-low, stirring constantly, until the firni thickens enough to coat the back of a spoon and a finger drawn across it leaves a clean line, about 10 minutes. Do not boil it hard or it turns gluey.","Take it off the heat. Stir in the rose water. Fish out the cardamom pods.","Pour into small bowls. Chop the pistachios and scatter them on top while the surface is still soft so they stick.","Cool, then chill at least 2 hours. Firni should be trembling and smooth, sweet but not heavy, and smell of cardamom and rose."]},
"afghan|Kofta": {"time":"About 50 minutes","steps":["Mix the minced lamb with half the finely chopped onion, the cilantro and cumin. Knead until sticky, then roll into balls or short sausages.","Brown the kofta in a pan and set them aside.","Fry the remaining onion until soft. Add the chopped tomato and cook to a thick sauce.","Nestle the kofta into the sauce, add a splash of water, and simmer 15 minutes until the meat is cooked through and the sauce clings.","Scatter more cilantro. Serve with bread or rice."]},
"afghan|Mantu": {"time":"About 1 hour 45 minutes","steps":["Make a firm dough with the flour and water. Rest it 20 minutes.","Mix the minced lamb with the finely chopped onion. Squeeze the onion first so the filling is not wet.","Roll the dough thin, cut squares, and put a little meat in each. Bring the four corners together and pinch them into a little parcel, leaving a small opening or sealing them fully.","Steam over boiling water for 30 to 40 minutes, until the dough is glossy and the lamb is cooked.","Serve with the yogurt spooned over and the tomato sauce warmed and ladled alongside. Both sauces belong on the plate."]},
"afghan|Qabuli Palaw": {"time":"About 2 hours","steps":["Soak the basmati rice 30 minutes. Cut the lamb into large pieces.","Brown the lamb in a dry heavy pot, cover with water, and simmer until tender, about 1 hour. Keep the broth.","Cut the carrot into matchsticks. Cook the carrot gently in the lamb fat until sweet and soft but not mushy. Stir in the raisins for the last 2 minutes so they plump.","Parboil the rice in plenty of water until half done. Drain.","In a heavy pot, put the lamb, then the rice, then the carrot and raisins. Pour over enough lamb broth to finish the rice. Cover with a cloth under the lid.","Steam on low for 25 minutes. Rest 10 minutes. Pile onto a platter and scatter the pistachios and almonds. The carrot on top is the qabuli."]},
"afghan|Shorwa": {"time":"About 1 hour 40 minutes","steps":["Soak the chickpeas overnight and drain them. Cut the lamb into large pieces, bone in if you have it.","Put the lamb in a pot, cover with cold water by 5 cm, and bring to a boil. Skim the foam. Add half the sliced onion, the salt and black pepper. Simmer until the lamb is nearly tender, about 1 hour.","Add the soaked chickpeas after the first 20 minutes so they cook in the broth.","Cut the potato and carrot into large chunks. Chop the tomato. Add them with the rest of the onion. Simmer until the vegetables are soft and the broth tastes of lamb, about 25 minutes.","The shorwa should be a clear soup with distinct pieces, not a thick stew. If it is cloudy, you boiled it too hard: keep it at a tremble.","Scatter the coriander. Serve in deep bowls, with the meat, chickpeas and vegetables in each one."]},
"american|Apple Pie": {"time":"About 1 hour 20 minutes","steps":["Rub the cold butter into the flour and salt until pea-sized pieces remain. Add just enough ice water to make a dough. Divide in two and chill 30 minutes.","Peel and slice the apples. Toss with the sugar, cinnamon and lemon juice.","Roll one dough and line a pie dish. Fill with the apples, mounded, because they sink.","Roll the lid, cover, seal the edge and cut vents.","Bake at 200°C for 20 minutes, then at 180°C until the crust is gold and the filling bubbles through the vents, about 35 minutes more.","Rest 1 hour so the juices thicken. The apples should be tender, the bottom crust cooked through, not pale and wet."]},
"american|BBQ Ribs": {"time":"About 3 hours","steps":["Pull the membrane off the back of the ribs. Mix the paprika, brown sugar, salt, pepper and garlic powder and rub it over both sides. Rest 30 minutes.","Wrap loosely and roast at 150°C until the meat has pulled back from the bones and a skewer meets little resistance, about 2 hours 30 minutes.","Stir the ketchup, vinegar and mustard into a sauce. Warm it.","Uncover the ribs, brush with sauce, and roast at 200°C for 15 minutes until the sauce is sticky and blotched.","Rest 10 minutes. The meat should come off the bone with a tug, not fall apart into mush, and not fight you.","Slice into individual ribs. The bark should be spiced and sweet-tangy."]},
"american|Buffalo Wings": {"time":"About 45 minutes","steps":["Pat the wings dry and salt them. Toss with the flour. Moisture is what stops wings from crisping.","Heat the oil and fry the wings until the skin is deep gold and crisp and the meat is cooked, about 10 minutes. Drain on a rack.","Melt the butter with the hot sauce. Do not let it separate; keep it warm, not boiling.","Toss the hot wings in the sauce until every piece is coated.","The skin should still have crunch under the sauce. If you sauce them and wait, they soften.","Serve at once with celery sticks. Buffalo wings are salty, hot and buttery, not breaded."]},
"american|Burger": {"time":"About 30 minutes","steps":["Divide the ground beef gently into balls. Do not knead it or the burger turns dense.","Heat a pan or grill until very hot. Smash or press each ball into a thin patty and cook until a crust forms. Flip once. Lay on the American cheese so it melts.","Toast the brioche buns on the cut side.","Build: ketchup and mustard on the bun, then patty, lettuce, tomato, red onion and pickles.","Serve at once. A thin smashed burger is done in a few minutes; a thick one needs a rest so the juices settle."]},
"american|Chili": {"time":"About 1 hour 30 minutes","steps":["Heat the oil and cook the onion until soft. Add the garlic, chilli powder and cumin and fry 1 minute so the spices bloom.","Add the beef and salt and cook until brown, breaking it up. Pour off excess fat if there is a pool of it.","Add the tomato and a cup of water. Simmer 45 minutes, until the chilli is thick and the raw spice taste has gone.","Stir in the kidney beans and cook 15 minutes more. They should be tender and the sauce should cling to the meat.","Taste the salt and the heat. Add a splash of water only if it sticks.","Rest 10 minutes. Chili thickens as it sits and tastes deeper the next day."]},
"american|Clam Chowder": {"time":"About 40 minutes","steps":["Steam the clams in a covered pot with a splash of water until they open, about 6 minutes. Strain the broth and discard any clam that stayed shut. Chop the clam meat.","Dice the potato, onion and celery. Fry the bacon until the fat renders. Cook the onion and celery in that fat until soft.","Stir in the flour and butter and cook 1 minute. Add the clam broth and potato. Simmer until the potato is tender, about 12 minutes.","Add the milk and warm it. Do not boil hard or the milk splits. Add the clams just to heat through, 1 minute. Longer and they turn rubber.","Taste the salt; the clams and bacon are already salty. Add pepper.","The chowder should be creamy and thick with potato, the clams tender, the broth tasting of the sea."]},
"american|Cornbread": {"time":"About 35 minutes","steps":["Heat the oven to 200°C. Put the butter in a small tin and set it in the oven until the butter melts and the tin is hot.","Whisk the cornmeal, flour, baking powder, sugar and salt. Stir in the buttermilk and egg, then the melted butter.","Pour into the hot tin. It should sizzle. That sizzle makes the crust.","Bake until the top is gold and a skewer comes out clean, about 20 minutes.","Rest 5 minutes. Cornbread should be moist, a little sweet, with a browned edge.","Serve warm. It dries if it sits uncovered."]},
"american|Fried Chicken": {"time":"About 45 minutes, plus brining","steps":["Salt the chicken and soak it in the buttermilk for at least 2 hours. The buttermilk seasons and tenderises.","Mix the flour with paprika, pepper, garlic powder and the rest of the salt.","Lift the chicken, let it drip, and press it into the flour so the coating is shaggy and thick. Rest 10 minutes so the crust sticks.","Heat the oil to medium, about the point where a crumb of flour sizzles steadily. Fry in batches, skin side down first, until deep gold and the largest piece is cooked through, about 12 minutes, turning once.","Drain on a rack, not on paper stacked, so the crust stays crisp.","Rest 5 minutes. The crust should shatter, the meat be juicy. If the crust is pale, the oil was too cool."]},
"american|Mac & Cheese": {"time":"About 45 minutes","steps":["Boil the elbow macaroni in plenty of water until just shy of done. Drain.","Melt the butter, stir in the flour, and cook 1 minute. Whisk in the whole milk a little at a time until you have a smooth sauce. Simmer 5 minutes.","Take it off the heat and stir in the sharp cheddar and most of the Gruyère until they melt. If you boil it after the cheese goes in, it turns grainy.","Fold in the pasta. Pour into a dish. Mix the breadcrumbs with the remaining Gruyère and scatter them on top.","Bake at 190°C until the crumbs are brown and the sauce is bubbling at the edge, about 20 minutes. Rest 5 minutes."]},
"american|Pancakes": {"time":"About 25 minutes","steps":["Whisk the flour, baking powder, sugar and salt. Whisk the milk and egg, then stir them into the flour until just combined. Lumps are fine. Overmixing makes tough pancakes.","Rest 5 minutes. Melt a little butter in a pan on medium.","Pour small rounds. Cook until bubbles set on the surface and the underside is gold, then flip once.","The second side needs only a minute. Flipping more than once deflates them.","Stack and butter. They should be thick, tender and browned in spots.","Serve hot. The pan is ready when a drop of water skitters."]},
"arabic|Falafel": {"time":"About 45 minutes, plus soaking","steps":["Soak the dried chickpeas for 12 to 24 hours. Do not use cooked chickpeas; they will not hold together and they will not be fluffy. Drain them very well.","Grind them with the parsley, cilantro, onion, garlic and cumin until the mixture looks like damp couscous and holds when you squeeze it. Do not puree it to hummus.","Rest 15 minutes. Shape into balls or discs. If they crack, pulse in a spoon of water.","Heat the oil for frying to medium-hot. Fry until deep brown and crisp outside, about 4 minutes. Break one open: the centre should be moist and green, not raw and white.","Drain and serve at once, in bread if you like."]},
"arabic|Harees": {"time":"About 2 hours","steps":["Soak the wheat berries overnight. Drain.","Put the wheat and chicken in a pot, cover with water by 5 cm, and add the salt. Bring to a boil, then cook on the lowest heat, covered, for 1 hour 30 minutes, until both the wheat and the chicken are falling apart. Stir now and then and add water if it looks dry.","Pull out the bones. Beat or mash the wheat and chicken together until the harees is a smooth, thick mass. This beating is the dish. Lumpy harees is not finished.","It should be heavy and cohesive, not a soup. Add a splash of hot water only if you cannot mash it.","Warm the ghee with the cinnamon.","Serve in bowls with the cinnamon ghee on top. Harees is mild, wheat-and-chicken, meant to be filling."]},
"arabic|Jareesh": {"time":"About 1 hour 30 minutes","steps":["Cover the chicken with water, add half the onion and the salt, and simmer until tender, about 35 minutes. Shred the meat and keep the broth.","Rinse the cracked wheat. Cook it in the broth, adding more broth as it drinks it, stirring often, until it breaks down into a thick porridge, about 40 minutes.","Stir the shredded chicken back in. The jareesh should be smooth and heavy, like a savoury pudding.","Slice the rest of the onion and fry it in the ghee with the cumin until deep gold.","Spoon the onion and ghee over the porridge.","Jareesh is eaten hot, the wheat fully broken, the top fragrant with cumin and onion."]},
"arabic|Kabsa": {"time":"About 1 hour 30 minutes","steps":["Soak the rice 20 minutes. Cut the chicken into pieces. Slice the onion.","Heat the oil and brown the chicken. Set it aside. Fry the onion until gold, then the garlic, tomato, tomato paste and baharat until the oil separates, about 6 minutes.","Return the chicken. Add the pierced dried limes, salt and enough water to cover. Simmer until the chicken is tender, about 30 minutes. Lift the chicken out. Measure 700 ml of the broth for the rice.","In the pot, add the drained rice and the measured broth. Scatter the chicken on top. Cover and cook on low until the rice is done, about 18 minutes.","Rest 10 minutes. Fry the almonds in a spoon of oil until gold, and warm the raisins in the same oil for 1 minute.","Pile the rice, lay the chicken on it, and scatter almonds and raisins. Kabsa should be spiced, the rice separate and stained by the tomato, the dried lime a quiet sourness."]},
"arabic|Mandi": {"time":"About 2 hours","steps":["Rub the lamb with the salt, baharat, crushed garlic and oil. Brown it in a heavy pot.","Add the sliced onion, pierced dried limes and water to come halfway up the meat. Cover and cook until the lamb is tender, about 1 hour 15 minutes. Keep the broth.","Soak the rice 20 minutes. Bloom the saffron in a spoon of warm broth.","Parboil the rice for 5 minutes in the lamb broth, then drain. The broth should taste of lamb before the rice goes in; if it is weak, reduce it.","Put the lamb in the pot, pile the rice over it, drizzle the saffron, cover with a cloth under the lid, and steam on low for 25 minutes.","Rest 10 minutes. Mandi rice is drier and more fragrant than a saucy stew, the lamb falling apart, the dried lime in the background."]},
"arabic|Mansaf": {"time":"About 2 hours 30 minutes","steps":["Soak the jameed in warm water and break it up, or whisk it, until you have a tangy yogurt sauce. Strain out lumps.","Brown the lamb. Cover with water and simmer until tender, about 1 hour 30 minutes.","Stir some of the hot cooking liquid into the jameed, then pour it over the lamb. Simmer gently, stirring, until the sauce thickens. Do not boil hard or the yogurt will split.","Cook the basmati rice. Fry the almonds and pine nuts in the ghee until gold.","Pile the rice on a large platter, arrange the lamb on top, pour over the jameed sauce, and scatter the nuts. Mansaf is eaten with the right hand, from a shared platter."]},
"arabic|Marqooq": {"time":"About 1 hour 30 minutes","steps":["Make a firm dough with the flour, a pinch of salt and water. Rest 20 minutes. Roll it very thin and cut wide pieces. These sheets are the marqooq.","Cut the lamb into pieces. Cover with water, add the sliced onion, salt and pepper, and simmer 40 minutes.","Add the chopped tomato, potato and zucchini. Simmer 10 minutes.","Slide the dough sheets in one at a time so they do not clump. They will soften and break into wide noodles.","Cook 10 minutes more, until the dough is tender and the stew is thick.","Marqooq should be a hearty lamb and vegetable stew with soft sheets of bread cooked in it, not a dry pilaf."]},
"arabic|Mutabbaq": {"time":"About 1 hour","steps":["Mix the flour with a pinch of salt and water to a soft, elastic dough. Knead 8 minutes. Rest 20 minutes. The dough must stretch thin without tearing.","Chop the onion and leek fine. Fry them in a spoon of the oil, add the lamb, the rest of the salt and the pepper, and cook until the meat is brown and dry. Cool, then stir in the egg.","Divide the dough into 6. Roll or stretch each piece very thin.","Spread filling on one half, fold into a square parcel, and press the edges.","Cook on a hot oiled pan until both sides are blotched and crisp, about 3 minutes a side.","Mutabbaq should be flaky at the edge and juicy inside, the leek still a little sweet. Eat hot."]},
"arabic|Saleeg": {"time":"About 1 hour 20 minutes","steps":["Brown the chicken with the sliced onion, cardamom and mastic in the oil. Add water to cover and the salt. Simmer until the chicken is tender, about 35 minutes. Keep the broth.","Rinse the short-grain rice. Cook it in 400 ml of the chicken broth until it starts to soften, about 10 minutes.","Pour in the milk a little at a time, stirring, and cook on low until the rice breaks down into a thick, creamy porridge, about 20 minutes. Add more broth if it stiffens. Saleeg is not a pilaf. It should be soft enough to eat with a spoon.","The mastic and cardamom should be a quiet perfume, not a loud spice.","Taste the salt. The porridge should be rich from the milk and savoury from the chicken broth.","Serve the chicken on or beside the rice porridge, with a little of the broth if you want it looser."]},
"arabic|Sambusa": {"time":"About 1 hour","steps":["Rub a spoon of oil into the flour with a pinch of salt. Add water and knead a firm dough. Rest 20 minutes.","Fry the chopped onion in a spoon of oil, add the beef, cumin, chopped chilli and the rest of the salt, and cook until the meat is dry. Cool it.","Roll the dough thin and cut rounds or strips. Put a spoon of filling on each, fold into a triangle, and seal with water.","Heat the frying oil to medium. Fry until even gold and crisp, about 4 minutes.","Drain. The pastry should shatter, the filling moist but not leaking.","Eat hot. Sambusa is a fried meat pastry, cumin-scented, smaller and tighter than a soft bread."]},
"bangladeshi|Bhapa Pitha": {"time":"About 1 hour","steps":["Warm the date palm jaggery with a spoon of water until it melts. Stir in the grated coconut and the seeds from the green cardamom. Cook, stirring, until the filling is thick and leaves the side of the pan. Cool it. Taste: it should be deeply sweet, not burnt.","Mix the rice flour with the salt. Sprinkle on hot water and rub it through your fingers until the flour looks like damp breadcrumbs and holds when you squeeze it. Too wet and the pitha turns gluey; too dry and it cracks.","Take a small bowl or a katori. Press a layer of the damp rice flour in the bottom and up the sides. Fill with the coconut-jaggery, then cover with more rice flour and press the edge to seal.","Set the bowls in a steamer over boiling water. Steam 15 to 18 minutes, until a skewer comes out clean and the top looks set.","Brush with the ghee while they are hot. Rest 5 minutes, then turn them out. Bhapa pitha is soft the day it is made and firmer the next morning."]},
"bangladeshi|Bhuna Khichuri": {"time":"About 1 hour 15 minutes","steps":["Wash the kalijeera rice, moong dal and masoor dal together and soak them 20 minutes. Bhuna khichuri is fried before it is boiled, so do not skip the soaking or the grains stay hard.","Heat the mustard oil with the cooking oil until the mustard oil loses its raw smell. Add the cumin seeds, shah jeera, green and black cardamom, cloves, cinnamon, bay leaves and black peppercorns. Let them crackle.","Add the thinly sliced onion and fry slowly until deep brown. Stir in the ginger paste and garlic paste and cook 2 minutes. Add turmeric, red chilli powder, coriander powder and a spoon of water.","Add the diced potato and green peas and fry 4 minutes. Tip in the drained rice and dals with the salt. Fry, stirring, until every grain looks glossy and separate. This bhuna step is the flavour.","Pour in hot water to sit about 3 cm above the grains, then the whole milk. Add half the green chillies. Cover and cook on low, stirring once or twice so the bottom does not catch, until the rice and dal are soft and the khichuri is thick enough to stand a spoon in. Add a splash more hot water only if it dries out.","Finish with the ghee, roasted cumin powder, garam masala, a grating of nutmeg and the mace, kewra water, fried onions and fresh coriander. Serve with the mixed pickle and the remaining green chillies on the side."]},
"bangladeshi|Chachchari": {"time":"About 40 minutes","steps":["Cut the potato, eggplant, pumpkin and ridge gourd or beans into similar small pieces so they cook together. Eggplant can be salted for 10 minutes and squeezed if you dislike the bitterness.","Heat the mustard oil until it smokes lightly. Add the panch phoron and the dried red chillies. When they darken, add the vegetables with the turmeric, red chilli powder, salt and sugar.","Fry on medium, stirring, for 8 minutes. The vegetables should take a little colour. Chachchari is a dry dish; do not add a lot of water.","If the pan looks dry before the vegetables are cooked, sprinkle two or three spoons of water, cover, and steam on low until they are tender, about 10 minutes.","Fry the bori in a spoon of the oil until puffed and golden, or tuck them in for the last 5 minutes so they soften slightly but keep a bite.","Add the green chillies at the end. Cook uncovered for 2 minutes so the dish looks glossy, not wet. Taste for salt and the little sweetness the sugar should give."]},
"bangladeshi|Chaler Ruti": {"time":"About 40 minutes","steps":["Put the rice flour and salt in a bowl. Chop the onion and green chillies very fine and mix them through — this is a masala chaler ruti, not a plain one.","Pour in hot water a little at a time, stirring with a spoon at first so you do not burn your hand. Knead while it is still warm until you have a soft dough that does not crack. Rice flour has no gluten, so it will never feel like wheat dough.","Rub the dough with a spoon of the oil, cover, and rest 15 minutes.","Divide into 8 balls. Pat or roll each into a thin round between oiled palms or on a banana leaf or plastic sheet. If it cracks, the dough needs a spoon more water.","Heat a tawa. Cook each ruti on medium, brushing with a little oil, until brown spots appear and the edges lift, about 1 minute a side.","Stack them in a cloth so they steam and stay soft. Eat the same day with beef curry, dim bhaji or just a pinch of salt."]},
"bangladeshi|Chingri Malai Curry": {"time":"About 45 minutes","steps":["Shell the prawns, keeping the tails if you like, and devein them. Rub with half the turmeric and a little salt.","Crack the green cardamom, cloves, cinnamon and black peppercorns lightly. Heat the mustard oil until it smokes and cools a little, then fry the prawns just until they turn pink. Take them out; they will finish in the gravy.","In the same oil fry the cumin seeds, bay leaves, dry red chillies and the whole spices for half a minute. Add the sliced onion and cook slowly until soft and light gold, not dark.","Add the ginger paste and garlic paste. When the raw smell goes, stir in the remaining turmeric, cumin powder, coriander powder and Kashmiri chilli powder with a splash of water.","Pour in the thick coconut milk, sugar and the rest of the salt. Simmer gently until the gravy thickens and the oil beads at the edge, about 8 minutes. Do not boil hard or the coconut will split.","Return the prawns and the slit green chillies. Cook 4 minutes more. Finish with the garam masala off the heat. The curry should be pale, rich and lightly sweet."]},
"bangladeshi|Chomchom": {"time":"About 1 hour 30 minutes","steps":["Split the whole milk with the lemon juice the same way as for rasmalai: boil, curdle, rinse the chhana, and hang it until moist. Knead in the semolina and a teaspoon of the ghee for 10 minutes until the dough is satin-smooth.","Shape into oblongs, fatter in the middle, the classic chomchom shape. Smooth out every crack.","Boil most of the sugar with water to a syrup that coats a spoon lightly. Poach the oblongs on a medium boil for 15 to 20 minutes until they are spongy and have grown. Rest them in the syrup 10 minutes, then lift them out.","Cook the khoya with a spoon of ghee, the cardamom seeds and a little of the syrup until it is a thick paste. Slit each sweet and tuck in a little khoya, or spread it over the top.","Roll the sweets in the grated coconut while they are still tacky. Splash with rose water. Chomchom should be juicy inside, not dry. Keep them in a little of the syrup if you are not serving at once."]},
"bangladeshi|Dhakai Bakarkhani": {"time":"About 2 hours","steps":["Warm the milk with the sugar and salt until they dissolve. Cool it to lukewarm. Mix it into the maida with a spoon of the oil and knead 10 minutes to a smooth, firm dough. Cover and rest 30 minutes.","Soften the ghee so it spreads but is not melted to oil. Divide the dough into balls. Roll one thin, brush it generously with ghee, and sprinkle a pinch of the dry flour if you have dusted any. Roll it up into a tight log, then coil the log into a disc. Repeat with the rest. Rest 20 minutes. This coil is what makes the layers.","Roll each coil gently into a flat round, not so thin that the layers disappear. Brush the tops with the remaining ghee, then the kewra water, and press on the poppy seeds.","Heat a heavy tawa on medium-low. Cook each bakarkhani slowly, turning often, until both sides are deep gold and the bread is crisp at the edge and flaky inside, 6 to 8 minutes. Press lightly with a cloth so it cooks through.","Cool on a rack, not stacked while hot, or the layers steam shut. A true Dhakai bakarkhani snaps. Eat it with tea, or with korma."]},
"bangladeshi|Fuchka": {"time":"About 1 hour 15 minutes","steps":["For the shells, mix the semolina and all-purpose flour with the ajwain and a pinch of the salt. Rub in a spoon of oil, then add warm water to a stiff dough. Knead 5 minutes, cover, and rest 20 minutes.","Roll the dough thin and cut small rounds. Heat the oil for frying until a scrap of dough puffs at once. Fry the rounds a few at a time, pressing them under the oil so they balloon and turn golden and crisp. Drain. They must be hollow; if they stay flat, the oil was not hot enough.","Boil the potatoes until soft, peel and crush them, leaving a few lumps. Mix with the boiled chickpeas, finely chopped onion, green chillies, roasted cumin, chaat masala, black salt, red chilli powder, half the coriander and a pinch of the regular salt.","For the tok, soak and mash the tamarind pulp with warm water, the jaggery and a pinch of roasted cumin, black salt and red chilli. It should be sour first, then a little sweet. Strain out fibres.","To serve, crack the top of a shell, tuck in the potato filling and a few chickpeas, and flood it with the tamarind water. Finish with mint and the rest of the coriander. Eat at once, before the shell softens."]},
"bangladeshi|Haleem": {"time":"About 3 hours","steps":["Soak the cracked wheat, chana dal, masoor dal, moong dal, urad dal and rice together in plenty of water for 1 hour.","Cut the beef or mutton into small pieces. In the ghee, fry half the sliced onion until brown and set it aside with the fried onions you already have — both go on at the end.","In the same ghee fry the remaining onion until soft. Add ginger paste, garlic paste, bay leaves, cinnamon, green cardamom and cloves. Cook 2 minutes.","Add the meat, turmeric, red chilli powder, coriander powder, yogurt and salt. Fry until the yogurt dries onto the meat. Cover with hot water and simmer until the meat is tender, about 1 hour.","Drain the grains and add them to the pot with more hot water. Cook on low, stirring often so the bottom does not stick, until the wheat and dals break down into a thick porridge, 45 to 60 minutes. Mash some of the meat into the gravy with the back of the spoon. Haleem should be smooth enough to fall from a spoon in a ribbon.","Stir in the garam masala. Ladle into bowls and top with the fried onions, chopped green chillies, mint, coriander, ginger julienne and a squeeze of lemon."]},
"bangladeshi|Hilsa Curry": {"time":"About 40 minutes","steps":["Cut the cleaned hilsa into steaks. Rub them with half the turmeric and a pinch of salt and leave them 10 minutes. Hilsa is oily; do not wash it again after this.","Heat the mustard oil until it just smokes, then take it off the heat for a minute so it loses its raw bite. Fry the fish gently until the skin sets, about a minute a side. Lift it out before it breaks.","In the same oil, fry the sliced onion until soft and pale gold. Add the garlic and ginger, chopped fine, and cook until the raw smell goes.","Stir in the remaining turmeric, red chilli powder, coriander powder and cumin powder with a spoon of water so the spices do not burn. Add the chopped tomato and salt and cook until the tomato melts into the oil.","Slide the fish back in with the green chillies and just enough hot water to come halfway up the steaks. Simmer gently, without stirring hard, for 8 to 10 minutes.","Finish with the fresh coriander. Taste the gravy: it should be light, sharp from the mustard oil, and not thick. Serve with plain rice."]},
"bangladeshi|Kacchi Biryani": {"time":"About 3 hours, plus marinating","steps":["Wash the basmati or kalijeera rice until the water runs clear, then soak it for 30 minutes. Kacchi is cooked raw — the rice only parboils later, it does not get fried.","Pat the mutton or goat dry. Mix it with the plain yogurt, ginger paste, garlic paste, red chilli powder, turmeric, garam masala, half the salt, slit green chillies, bay leaves, cinnamon, green and black cardamom, cloves, shah jeera, mace and a grating of nutmeg. Cover and marinate at least 4 hours, overnight if you can.","Peel the potatoes, halve them, and rub with a little turmeric and salt. Fry them in mustard oil until the edges colour, then set them aside. In the same oil, fry half the sliced onion slowly until deep brown; that is extra beresta to go with the fried onions already on your list.","Heat the ghee with the remaining mustard oil. Add the marinated meat and cook on a lively flame until the yogurt splits and the meat starts to brown, about 15 minutes. Stir in the remaining raw onion. Add a splash of water, cover, and braise until the mutton is nearly tender. The gravy should be thick, not soupy — kacchi rice drinks it.","Meanwhile boil a large pot of water with a little salt, the whole milk, and a few of the whole spices from the marinade. Add the drained rice and boil until it is just half cooked, still with a bite. Drain at once.","Warm the saffron in a spoon of the warm milk with the kewra water and rose water.","In a heavy pot, spread the meat and its gravy, then the fried potatoes. Pile on the rice. Scatter the fried onions, mint, coriander, almonds, raisins, the rest of the ghee, a squeeze of lemon, and the saffron milk. Seal the lid with a strip of dough or a tight cloth.","Cook on high for 5 minutes, then on the lowest flame for 40 to 45 minutes. Rest 10 minutes off the heat before you open it. Serve from the bottom so every plate gets meat, potato and saffron rice."]},
"bangladeshi|Labra": {"time":"About 50 minutes","steps":["Peel and cut the potato, sweet potato, pumpkin, radish and raw banana into chunks. Cut the eggplant, French beans and cauliflower a little smaller. Keep them separate; they do not take the same time.","Heat the mustard oil until it smokes, then cool it slightly. Add the panch phoron and bay leaves. When they pop, add the ginger paste and fry 1 minute.","Add turmeric, cumin powder, coriander powder and red chilli powder with a splash of water. Fry until the oil shows at the edge.","Add the raw banana, potato, sweet potato and radish first with the salt and sugar. Fry 5 minutes. Add a cup of hot water, cover, and cook 8 minutes.","Add the pumpkin, beans, eggplant and cauliflower. Stir once. Cover and cook until everything is tender and the gravy is thick enough to coat the vegetables, about 12 minutes. Labra should be moister than chachchari, but not a soup.","Stir in the garam masala and green chillies. Rest 5 minutes. Finish with the fresh coriander. It tastes better after sitting, when the vegetables have swapped flavours."]},
"bangladeshi|Lau Chingri": {"time":"About 35 minutes","steps":["Peel the bottle gourd, scrape out the seeds if they are hard, and cut it into small cubes. Sprinkle with a little salt and leave 10 minutes, then squeeze gently. This keeps the curry from going watery.","Rub the prawns with half the turmeric and a pinch of salt.","Heat the mustard oil until it just smokes, then lower the heat. Add the panch phoron and let it pop. Add the chopped onion and ginger and cook until the onion is soft.","Add the prawns. Fry until they turn pink, then take most of them out so they do not turn rubbery. Leave the flavoured oil behind.","Stir in the remaining turmeric, red chilli powder and cumin powder. Add the bottle gourd, the rest of the salt and the sugar. Fry 4 minutes.","Add a small cup of water and the green chillies. Cover and cook until the gourd is soft but not mush, about 12 minutes. There should be a little gravy, not a dry fry.","Return the prawns for 2 minutes. Finish with the fresh coriander. Serve with rice."]},
"bangladeshi|Lobongo Lotika": {"time":"About 1 hour 15 minutes","steps":["Rub the ghee into the all-purpose flour and semolina with your fingertips until it feels like damp sand. Add cold water a spoon at a time and bring it into a firm dough. Knead 5 minutes, cover, and rest 20 minutes.","Warm the khoya with the cardamom seeds and a splash of rose water until it is a thick, sweet-smelling paste that holds a ball. Cool it. If it is loose, the parcels will burst.","Roll the dough thin and cut squares. Put a spoon of khoya in the centre. Fold into a triangle or a little purse, sealing the edges with water. Press one clove through the centre of each — the clove both flavours it and pins it shut. That is the lotika.","Heat the oil for frying on medium, not smoking. Fry the parcels a few at a time until even gold, turning often. Drain.","Boil the sugar with a little water to a one-string syrup: it should form a single thread between finger and thumb. Dip the hot pastries in the syrup for a few seconds and lift them onto a plate.","Cool until the sugar crusts. They should shatter, not chew. Store in an airtight tin once cold."]},
"bangladeshi|Luchi": {"time":"About 45 minutes","steps":["Mix the maida with the salt and sugar. Rub in a spoon of the oil so the dough stays soft. Add warm water a little at a time and knead for 8 minutes until it is smooth and slightly tacky.","Cover with a damp cloth and rest 20 minutes. A rested dough puffs; a tight one stays flat.","Divide into small balls, a little smaller than a lime. Roll each into a thin round, about 10 cm, dusting with flour only if it sticks. Do not stack them or they sweat.","Heat the rest of the oil in a kadai until a pinch of dough rises at once. Slide in one luchi. Press it gently with a slotted spoon so it balloons.","When the first side is pale gold, flip it for a few seconds. Lift it out onto paper. Fry one or two at a time so the oil stays hot.","Serve immediately with bhuna khichuri, cholar dal or a potato curry. Luchi softens if it waits."]},
"bangladeshi|Mezban Beef": {"time":"About 2 hours","steps":["Cut the beef into large pieces, bone left in. Bone is not optional in mezban — it makes the gravy.","Blend or finely chop the onion. Heat the mustard oil until it smokes, then cool it slightly. Fry the onion on medium until it is soft and the coconut fat separates. Do not brown it hard.","Add the ginger paste and garlic paste and cook 3 minutes. Stir in the red chilli powder, turmeric, cumin powder, coriander powder and black pepper with a spoon of water. Mezban is meant to be very hot; do not cut the chilli.","Add the beef and salt. Fry until the meat loses its raw colour and the spices coat it. Add the bay leaves, cinnamon, green cardamom and cloves.","Pour in enough hot water to just cover. Bring to a boil, skim, then cover and simmer until the beef is nearly tender, about 1 hour to 1 hour 15 minutes. Add water only if it catches.","Add the potatoes, cut in large chunks, and the whole green chillies. Cook until the potatoes are soft and the gravy is reddish and oily, not thin.","Rest 10 minutes. Scatter the fresh coriander. Serve with lots of plain rice — mezban is eaten in a crowd, from one big pot."]},
"bangladeshi|Morog Polao": {"time":"About 1 hour 20 minutes","steps":["Wash the kalijeera or basmati rice and soak it 30 minutes. Joint the chicken, keeping the bone in.","Whisk the yogurt with the ginger paste, garlic paste, turmeric, half the salt and half the garam masala. Coat the chicken and rest it 30 minutes.","Heat the cooking oil with half the ghee. Fry the sliced onion slowly until golden. Add bay leaves, cinnamon, green cardamom, cloves and shah jeera.","Add the chicken with its marinade and the green chillies. Fry until the yogurt dries and the chicken takes a little colour, about 12 minutes. Pour in a cup of hot water, cover, and cook until the chicken is just tender and the gravy is clinging, not swimming.","In another pot heat the remaining ghee. Add the drained rice and fry 2 minutes. Stir in the chicken and gravy, the whole milk, the rest of the salt and garam masala, and hot water to sit a finger-joint above the rice.","Cover and cook on low until the rice is done and the steam holes show, about 15 minutes. Rest 10 minutes.","Open the lid and scatter the fried onions, raisins, almonds, kewra water and fresh coriander. Fluff from the sides. Serve with salad or borhani."]},
"bangladeshi|Muri Ghonto": {"time":"About 50 minutes","steps":["Wash the gobindobhog or kalijeera rice and soak it 20 minutes. Rub the fish head with half the turmeric and a little salt.","Heat the mustard oil until it smokes, then cool it a moment. Fry the fish head until the skin is golden and the cut side is sealed. Break it into large pieces. Set it aside.","In the same oil fry the bay leaves, cinnamon, green cardamom and cloves. Add the sliced onion and cook until golden. Stir in the ginger paste and garlic paste.","Add the remaining turmeric, red chilli powder, cumin powder and coriander powder. Then the chopped tomato. Cook until the coconut fat separates.","Add the diced potato and the fish-head pieces. Fry 3 minutes. Tip in the drained rice and salt and fry until the grains look glossy — this is the muri, the fried rice, meeting the ghonto.","Pour in hot water to sit just above the rice. Add the green chillies. Cover and cook on low until the rice is soft and the fish has flavoured every grain, about 15 minutes. It should be moist, not a dry pulao and not a soup.","Finish with the ghee, garam masala and fresh coriander. Rest 5 minutes before serving."]},
"bangladeshi|Narkeler Naru": {"time":"About 40 minutes","steps":["If the coconut is moist from the shell, dry it in a dry pan for 3 minutes, stirring, so the naru will set. Do not let it brown.","Add the jaggery or sugar and the condensed milk. Cook on medium, stirring constantly, until the mixture thickens, turns glossy, and a little dropped on a plate holds its shape. This can take 15 to 20 minutes. If it still flows, it is not ready.","Stir in the ghee and the seeds from the green cardamom. Cook 1 minute more. The ghee keeps the naru from tasting dry.","Cool until you can handle it. Grease your palms and roll firm balls. If they slump, cook the mixture a little longer and try again.","Cool completely on a plate. They firm up as they cool. Keep them in a tin; they soften in the fridge unless you bring them back to room temperature."]},
"bangladeshi|Nihari": {"time":"About 5 hours, or overnight on the lowest heat","steps":["Ask for beef shank cut across the bone so each piece shows marrow. Pat it dry.","Heat the ghee and fry the sliced onion until deep brown. Add ginger paste, garlic paste, bay leaves, cinnamon, green and black cardamom, cloves, mace, a grating of nutmeg and the black pepper. Cook 2 minutes.","Add the red chilli powder, turmeric, coriander powder and fennel powder with a splash of water. Then add the shank and salt. Brown it well.","Stir in the yogurt. When it clings to the meat, add hot water to cover by 4 cm. Bring to a boil, skim the foam, then cover.","Cook on the lowest flame for 3 to 4 hours, until the meat slides off the bone and the marrow is soft. Nihari is traditionally left overnight; if you can, cook it even slower.","Whisk the wheat flour with cold water to a smooth slurry. Stir it into the simmering gravy and cook 15 minutes until the nihari turns glossy and thick enough to coat the back of a spoon.","Finish with kewra water. Serve in deep bowls with fried onions, ginger julienne, green chillies, fresh coriander, lemon, and naan or ruti to dip."]},
"bangladeshi|Patishapta Pitha": {"time":"About 1 hour","steps":["For the filling, melt the jaggery, stir in the grated coconut and the cardamom seeds, and cook until it is thick and sticky. Cool it. It should hold a shape.","Whisk the rice flour and all-purpose flour with the sugar, salt, milk and enough water to make a thin batter, like dosa batter. Rest it 15 minutes. Stir in a spoon of the oil.","Heat a non-stick pan and grease it lightly with oil. Pour a ladle of batter and swirl it into a thin round.","Cook on medium until the top looks dry and the underside is spotted. Do not flip it.","Spoon a line of coconut filling across the centre and roll the crepe while it is still flexible. The raw-looking top sticks to itself and seals the roll.","Wipe the pan, add a drop more oil, and repeat. Serve warm. Patishapta is best the day it is made."]},
"bangladeshi|Rasmalai": {"time":"About 1 hour 30 minutes, plus chilling","steps":["Boil 1 litre of the whole milk, stirring so it does not catch. Take it off the heat and add the lemon juice a little at a time until the milk splits into soft curds and pale whey. If it does not split, add a few more drops.","Pour into a cloth, rinse the chhana under cold water to remove the lemon taste, and hang it 20 minutes. It should be moist, not dripping. Knead it with the heel of your hand for 8 minutes until it is absolutely smooth. A grain of curd will make the rasmalai crack.","Divide into 8 smooth balls and press them gently into fat discs.","Boil half the sugar with 3 cups of water to a thin syrup. Slide in the discs and boil on a steady medium flame for 15 minutes. They should double and feel spongy. Lift them into warm water for 2 minutes, then squeeze very gently.","For the rabri, simmer the remaining milk with the rest of the sugar and the cardamom pods, stirring often, until it thickens to a pouring cream, about 25 minutes. Crumble in the saffron, add the rose water, and take it off the heat.","Soak the discs in the warm rabri. Chill at least 4 hours. Serve cold, scattered with chopped pistachios and almonds."]},
"bangladeshi|Shorshe Ilish": {"time":"About 35 minutes","steps":["Soak the black and yellow mustard seeds in a little warm water for 15 minutes, then grind them with two of the green chillies and a pinch of salt to a thick paste. Do not grind it silky — a little graininess is how shorshe should taste. Stir in half the turmeric.","Rub the hilsa steaks with the remaining turmeric and salt. Heat the mustard oil until it smokes, cool it a moment, then fry the fish just until the skin firms. Set it aside.","Lower the heat. Add the black cumin seeds, then the mustard paste. Fry it gently for 3 to 4 minutes. If it splatters, add a spoon of water. Stir in the red chilli powder and sugar.","Pour in the coconut milk and enough water to make a thin gravy. Bring it just to a simmer — a hard boil turns mustard bitter.","Lay the fish in the gravy with the remaining green chillies, some slit and some whole. Cover and cook on low for 8 minutes, until the fish is just done and the gravy smells sweet-sharp.","Taste for salt. Rest 5 minutes before serving so the hilsa stays in pieces. Eat with hot rice."]},
"bangladeshi|Sutki Bhorta": {"time":"About 25 minutes","steps":["Dry-roast the loitta shutki in a pan, or hold it over a flame, until it is brittle and smells toasty, not raw. Rub off any burnt bits. This step takes away the muddy smell.","Soak the roasted shutki in warm water for 5 minutes, then squeeze it dry and flake it with your fingers, picking out bones.","In the mustard oil, fry the dried red chillies until they darken, then the chopped garlic until just gold. Add the flaked fish, turmeric, red chilli powder and salt. Fry 3 to 4 minutes, mashing as you go.","Take it off the heat. Mix in the finely chopped onion, tomato, green chillies and most of the fresh coriander. The onion should stay raw and sharp — that is bhorta, not a curry.","Finish with the lime and the remaining coriander. Taste: it should be salty, hot and bright. Serve with hot rice and a little extra mustard oil on the plate if you like it richer."]},
"brazilian|Acarajé": {"time":"About 45 minutes, plus soaking","steps":["Soak the black-eyed peas, then rub off the skins and rinse them away. The skins make a heavy fritter.","Blend or pound the peas with the onion and salt to a smooth, airy paste. Beat it until it lightens. A dense paste fries heavy.","Pound the dried shrimp with the chilli and ginger for a rough relish, and salt it.","Heat the palm oil until a dab of paste sizzles and swells. Spoon in oval fritters.","Fry until deep orange-gold and cooked through, about 4 minutes, turning once.","Split them and spoon in the shrimp relish. Acarajé should be crisp outside, soft and beany inside, the relish hot and salty."]},
"brazilian|Bobó de Camarão": {"time":"About 45 minutes","steps":["Peel the cassava, boil it until very soft, and mash it with a ladle of the cooking water until smooth. Fibrous bits should be picked out.","Salt the shrimp and squeeze over half the lime.","Warm the palm oil and soften the onion and garlic. Add the tomato and cook until it breaks down.","Stir in the cassava puree and coconut milk. Simmer until thick and creamy, about 10 minutes. Add salt.","Fold in the shrimp and cook only until they turn pink, about 3 minutes. Longer and they toughen.","Finish with coriander and the rest of the lime. Bobó should be a thick shrimp stew, pale orange, the cassava creamy."]},
"brazilian|Brigadeiro": {"time":"About 25 minutes","steps":["Put the condensed milk, cocoa, butter and salt in a pan. Cook on medium, stirring constantly, until the mixture thickens and pulls from the bottom in a mass, about 12 minutes. A spoon dragged through should leave a trail.","If it is still glossy and flowing, it is not ready and the sweets will not hold.","Cool until you can handle it. Grease your hands with a little butter.","Roll small balls and coat them in sprinkles.","They firm as they cool. If they slump, cook the mixture a few minutes more and try again.","Brigadeiro should be fudgy, not cakey, and taste of cooked condensed milk and cocoa."]},
"brazilian|Coxinha": {"time":"About 1 hour 15 minutes","steps":["Simmer the chicken with the onion and salt until tender. Shred the meat and keep 300 ml of broth. Mash the potato.","Bring the broth to a boil with the butter. Tip in the flour and stir until a dough forms and leaves the pan. Mix in the mashed potato. Cool until you can handle it.","Take a piece of dough, flatten it, fill with shredded chicken, and shape a teardrop, the coxinha shape. Seal it.","Dip in beaten egg and breadcrumbs.","Fry in medium-hot oil until deep gold and crisp, about 4 minutes.","The crust should shatter, the chicken inside moist. Drain and eat hot."]},
"brazilian|Farofa": {"time":"About 15 minutes","steps":["Slice the onion and cook it in the butter until soft and sweet.","Push it aside and scramble the eggs until just set.","Add the cassava flour and salt. Toast, stirring, until the flour smells nutty and turns gold, about 5 minutes. Raw flour tastes dusty.","The farofa should be loose and sandy, not a paste. If it clumps, the heat is too low or the butter was too much; keep stirring.","Fold in the parsley.","Serve as a side. It should crunch softly against richer food."]},
"brazilian|Feijoada": {"time":"About 3 hours","steps":["Soak the black beans overnight if they are dried. Drain.","In a heavy pot, brown the bacon. Add the pork shoulder, pork ribs and smoked sausage, cut into pieces, and brown them.","Add the beans, the garlic, bay leaf and water to cover. Simmer gently, skimming, until the beans are soft and the meats are tender, about 2 hours. Add water if it gets too dry. The sausage and bacon are already salty.","Fry or braise the collard greens separately with a little garlic until just tender.","Serve the beans and meats with rice, the greens, and orange slices. The orange cuts the fat."]},
"brazilian|Moqueca": {"time":"About 40 minutes","steps":["Cut the fish into large pieces. Salt it and squeeze over the lime. Rest 15 minutes.","Slice the onion, pepper and tomato. In a wide pot, warm the palm oil and soften the onion, garlic and pepper. Palm oil gives the orange colour and the taste; do not let it smoke.","Add the tomato and chilli and cook until the tomato softens, about 6 minutes.","Lay in the fish. Pour the coconut milk around it. Simmer gently, without stirring hard, until the fish is just cooked, about 10 minutes.","Taste the salt. The sauce should be coconut-rich, a little sour from the lime already on the fish, and coloured by the palm oil.","Finish with coriander. Moqueca is a fish stew, the pieces intact, not a dry fry."]},
"brazilian|Picanha": {"time":"About 40 minutes","steps":["Leave the fat cap on. Cut the picanha into thick steaks, each with a ribbon of fat. Salt them on all sides with coarse salt. The salt is the seasoning; do not add a wet marinade.","Let them sit 20 minutes so the salt starts to dissolve on the surface.","Grill or pan-sear fat side first so the fat renders and bastes the meat. Then sear the cut sides.","Cook to a rosy centre. Picanha cooked grey is a waste of the cut. Rest 8 minutes.","Slice across the grain, each slice still showing the fat cap.","Finish with pepper. The crust should be salty, the inside juicy, the fat softened."]},
"brazilian|Pudim": {"time":"About 1 hour, plus chilling","steps":["Melt the sugar in a dry pan until it is a deep amber caramel. Pour it into a mould and tilt to coat the bottom. Work fast; it sets.","Blend or whisk the condensed milk, milk and eggs until smooth. Do not whip in a lot of air or the pudim will be full of holes.","Pour over the caramel. Cover the mould.","Bake in a bain-marie at 160°C until the centre is just set, about 50 minutes. A hard boil around the tin makes bubbles in the custard.","Cool, then chill at least 4 hours. Run a knife around the edge and invert. The caramel becomes the sauce.","Pudim should be silky and trembling, sweet, with a bitter edge from the caramel."]},
"brazilian|Pão de Queijo": {"time":"About 45 minutes","steps":["Heat the milk and oil together until just steaming.","Pour the hot liquid over the tapioca flour and stir until it forms a sticky mass. Cool until you can handle it.","Work in the egg, then the grated Minas cheese and parmesan. The dough will be soft and slightly elastic.","With wet hands, roll small balls and set them on a baking sheet.","Bake at 200°C until puffed and golden, about 15 to 20 minutes. They should be crisp-tender outside and chewy inside.","Eat warm. They toughen as they cool and revive in a hot oven."]},
"burmese|Coconut Rice": {"time":"About 30 minutes","steps":["Rinse the jasmine rice until the water runs almost clear. Drain it well.","Put the rice, coconut milk, water and salt in a pot. Stir once so the salt does not sit on the bottom.","Bring to a boil, then cover and cook on the lowest heat until the liquid is absorbed and steam holes appear, about 15 minutes.","Rest, covered, off the heat for 10 minutes. Do not stir yet or you smash the grains.","Fluff from the sides. The rice should be glossy and separate, tasting of coconut and a little salt, not wet.","Scatter the fried onion on top. Serve hot, as the rice beside a curry, or on its own."]},
"burmese|Htamin Jin": {"time":"About 40 minutes","steps":["Cook the rice until just done and spread it so it stays separate. Day-old rice also works and fries more cleanly.","Soak the dried chilli until soft, then pound it with the garlic.","Heat the oil and fry the chilli-garlic paste until the oil turns red. Add the fish paste and fry 1 minute until it smells cooked, not raw.","Add the chopped tomato and cook until it becomes a thick sauce, about 6 minutes.","Add the rice and salt and toss until every grain is stained and hot, about 3 minutes. Do not add water. Htamin jin is a dry tomato rice.","Taste. It should be savoury from the fish paste, sour from the tomato, and hot. Finish with coriander."]},
"burmese|Mohinga": {"time":"About 1 hour 15 minutes","steps":["Simmer the catfish in water with the lemongrass until it flakes, about 15 minutes. Lift it out, discard the bones, and flake the flesh. Keep the broth.","Slice the banana stem thin and boil it in the broth for 10 minutes so it loses its raw bite. Slice the onion.","Heat the oil and fry the onion, garlic, ginger and chilli until soft. Add the chickpea flour and stir for 1 minute so it loses its raw taste.","Pour in the fish broth, whisking, so the flour does not lump. Add the flaked catfish, banana stem, fish sauce and salt. Simmer 15 minutes until the soup is lightly thick and tastes of fish and lemongrass.","Cook the rice noodles and divide them between bowls.","Ladle the boiling soup over the noodles. Mohinga should be savoury and herbal, thick enough to cling to the noodles, with pieces of fish you can see."]},
"burmese|Mont Lone Yay Paw": {"time":"About 45 minutes","steps":["Warm the palm sugar with a spoon of water until it melts, then mix it with the grated coconut and a pinch of the salt. Cook, stirring, until it is a thick paste that holds a ball. Cool it. This is the filling.","Mix the glutinous rice flour with the rest of the salt and warm water, a spoon at a time, until you have a soft dough that does not crack. It should feel like an earlobe.","Pinch off balls of dough, flatten them, and wrap a small ball of coconut-jaggery inside. Seal every crack. A crack leaks sugar into the water and the dumpling bursts.","Drop the balls into a wide pot of boiling water. They are done when they float and look glossy, about 5 minutes.","Lift them out with a slotted spoon onto a plate. Do not stack them while they are streaming hot or they glue together.","Mont lone yay paw should be chewy, the centre molten and sweet. Eat them warm."]},
"burmese|Nan Gyi Thoke": {"time":"About 35 minutes","steps":["Slice the chicken. Fry the sliced onion and garlic with the turmeric until soft. Add the chicken and salt and cook until just done.","Stir in the chickpea flour and 200 ml of water. Simmer until you have a thick chicken gravy, about 8 minutes. Add the fish sauce. The gravy must be thick because it is tossed, not souped.","Cook thick rice noodles until tender, rinse, and drain well. Wet noodles dilute the dressing.","Toss the warm noodles with the chicken gravy, chilli oil and the juice of the lime.","The noodles should be coated, stained gold and red, and taste salty, hot and sour.","Scatter coriander. Nan gyi thoke is a salad of thick noodles, eaten warm, not a bowl of broth."]},
"burmese|Ohn No Khao Swe": {"time":"About 50 minutes","steps":["Slice the onion. Heat the oil and fry the onion, garlic and ginger with the turmeric until the onion is gold and the oil is yellow.","Add the chicken pieces and salt and brown them lightly. Pour in the coconut milk and 300 ml of water. Simmer until the chicken is tender, about 25 minutes.","Whisk the chickpea flour with a ladle of the hot broth, then stir it back in. Simmer 5 minutes until the gravy thickens enough to coat the noodles. Add the fish sauce.","Shred the chicken. Cook the wheat noodles until just tender and rinse them.","Put noodles in bowls, then chicken and the coconut gravy.","Serve with lime. Ohn no khao swe is a mild coconut chicken noodle soup, golden from turmeric, richer than mohinga and not fiercely hot."]},
"burmese|Pork Curry": {"time":"About 1 hour 10 minutes","steps":["Cut the pork into large chunks, leaving a little fat on. Slice the onion and pound the garlic and ginger.","Heat the oil and fry the onion slowly until it is soft and sweet, about 10 minutes. Burmese pork curry starts with a lot of onion, cooked down.","Add the garlic, ginger, turmeric and chilli. Fry until the oil separates.","Add the pork and salt. Fry until the meat loses its raw colour and the spices coat it.","Add the fish sauce and a cup of water. Cover and simmer until the pork is tender and the gravy is thick and oily, about 45 minutes. Add water only if it catches.","The curry should be rich and hot, the onion melted into the sauce. Rest 10 minutes before serving."]},
"burmese|Shan Noodles": {"time":"About 35 minutes","steps":["Cook the rice noodles until just tender, rinse, and drain.","Fry the pork, minced or chopped small, until browned. Add the chopped tomato and shallot and cook until the tomato becomes a thick sauce around the meat.","Toss the noodles with a spoon of the chilli oil.","Top with the pork and tomato, more chilli oil, the fried shallot if you held any back, and the peanuts.","Serve warm. It should be spicy, tomato-sour and nutty."]},
"burmese|Tea Leaf Salad": {"time":"About 20 minutes","steps":["Rinse the fermented tea leaves and squeeze them dry. Chop them fine. Taste a pinch: they are sour, bitter and savoury.","Mix the tea leaves with a squeeze of the lime.","Arrange in piles, not mixed yet: the tea, fried garlic, fried shallot, roasted peanuts, sesame seeds and chopped tomato.","At the table, toss everything together. The textures — crisp garlic, crunchy peanut, soft tomato — are the dish.","Eat with rice. A little goes a long way."]},
"burmese|Tofu Nway": {"time":"About 30 minutes","steps":["Soak the chickpeas if they are dried, then boil them until soft. Drain.","Whisk the chickpea flour with 700 ml of cold water, the turmeric and salt until there are no lumps.","Cook this mixture on medium, stirring constantly, until it thickens to a smooth porridge that holds a line from the spoon, about 10 minutes. Shan tofu is this porridge, set or soft. For tofu nway, leave it pourable and silky.","Slice the onion and crush the garlic. Stir them through, raw, with the boiled chickpeas.","Ladle into bowls. Spoon on the chilli oil.","Finish with lime and coriander. Tofu nway is eaten warm, mild and turmeric-yellow, the heat and sourness added at the end."]},
"cambodian|Amok": {"time":"About 50 minutes","steps":["Cut the white fish into slices. Mix the kroeung with half the coconut milk.","Stir the egg into the remaining coconut milk, then into the curry mixture. You want a custard, not a scrambled egg.","Line the banana leaf cups, or small bowls, with a little of the mixture. Lay in the fish. Pour the rest over.","Steam over gentle heat for 20 to 25 minutes, until the centre is just set and still trembling. A hard boil makes it porous.","Serve in the cups. Amok should be soft enough to eat with a spoon, fragrant with the curry paste."]},
"cambodian|Bai Sach Chrouk": {"time":"About 40 minutes, plus marinating","steps":["Slice the pork into thin pieces. Pound the garlic with the palm sugar, fish sauce, black pepper and a pinch of salt. Coat the pork and rest it at least 2 hours, overnight if you can. The sugar is what lacquers the meat.","Rinse the jasmine rice and cook it with the salt until just done and fluffy. Fold the chopped scallion through the hot rice.","Heat the oil in a pan until very hot. Fry the pork in batches so it browns instead of stewing, about 2 minutes a side.","When the last batch is done, pour any leftover marinade into the pan and let it bubble until sticky. Toss the pork through it.","The pork should be sweet-salty, with dark edges and a juicy centre. Do not cook it until it is grey and dry.","Serve the rice with the pork on top and the sticky juices from the pan. This is the Cambodian grilled-pork rice plate, here cooked in a pan."]},
"cambodian|Cha Kroeung": {"time":"About 25 minutes","steps":["Slice the beef thin against the grain. Cut the green beans on a sharp diagonal.","Heat the oil in a wok until it smokes. Fry the kroeung and garlic for 1 minute until the paste smells toasted and the oil is fragrant.","Add the beef and stir-fry until it just browns, about 2 minutes. Do not let it stew.","Add the green beans, fish sauce, sugar and salt. Toss until the beans are bright and still crisp and the sauce glazes the beef, about 3 minutes.","If the pan looks dry, add a spoon of water, not a ladle. Cha kroeung is a dry stir-fry.","Serve at once. You should taste the kroeung clearly, with sweet-salty beef and beans that still snap."]},
"cambodian|Kuy Teav": {"time":"About 1 hour 30 minutes","steps":["Cover the pork bones with cold water, bring to a boil, and skim. Simmer with half the garlic and the salt for 1 hour, until the broth tastes clearly of pork. Strain and keep it hot.","Fry the ground pork with the rest of the garlic until brown and crumbly. Season with a spoon of the fish sauce.","Cook the rice noodles until just tender, rinse, and divide between bowls.","Taste the broth and add the rest of the fish sauce. It should be clean and savoury, not cloudy. If you boiled it hard, let it settle and ladle from the top.","Ladle boiling broth over the noodles. Top with the fried pork, bean sprouts and scallion.","Serve lime on the side. Kuy teav is a pork noodle soup, lighter than a curry soup, and the lime is squeezed in at the table."]},
"cambodian|Lap Khmer": {"time":"About 25 minutes","steps":["Slice the lemongrass paper-thin and chop the shallot, mint and chilli. Chop the peanuts coarsely.","Slice the beef very thin against the grain, or mince it. Warm a dry pan and stir the beef only until it loses its raw colour, about 1 minute. It should stay tender. Tip it into a bowl with its juices.","While it is warm, add the juice of the lime, the fish sauce and salt. The warmth of the beef pulls the lime into the meat.","Fold in the lemongrass, shallot, chilli and most of the mint and peanuts.","Taste. Lap Khmer is sour first, then salty, with crunch from the peanuts and perfume from the lemongrass. Add lime if it tastes flat.","Scatter the rest of the mint. Serve warm, not hot, so the herbs stay bright."]},
"cambodian|Lok Lak": {"time":"About 25 minutes","steps":["Slice the beef sirloin thin, against the grain. Toss it with the soy sauce and oyster sauce.","Heat a pan until very hot. Sear the beef in batches for a minute or two so it stays tender. Do not crowd it.","Arrange the lettuce and sliced tomato on a plate. Spoon the beef and its juices over them.","Serve with steamed rice and a dipping sauce of lime and a little of the soy, if you can spare it, with black pepper.","Eat at once, while the beef is still pink in the middle."]},
"cambodian|Nom Banh Chok": {"time":"About 1 hour","steps":["Simmer the fish in water until it flakes. Lift it out, discard the bones, and pound or break the flesh fine. Keep the broth.","Warm the coconut milk with the lemongrass, bruised, and the turmeric. Add the flaked fish and enough of the fish broth to make a thin, yellow, fragrant gravy. Simmer 15 minutes. Remove the lemongrass.","Cook or refresh the rice noodles.","Put noodles in bowls and ladle the fish gravy over them.","Serve with raw vegetables if you have them. The gravy should taste of turmeric and lemongrass, not of a thick curry."]},
"cambodian|Num Pang": {"time":"About 30 minutes","steps":["Slice the pork belly. Rub it with the salt and a spoon of the fish sauce. Fry or roast it until the skin is crisp and the fat has rendered, about 15 minutes. Slice it thin.","Cut the carrot into matchsticks. Mix the rice vinegar, sugar and a pinch of salt until the sugar dissolves. Soak the carrot for 15 minutes, then drain. This quick pickle is the sour bite in the sandwich.","Split the baguettes and warm them so the crust crackles and the inside is soft.","Slice the cucumber. Chop the coriander.","Fill each baguette with pork, cucumber, pickled carrot and coriander. Spoon a little of the remaining fish sauce over the pork.","Num pang should be eaten while the bread is crisp and the pork fat is still warm. Press it closed so the juices run into the crumb."]},
"cambodian|Pleah Sach Ko": {"time":"About 25 minutes","steps":["Slice the lemongrass and shallot very thin. Slice the beef paper-thin.","Bring a pot of water to a boil. Dip the beef in for 10 seconds, just until it turns grey at the edge and is still pink inside. Drain at once. Overcooked beef makes a dry salad.","Toss the warm beef with the lime juice, fish sauce and salt.","Fold in the lemongrass, shallot, chilli, roasted rice powder and most of the mint.","The rice powder should cling and smell toasted. If the salad looks wet, add a pinch more of it; it drinks the lime.","Taste for sour and salt. Scatter the rest of the mint. Pleah is eaten slightly warm, the beef tender, the lemongrass still crunchy."]},
"cambodian|Samlor Korko": {"time":"About 50 minutes","steps":["Cut the pork ribs into pieces. Cut the pumpkin, eggplant, papaya and beans into similar chunks. Green papaya is firm; ripe papaya will dissolve.","Stir the prahok with a spoon of water until it loosens. Prahok is fermented fish; a little seasons the whole pot. Do not add more than the amount listed or the stew turns harsh.","Heat a spoon of the pork fat by browning the ribs in a dry pot until they colour. Add the kroeung and fry 2 minutes until fragrant.","Add the prahok, palm sugar and salt. Pour in the water and simmer the ribs 20 minutes.","Add the papaya and pumpkin first, then the eggplant and beans. Simmer until all the vegetables are tender and the broth is lightly thick, about 15 minutes.","Samlor korko should taste herbal from the kroeung, a little sweet from the palm sugar and pumpkin, and savoury from the prahok, with the vegetables still in pieces."]},
"chinese|Char Siu": {"time":"About 1 hour 20 minutes, plus marinating","steps":["Cut the pork shoulder into long strips about 4 cm thick, along the grain. Pat them dry.","Mix the hoisin, soy sauce, half the honey, five-spice, grated garlic, Shaoxing wine and salt. Coat the pork and marinate at least 4 hours, overnight if you can. Turn it once.","Heat the oven to 200°C. Set the strips on a rack over a tray so the heat reaches all sides. Roast 25 minutes.","Brush with the remaining marinade and the rest of the honey. Roast 15 to 20 minutes more, until the edges char in spots and the centre is just cooked, still juicy. Honey burns, so watch the last minutes.","Rest 10 minutes before slicing. The lacquer should be sticky and red-brown, the meat blushing pink at the edge from the sauce, not from being raw.","Slice across the grain. Char siu is the roast itself; the sticky juices on the tray are the glaze, spooned back over the slices."]},
"chinese|Dan Dan Noodles": {"time":"About 35 minutes","steps":["Toast the Sichuan peppercorn in a dry pan until fragrant, then crush it. This is the numb, not just the heat.","Heat the cooking oil and fry the minced pork with a pinch of the salt until it is brown and crisp at the edges, about 6 minutes. Add the doubanjiang and half the garlic and fry until the oil turns red.","Whisk the sesame paste with the soy sauce, the rest of the garlic, crushed, the chilli oil and 4 spoons of the noodle-cooking water you will save. It should be a thick sauce that loosens when the hot noodles hit it.","Boil the wheat noodles until just tender. Save a cup of the water, then drain.","Divide the sesame sauce between bowls. Add the noodles and toss. Top with the pork, the crushed peppercorn and the chopped scallion.","Each person mixes their own bowl. Dan dan should be hot, numbing, and slick with sesame, with crisp pork against soft noodles. Add a spoon of the saved water only if it is too thick to toss."]},
"chinese|Dumplings": {"time":"About 1 hour 30 minutes","steps":["Squeeze the chopped napa cabbage dry in a cloth. Mix it with the ground pork, grated ginger, soy sauce and sesame oil. Stir in one direction until the filling is sticky.","Put a spoon of filling in each dumpling wrapper. Wet the rim, fold into a half-moon, and pleat one side so the dumpling sits up.","To boil: lower them into boiling water, add a cup of cold water each time it returns to the boil, three times, until they float and the skins are glossy.","To pan-fry: fry in a little oil until the bottoms brown, add a splash of water, cover, and steam until the water is gone and the bottoms are crisp again.","Serve with more soy sauce and a few drops of sesame oil."]},
"chinese|Fried Rice": {"time":"About 20 minutes","steps":["The jasmine rice must be cooked and cold, ideally from yesterday. Fresh hot rice turns to porridge. Break up any clumps with wet fingers.","Beat the egg. Heat a wok until it smokes, add a little oil, and scramble the egg in large curds. Lift it out.","In the same wok stir-fry the char siu, sliced, and the shrimp until the shrimp turn pink.","Add the rice and the peas. Press and toss on the highest heat so grains fry instead of steam. Splash in the soy sauce around the side of the wok so it seasons as it hits the hot metal.","Return the egg. Finish with the sesame oil off the heat. Taste. It should smell nutty, not wet."]},
"chinese|Hot Pot": {"time":"About 40 minutes at the table","steps":["Freeze the beef sirloin and lamb for 30 minutes so you can slice them paper thin. Arrange them raw on plates. Keep the napa cabbage, enoki, tofu and fish balls on separate plates.","Prepare the hot pot broth base in a pot that can sit on a burner at the table, with enough water to stay deep. Bring it to a real boil before anyone cooks.","Each person cooks their own food in the boiling broth: meat for a few seconds, cabbage and tofu longer, fish balls until they float and are hot through.","Skim the broth now and then. Do not let it drop off the boil or the meat will stew grey.","Eat as you cook. The last of the broth, now rich from everything that passed through it, is the best bowl."]},
"chinese|Kung Pao Chicken": {"time":"About 25 minutes","steps":["Cut the chicken thigh into small pieces. Toss with a spoon of the soy sauce.","Heat a wok until smoking. Fry the dried red chilli and Sichuan peppercorn for a few seconds until they smell fragrant, not burnt. Lift them out.","Fry the chicken until just cooked and lightly browned. Add the roasted peanuts and return the chilli and peppercorn.","Pour in the rest of the soy sauce and the black vinegar. Toss until the sauce glazes the chicken and the peanuts stay crisp.","Serve at once. The vinegar should cut the chilli; if it tastes flat, it needs another drop of vinegar, not more soy."]},
"chinese|Mapo Tofu": {"time":"About 30 minutes","steps":["Cut the silken tofu into cubes and slide them into hot water for 2 minutes so they firm slightly. Drain gently.","Fry the minced pork until it browns and the fat renders. Add the garlic and ginger and cook 30 seconds.","Stir in the doubanjiang and fry it in the pork fat until the fat turns red. Add the Sichuan peppercorn, crushed.","Pour in a cup of water and the tofu. Simmer very gently 5 minutes. Do not stir hard or the tofu breaks into crumbs. Nudge the pan instead.","Taste it. The sauce should be hot, numbing and glossy, pooling around soft tofu. Serve over rice."]},
"chinese|Peking Duck": {"time":"About 2 days of drying, then 1 hour 30 minutes","steps":["Rinse the duck and pat it very dry, inside and out. Pour a kettle of boiling water over the skin so it tightens. Dry it again.","Brush the skin with the maltose syrup loosened with a little warm water. Hang the duck in a cold, airy place, or leave it uncovered in the fridge, until the skin is dry to the touch. This is what makes it lacquer.","Roast on a rack at 190°C until the skin is deep mahogany and crisp and the juices at the thigh run clear, about 1 hour to 1 hour 15 minutes. Rest 15 minutes.","Slice the skin and meat separately. Serve with the mandarin pancakes, hoisin sauce, scallion brushes and cucumber batons. A pancake gets sauce, scallion, cucumber, skin, then meat."]},
"chinese|Spring Rolls": {"time":"About 1 hour","steps":["Stir-fry the pork until cooked. Add the napa cabbage and carrot, cut into thin strips, and cook until the cabbage wilts and its water cooks off. Cool completely. A hot filling makes the wrapper soggy and bursts.","Lay a spring roll wrapper with a corner toward you. Place a line of filling, fold in the sides, and roll tightly, sealing the last corner with water.","Heat the oil for frying to medium-hot. Fry a few at a time until evenly gold and crisp, turning once.","Drain on a rack. The cabbage must be dry inside or the roll steams itself soft.","Eat the day they are fried."]},
"chinese|Xiaolongbao": {"time":"About 2 hours","steps":["The soup inside xiaolongbao is solid aspic, not liquid, when you wrap it. Simmer the pork skin in water with half the ginger until the skin is meltingly soft, about 1 hour. Strain, then chill the liquid until it sets to a jelly. Dice that jelly small.","Mix the flour with a pinch of the salt and warm water to a smooth dough. Knead 8 minutes. Rest, covered, 30 minutes.","Mix the ground pork with the soy sauce, sesame oil, Shaoxing wine, sugar, the rest of the salt, grated ginger and chopped scallion. Stir in one direction until sticky. Fold in the diced jelly.","Roll the dough into thin rounds, thicker in the centre than at the edge. Put a spoon of filling in each. Pleat the edge up into a closed purse with a little topknot. The pleats must seal or the soup leaks.","Line a steamer with cabbage leaves or parchment with holes. Steam over boiling water for 8 minutes, until the wrappers look glossy and the jelly inside has melted. Do not open the lid early.","Eat at once, from the steamer. Pick a bun up by the topknot, bite a corner, and suck the hot soup before you eat the rest. The wrapper should be thin and the soup clear."]},
"egyptian|Basbousa": {"time":"About 50 minutes","steps":["Melt the butter and mix it with the semolina, 80 g of the sugar, the yogurt and baking powder to a thick batter.","Spread it in a buttered tin about 2 cm deep. Score diamonds and press an almond into each.","Bake at 180°C until gold and a skewer comes out clean, about 30 minutes.","Meanwhile simmer the remaining sugar with 120 ml of water and the lemon juice until it is a thin syrup. Cool it slightly.","Pour the cool syrup over the hot cake so it drinks it in. Let it stand 20 minutes.","Basbousa should be moist and grainy from the semolina, sweet but not sticky-wet. Cut along the scored lines."]},
"egyptian|Feteer Meshaltet": {"time":"About 50 minutes","steps":["Mix the flour and salt with water and the milk to a soft dough. Knead 8 minutes. Rest 20 minutes.","Divide into 4. Grease the board with ghee and stretch each piece as thin as you can, until you can nearly see through it.","Brush with melted ghee, fold the edges in to make a layered square, then stretch gently again. Repeat the fold once more so there are many thin layers.","Cook on a hot pan, brushing with ghee, until both sides are blotched gold and the inside is cooked, about 4 minutes a side. Or bake at 220°C until puffed and gold.","The layers should shatter a little at the edge and stay soft in the middle.","Eat hot. Feteer is a flaky layered bread, rich with ghee."]},
"egyptian|Ful Medames": {"time":"About 2 hours, or 20 minutes if the beans are already cooked","steps":["Soak the dried fava beans overnight. Drain, cover with fresh water, and simmer until they are completely soft, 1 to 2 hours.","Drain most of the liquid, keeping a cup. Mash about half the beans and fold them back through the whole ones so the dish is partly crushed, partly whole.","Beat the garlic, cumin, lemon juice and olive oil into the beans. Loosen with a little of the cooking liquid.","Chop the tomato and parsley and the hard-boiled egg.","Serve warm, with more olive oil on top, the tomato, parsley and egg. It should taste of lemon and cumin, not of plain boiled beans."]},
"egyptian|Hawawshi": {"time":"About 40 minutes","steps":["Mix the mince with the grated onion, chopped chilli, tomato, parsley, cumin, salt and olive oil. Fry a teaspoon and taste the seasoning before you fill the bread.","Split each pita along the seam to make a pocket. Fill it with a layer of the meat mixture, not so full that it cannot close.","Press the pocket flat so the meat is an even layer.","Brush the outside lightly with olive oil. Bake at 200°C, or cook in a hot pan under a weight, until the bread is crisp and the meat is cooked through, about 15 minutes, turning once.","Rest 3 minutes. Cut into wedges.","Hawawshi should be crisp outside, the spiced beef juicy inside, the onion cooked by the heat of the meat."]},
"egyptian|Kofta": {"time":"About 30 minutes","steps":["Grate the onion and squeeze out the juice so the kofta does not steam and fall apart. Mix the onion with the mince, parsley, garlic, cumin and salt.","Knead until the mixture is sticky and holds together, about 2 minutes.","Shape onto skewers or into fat fingers. Oil them.","Grill or pan-fry over high heat, turning, until browned and just cooked through, about 8 minutes. Do not press them or the juices run out.","Rest 3 minutes.","The outside should be charred in spots, the inside moist, tasting of parsley, onion and cumin."]},
"egyptian|Koshari": {"time":"About 1 hour","steps":["Cook the rice, the brown lentils and the macaroni separately, each in its own pot, each drained well. Rinse the macaroni so it does not glue itself.","Warm the chickpeas.","Simmer the tomato sauce with the garlic until thick and sharp. Stir the vinegar and chilli flakes into a little of the sauce, or serve that hot sauce beside it.","Cook the onion slowly in a dry pan, stirring, until dark brown and crisp. Drain it.","In a bowl, layer rice, lentils, macaroni and chickpeas. Spoon over the tomato sauce and finish with a handful of the fried onion.","Serve the vinegar-chilli on the side so each person can make it as hot as they want."]},
"egyptian|Mahshi": {"time":"About 1 hour 15 minutes","steps":["Hollow the zucchini with a corer, leaving a wall about 5 mm thick. Salt the insides lightly.","Mix the uncooked rice with the chopped onion, parsley, mint, half the tomato, the olive oil and salt.","Fill each zucchini loosely. The rice swells; a tight pack will split the vegetable.","Lay them in a pot on the rest of the sliced tomato. Add water to come halfway up, and the lemon juice. A plate on top keeps them from bobbing.","Simmer covered until the rice is cooked and the zucchini is tender, about 45 minutes.","Rest 10 minutes. Mahshi should be lemony, the rice separate, the zucchini soft but holding its shape."]},
"egyptian|Molokhia": {"time":"About 35 minutes","steps":["Simmer the chicken with the onion and salt until tender. Keep the broth. Shred or joint the chicken.","If the molokhia is whole, chop it very fine. Frozen chopped molokhia can go in as it is.","Bring 800 ml of the chicken broth to a boil. Add the molokhia and cook only 4 minutes, stirring. It should turn silky and slightly slippery. Do not boil it long or it loses its green and can turn stringy.","Crush the garlic with the coriander. Fry them in the ghee until the garlic is gold and fragrant, about 1 minute.","Pour the garlic ghee into the pot and stir once. The taqliya is the whole fragrance of the soup.","Serve the chicken in the green broth. It should taste of garlic, coriander and chicken, the leaves soft."]},
"egyptian|Taameya": {"time":"About 30 minutes, plus soaking","steps":["Soak the fava beans overnight. They stay green inside; do not cook them first. Drain and blend with the onion, garlic, coriander, cumin and salt to a coarse paste, using almost no water.","Rest 15 minutes. The paste should hold a ball.","Shape flat patties and press sesame onto both sides.","Heat the oil to medium. Fry until deep gold and cooked through, about 4 minutes, turning once.","Drain. The outside should be crisp, the inside green, herby and soft, not wet and raw.","Taameya is the Egyptian fava falafel, brighter and herbier than chickpea falafel."]},
"egyptian|Umm Ali": {"time":"About 40 minutes","steps":["Bake the puff pastry at 200°C until deep gold and dry, about 15 minutes. Break it into pieces.","Warm the milk with 60 g of the sugar until the sugar dissolves. Do not boil it hard.","Layer the pastry in a dish with the almonds, raisins and coconut. Pour the sweet milk over. It should come nearly to the top.","Pour the cream over and scatter the rest of the sugar.","Bake at 200°C until the top is blistered and gold and the milk has thickened around the pastry, about 15 minutes.","Rest 10 minutes. Umm Ali should be creamy, the pastry soft in places and crisp on top, the nuts and raisins through it."]},
"ethiopian|Atkilt": {"time":"About 35 minutes","steps":["Cut the potato and carrot into chunks and shred the cabbage. Slice the onion.","Cook the onion in the niter kibbeh until soft. Add the garlic, ginger and turmeric and fry 1 minute.","Add the carrot and potato with the salt and a small cup of water. Cover and cook 10 minutes.","Add the cabbage. Cover and cook until the potato is tender and the cabbage is soft, about 10 minutes. There should be little liquid left.","Atkilt is a mild turmeric vegetable stew, the vegetables distinct, not mashed.","Taste the salt. Rest 5 minutes."]},
"ethiopian|Doro Wat": {"time":"About 1 hour 45 minutes","steps":["Slice the red onion very thin. Cook it in a dry pot, stirring, until it softens and sweetens and the moisture cooks off, about 15 minutes. This long onion base is the wat.","Add the niter kibbeh and the garlic and ginger. Cook 5 minutes.","Stir in the berbere and a splash of water. Fry the spice until the fat turns red.","Add the chicken and enough water to make a thick sauce. Cover and simmer until the chicken is tender, about 40 minutes.","Score the hard-boiled eggs all over and tuck them into the sauce for the last 10 minutes so they take on the berbere.","The sauce should be thick enough to stand on injera. Rest 10 minutes."]},
"ethiopian|Firfir": {"time":"About 20 minutes","steps":["Tear the injera into pieces. Day-old injera works best because it holds together.","Cook the sliced onion until soft. Add the niter kibbeh, garlic, berbere and tomato. Fry until you have a thick red sauce, about 6 minutes. Add a splash of water and the salt.","Add the injera and toss until every piece is coated and softened but not dissolved into porridge, about 2 minutes.","If it is dry, add a spoon of water. Firfir should be moist and scoopable.","Taste. It should be spicy and tangy from the injera.","Eat at once, warm. It is a way of using leftover injera, and it does not improve by sitting."]},
"ethiopian|Gomen": {"time":"About 25 minutes","steps":["Strip the collard stems and slice the leaves thin. Blanch them in boiling water for 3 minutes, then drain and squeeze. This takes the bitterness down.","Cook the sliced onion in the niter kibbeh until soft. Add the garlic, ginger and slit chilli.","Add the greens and salt. Fry, stirring, until they are tender and coated in the niter kibbeh, about 8 minutes.","Add a spoon of water only if they threaten to catch. Gomen is a dry fry of greens, not a soup.","Taste the salt.","The greens should be soft, fragrant with ginger and niter kibbeh, still green."]},
"ethiopian|Injera": {"time":"About 3 days of fermenting, then 30 minutes of cooking","steps":["Mix the teff flour with water and the yeast or starter to a smooth, thin batter, like a crepe batter. Use a clean bowl.","Cover it loosely and leave it at room temperature. Stir it once a day. It is ready when it smells sour and is full of small bubbles, usually 2 to 3 days. The sourness is the bread.","Heat a wide non-stick pan or a mitad. Heat it until a drop of water skitters across the surface.","Pour batter in a spiral from the outside in, or in one pour, to cover the pan in a thin layer. Do not spread it like a pancake; tilt the pan.","Cover and cook until the top is set, full of eyes, and the edge lifts. Do not flip it. The underside should be barely coloured.","Cool in a stack under a cloth so they stay flexible. Injera is the plate: lay stew on it and tear pieces to eat."]},
"ethiopian|Kik Alicha": {"time":"About 40 minutes","steps":["Rinse the split peas and simmer them until soft, about 25 minutes. They should break but not turn to glue yet.","Cook the sliced onion in a dry pan until sweet, about 10 minutes. Add the niter kibbeh, garlic, ginger and turmeric. Fry until the onion is yellow and fragrant.","Add the peas and salt, with a splash of the cooking water. Simmer 10 minutes, mashing some of the peas so the stew thickens.","Alicha is the mild stew, turmeric-yellow, without berbere. Do not add chilli.","It should be thick enough to scoop.","Rest 5 minutes. The ginger and niter kibbeh should be clear, the peas soft."]},
"ethiopian|Kitfo": {"time":"About 15 minutes","steps":["Mince the lean raw beef very fine with a sharp knife, at the last moment. It must be fresh, cold, and from a cut you trust to eat raw. This is not a place for meat that has been sitting.","Warm the niter kibbeh until it is just liquid, not hot enough to cook the meat.","Fold the niter kibbeh and the mitmita through the beef. The meat should be coated and red, not grey. If the niter kibbeh is hot, you have made lebleb, the lightly cooked version, which is a different dish.","Serve at once with the ayib alongside, and injera.","Eat it the minute it is mixed."]},
"ethiopian|Misir Wat": {"time":"About 45 minutes","steps":["Slice the onion fine. Cook it in a dry pan on medium, stirring, until it softens and sweetens and the moisture cooks off, about 15 minutes. Ethiopian wat starts with the onion cooked down until it is sweet.","Add the niter kibbeh, garlic and ginger. Fry 2 minutes.","Add the berbere and fry it in the niter kibbeh until the raw spice smell goes and the onion turns deep red, about 3 minutes. Add a spoon of water if it sticks.","Rinse the lentils and add them with water to cover by 3 cm, and the salt. Simmer until the lentils collapse into a thick stew, about 20 minutes.","Stir often near the end. The wat should be thick enough to scoop with injera, not a soup.","Rest 10 minutes. Misir wat is spicy, onion-sweet and red from the berbere."]},
"ethiopian|Shiro": {"time":"About 30 minutes","steps":["Cook the sliced onion in a dry pan until soft and sweet, about 10 minutes.","Add the niter kibbeh, garlic, berbere and tomato. Fry until the fat separates and the paste is fragrant.","Whisk the chickpea flour with 500 ml of cold water so there are no lumps.","Pour it into the pan, whisking. Add the salt. Cook, stirring, until the shiro thickens to a smooth porridge that holds a ribbon, about 10 minutes.","If it is stiff, add a splash of water. It should be pourable and silky, not a dry paste.","Rest 5 minutes. Shiro is a chickpea stew, spicy and smooth, eaten with injera."]},
"ethiopian|Tibs": {"time":"About 30 minutes","steps":["Cut the beef into strips. Salt it.","Heat the niter kibbeh until hot. Sear the beef in batches so it browns, about 2 minutes. Set it aside still pink inside.","In the same pan, cook the sliced onion until soft. Add the garlic, rosemary and berbere and fry 1 minute.","Add the tomato and cook until it softens.","Return the beef and its juices just to warm through. Do not boil it or the strips turn tough.","Tibs should be a lively fry, the meat tender, the berbere fragrant, with a little sauce but not a stew."]},
"filipino|Adobo": {"time":"About 50 minutes","steps":["Put the chicken and pork belly in a pot with the soy sauce, cane vinegar, crushed garlic, bay leaves and whole peppercorn. Do not stir the vinegar in yet if you can help it; many cooks bring the vinegar to a boil untouched so it cooks off its raw edge.","Bring to a boil, then simmer uncovered until the meat is tender and the sauce has reduced to a dark, salty-sour glaze, about 35 to 40 minutes. Turn the meat now and then.","If you want it richer, let the pork fat render at the end so the sauce fries a little.","Rest 10 minutes. The peppercorns and bay stay in the pot.","Serve with rice. Adobo is better the next day."]},
"filipino|Arroz Caldo": {"time":"About 45 minutes","steps":["Slice the ginger into thin coins and the onion fine. Heat the oil and fry the ginger until the edges colour. This ginger fry is the backbone of arroz caldo; do not rush it.","Add the garlic and onion and cook until soft. Add the chicken pieces and brown them lightly.","Rinse the glutinous rice and stir it in until every grain looks glossy. Add the fish sauce, salt and annatto or saffron.","Pour in water to cover by 4 cm. Simmer, stirring often so the rice does not catch, until the rice breaks down into a thick porridge and the chicken is tender, about 30 minutes. Add water if it becomes stiff. It should fall from the spoon in a ribbon.","The chicken can be pulled into shreds and stirred back, or left in pieces on top.","Finish with scallion and lemon. Arroz caldo is ginger-hot and savoury, thick enough to eat with a spoon, brightened by the lemon."]},
"filipino|Chicken Inasal": {"time":"About 40 minutes, plus marinating","steps":["Cut the chicken into serving pieces, bone in. Pound the garlic, ginger and lemongrass with the vinegar, annatto, salt and sugar. Coat the chicken and rest it at least 2 hours.","The annatto stains the meat orange. If the marinade looks thin, that is right; inasal is not a thick paste.","Grill or roast at a high heat, turning and brushing with the oil, until the skin chars in spots and the juices at the thigh run clear, about 25 minutes.","Baste once more in the last 5 minutes with the leftover marinade, boiled first for 1 minute so it is safe, then reduced until sticky.","Rest 8 minutes. Cutting too soon spills the juices and the meat looks dry even when it is not.","Chicken inasal should taste sour from the vinegar, a little sweet, and smell of lemongrass and char."]},
"filipino|Halo-Halo": {"time":"About 30 minutes, plus chilling","steps":["Chill every sweet component. Halo-halo fails if the beans and jackfruit are warm; the ice dies at once.","In a tall glass, layer the red beans, jackfruit, coconut gel and a spoon of ube.","Pack the glass with shaved ice, higher than the rim. Crush it only enough to pack; large wet cubes turn the glass into juice.","Pour evaporated milk over the ice so it runs down in streaks.","Crown with a slice of leche flan and a thread of palm sugar syrup if the sweets are not already sweet enough. Taste a bean first.","Serve with a long spoon and mix only at the table. The point is the cold hit of ice, milk, purple ube and the chew of the beans and gel."]},
"filipino|Kare-Kare": {"time":"About 2 hours","steps":["Cover the oxtail with water and simmer until the meat is tender and the broth is rich, about 1 hour 30 minutes. Skim. Lift the meat out and keep 500 ml of broth.","Slice the onion. Heat the oil and fry it with the garlic and annatto until the oil turns orange and the onion is soft. Annatto is the colour; it should not taste bitter, so do not burn it.","Stir in the peanut butter and the broth. Simmer until the sauce is thick enough to coat the oxtail, about 10 minutes. Add the salt.","Return the oxtail. Add the eggplant, beans and banana blossom. Simmer until the vegetables are tender but the eggplant still holds a shape, about 10 minutes.","The sauce should be peanut-thick and savoury, not sweet. Kare-kare is not a spicy curry.","Serve the shrimp paste on the side, a small spoon on each plate. Stir only as much as you want into the sauce. The paste is salty and funky; the stew itself stays mild."]},
"filipino|Lechon Kawali": {"time":"About 1 hour 20 minutes","steps":["Cut the pork belly into large slabs. Cover with water, add the garlic, bay leaves, pepper and half the salt, and simmer until the meat is tender, about 45 minutes. The skin should be soft, not yet crisp.","Lift the pork out and pat it completely dry. Score the skin. Rub with the rest of the salt. Chill uncovered for 20 minutes so the skin dries further. Wet skin will not blister.","Heat the oil until a wooden skewer bubbles hard. Fry the pork skin side down first, then turn, until the skin puffs and turns deep gold, about 8 minutes. Stand back; water in the skin spatters.","Drain and rest 5 minutes, then chop into pieces. The skin should shatter, the fat be clear, and the meat still moist.","Stir the vinegar and soy sauce together as a dipping sauce. Do not pour it over the pork or the crust dies.","Serve the pork hot with the sauce on the side. Lechon kawali is fried belly, not a roast, and the crackling is the point."]},
"filipino|Lumpia": {"time":"About 45 minutes","steps":["Shred the carrot and cabbage fine. Fry the garlic, then the pork, until the pork is brown and its liquid has gone. Add the vegetables and cook until the cabbage wilts and the pan is dry. Season with soy sauce, pepper and salt. Cool completely. A hot filling steams the wrapper and splits it.","Lay a wrapper with a corner toward you. Place a spoon of filling, fold the corner over, tuck the sides, and roll tightly. Seal the last corner with a dab of water.","Heat the oil to medium. A scrap of wrapper should puff in a few seconds, not burn at once.","Fry the lumpia in batches until even gold and crisp, about 3 minutes, turning once.","Drain on a rack. Stacking them while hot softens the crust.","Eat the hour they are fried. The wrapper should shatter, the filling moist but not wet."]},
"filipino|Pancit": {"time":"About 35 minutes","steps":["Slice the chicken and shred the cabbage and carrot. Soak the rice noodles in warm water until they bend, then drain.","Heat the oil and fry the garlic until pale gold. Add the chicken and cook until just done. Set the chicken aside.","In the same pan, toss the carrot and cabbage until the cabbage wilts but stays bright, about 2 minutes.","Add the noodles, soy sauce, fish sauce, salt and 100 ml of water. Toss until the noodles are tender and have drunk the liquid, about 4 minutes. They should be glossy, not soupy.","Return the chicken and toss once.","Serve with lemon cut in wedges. Pancit is finished with lemon at the table, and the noodles should taste savoury, not heavy with oil."]},
"filipino|Sinigang": {"time":"About 1 hour","steps":["Cover the pork ribs with water and simmer until nearly tender, about 40 minutes. Skim the foam.","Add the tomato, radish and eggplant. Cook until the radish is starting to soften.","Stir in the tamarind mix. Taste. Sinigang must be frankly sour. Add more tamarind if it tastes mild.","Add the kangkong in the last 2 minutes so it stays green.","Serve as a sour soup with rice. The ribs should be tender and the broth clear, not thick."]},
"filipino|Sisig": {"time":"About 1 hour 30 minutes","steps":["Boil the pork face or cheeks and the pork liver until the cheeks are tender. Drain and chop everything fine, liver included but not so much that it turns the pan bitter.","Fry the chopped pork in its own fat until the edges are crisp. Add the chopped onion and green chilli.","Season with calamansi. Keep frying until some pieces are crunchy and some are still soft.","Serve on a hot plate. Crack the egg on top at the table and mix it through so it half-cooks on the sizzling meat.","Scatter the crushed chicharon. Eat with rice while it still crackles."]},
"french|Bouillabaisse": {"time":"About 1 hour","steps":["Cut the fish into large pieces, firmer fish separate from delicate ones. Slice the fennel and onion.","Heat the olive oil and cook the fennel, onion and garlic until soft. Add the tomato, orange peel, saffron and salt. Pour in water to make a broth and simmer 15 minutes. The broth should taste of fennel and saffron before any fish goes in.","Add the firm fish first and simmer 5 minutes. Add the delicate fish and cook until just opaque, about 5 minutes more. Do not stir hard.","Taste the salt. The broth should be fragrant and light, not a thick stew.","Toast the baguette.","Serve the fish in the broth, with the bread to soak. Lift the orange peel out. Bouillabaisse is a fish soup of the catch, the saffron and fennel telling you where it is from."]},
"french|Cassoulet": {"time":"About 3 hours","steps":["Soak the beans overnight. Simmer them with the onion, garlic and thyme until just tender, about 45 minutes. Salt near the end.","Brown the duck legs and set them aside. Brown the pork belly and the sausage in the duck fat.","Add the tomato to the pot and cook it down. Stir in the beans and their liquid.","Nestle the duck, belly and sausage into the beans. The liquid should just cover. Bake at 160°C, uncovered, for 1 hour 30 minutes.","Every 30 minutes, break the crust that forms and let it sink back. That repeated crust is the cassoulet.","The beans should be creamy, the meats tender, the top brown. Rest 15 minutes. It is a thick pot, not a soup."]},
"french|Coq au Vin": {"time":"About 1 hour 45 minutes","steps":["Brown the bacon lardons and set them aside. In the bacon fat, brown the chicken pieces and set them aside too.","Fry the pearl onions and mushrooms in the same pot until coloured. Add the carrot.","Return the chicken. Pour in the red Burgundy and the chicken stock. Add the thyme and the bay leaf. The wine should come most of the way up the chicken.","Cover and simmer gently until the chicken is tender, about 45 to 60 minutes.","Lift out the chicken. Boil the sauce to thicken it, or mash a little of the butter with flour and whisk it in. Return the chicken, lardons and mushrooms.","Finish with the rest of the butter, swirled in off the heat. The sauce should be glossy and taste of wine, not of raw alcohol."]},
"french|Crème Brûlée": {"time":"About 1 hour, plus chilling","steps":["Split the vanilla bean and scrape the seeds into the heavy cream. Warm the cream with the pod until it just steams. Do not boil.","Beat the egg yolks with half the sugar. Pour in the hot cream slowly, whisking. Strain into a jug.","Pour into shallow ramekins. Bake in a bain-marie at 150°C until the centres still tremble, about 30 to 40 minutes.","Cool, then chill at least 4 hours.","Just before serving, sprinkle the rest of the sugar in a thin even layer and caramelise it with a flame or a very hot grill until it is a hard amber sheet. Serve within the hour, while the top still cracks."]},
"french|Crêpes": {"time":"About 30 minutes","steps":["Whisk the flour, eggs, sugar and salt. Add the milk a little at a time until the batter is as thin as single cream. Rest 20 minutes. A rested batter spreads; a fresh one tears.","Melt a knob of the butter in a hot non-stick pan and pour it into the batter, leaving a film in the pan.","Pour a small ladle of batter and swirl it thin. Cook until the edge lifts and the underside is spotted, about 1 minute. Flip and cook 20 seconds.","Stack the crêpes with a dot of butter between them so they stay supple.","Serve with the rest of the butter and the lemon, squeezed over. They should be thin enough to see a shadow through, tender, not rubbery.","If the first one sticks, the pan was not hot enough. The second will be better."]},
"french|French Onion Soup": {"time":"About 1 hour 15 minutes","steps":["Slice the onions thin. Melt the butter and cook the onions with the thyme on medium-low, stirring often, until they are deep brown and sweet, about 40 minutes. Pale onions make a dull soup. Do not rush this.","Add the wine and let it cook away. Add the stock and salt. Simmer 15 minutes.","Toast the baguette slices until dry and crisp.","Ladle the soup into oven bowls. Float the toast and cover it with grated Gruyère.","Grill or bake until the cheese is bubbling and spotted brown.","The soup underneath should be dark and sweet, the cheese a molten lid. Rest 2 minutes so you do not burn your mouth on the first spoon."]},
"french|Quiche Lorraine": {"time":"About 1 hour 10 minutes","steps":["Rub the butter into the flour with a pinch of salt until it looks like breadcrumbs. Add just enough cold water to make a dough. Rest it 30 minutes in the fridge.","Roll the pastry and line a tart tin. Prick the base. Line with paper and bake at 190°C for 15 minutes, then remove the paper and bake 8 minutes more until the base is dry and pale gold. A wet base makes a soggy quiche.","Fry the lardons until the fat renders and the edges are crisp. Scatter them in the tart with the grated Gruyère.","Whisk the eggs, cream, the rest of the salt and the pepper. Pour over the bacon.","Bake at 180°C until the centre is just set and still has a slight wobble, about 25 minutes. A hard centre means it overcooked and will be rubbery.","Rest 10 minutes. Quiche Lorraine is bacon, cream and cheese in pastry, no extra vegetables."]},
"french|Ratatouille": {"time":"About 1 hour 15 minutes","steps":["Pat the eggplant and zucchini dry. Cut all the vegetables a similar size.","Cook the eggplant, zucchini and bell pepper separately in the olive oil until each is tender and lightly coloured. Crowding them together at the start makes a stew.","In the same pan, soften the onion and garlic. Add the tomato and cook until thick.","Fold the cooked vegetables back in with the torn basil. Simmer gently 15 minutes so they swap flavours but keep their shape.","Rest 10 minutes. It is better warm than screaming hot, and better again the next day. Finish with a thread of olive oil."]},
"french|Salade Niçoise": {"time":"About 30 minutes","steps":["Boil the potato until just tender, and the green beans until bright and still crisp. Boil the eggs for 8 minutes so the yolks are just set. Cool them all.","If the tuna is raw, sear it quickly so the centre stays pink, then flake or slice it. If it is oil-packed, drain it.","Cut the tomato into wedges and the potato into slices. Halve the eggs.","Arrange, do not toss into a mush: beans, potato, tomato, tuna, eggs, olives and anchovy.","Dress with olive oil, lemon juice and salt at the last minute.","Salade niçoise should taste of olive oil and lemon, the vegetables distinct, the anchovy salty against the mild potato."]},
"french|Tarte Tatin": {"time":"About 1 hour 10 minutes","steps":["Rub 60 g of the butter into the flour and salt. Add just enough cold water to make a dough. Rest it 30 minutes.","Peel and quarter the apples. They must be firm or they turn to sauce.","In an ovenproof pan, melt the rest of the butter with the sugar until the sugar dissolves and turns a deep amber caramel. Do not stir once it colours or it may crystallise; swirl the pan.","Pack the apples in tight, rounded side down. Cook on the stove 10 minutes until they start to soften and the caramel darkens.","Roll the pastry to cover the pan and tuck the edges down inside, around the apples. Bake at 190°C until the pastry is gold, about 30 minutes.","Rest 5 minutes, then invert onto a plate in one turn. The apples should be glazed and dark, the pastry crisp. If caramel sticks in the pan, warm it and pour it over the tart."]},
"german|Black Forest Cake": {"time":"About 1 hour 20 minutes, plus chilling","steps":["Whip the eggs with 100 g of the sugar and the salt until thick and pale. Fold in the flour and cocoa. Do not deflate the eggs.","Bake in a lined tin at 170°C until a skewer comes out clean, about 25 minutes. Cool and split into three layers.","Simmer the cherries with 30 g of the sugar until they soften and give a syrup. Stir in the kirsch. Cool.","Whip the cream with the rest of the sugar until it holds soft peaks.","Brush each cake layer with cherry syrup. Spread cream and cherries between the layers and over the top.","Chill at least 2 hours. The cake should be light from the eggs, the cherries sharp, the cream still soft, not whipped until it turns grainy."]},
"german|Bratwurst": {"time":"About 25 minutes","steps":["Do not prick the sausages or the fat runs out and they dry. Warm them gently in a pan with a spoon of water, covered, for 8 minutes so they cook through without splitting.","Uncover, add the butter, and brown them on all sides until the skins are blotched and taut.","Slice the onion and cook it in the same fat until soft and sweet.","Warm the sauerkraut with a pinch of salt. If it is very sour, rinse it first.","Serve the sausages with the onion, sauerkraut and mustard.","The sausage should be juicy when cut, the skin browned, not grey and boiled."]},
"german|Currywurst": {"time":"About 20 minutes","steps":["Warm the ketchup with the sugar, Worcestershire, half the curry powder and the paprika until the sugar dissolves and the sauce smells of curry. Do not boil it hard.","Fry or grill the sausages in the oil until browned and cooked through. Do not prick them first.","Slice them into thick coins.","Pour the warm sauce over the slices.","Dust generously with the rest of the curry powder so you can see it.","Currywurst is street food: sliced sausage, sweet-tangy curry ketchup, eaten hot."]},
"german|Kartoffelsalat": {"time":"About 35 minutes","steps":["Boil the potatoes in their skins until just tender. Peel them while hot and slice them. Hot potato drinks the dressing; cold potato stays dry.","Slice the onion thin and chop the pickle.","Whisk the mustard, vinegar, oil and salt. Pour it over the hot potato with the onion and pickle. Fold gently so the slices stay whole.","Rest 15 minutes. Taste the salt and vinegar. A southern German salad is sharp, bound by potato starch and oil, not by mayonnaise.","Fold in the chives.","Serve just warm or at room temperature. It should be glossy and tangy."]},
"german|Pretzel": {"time":"About 1 hour 30 minutes","steps":["Mix the flour, yeast, sugar, 8 g of the salt, the butter and 230 ml of warm water. Knead 8 minutes. Rise 45 minutes.","Divide into 8. Roll long ropes and twist them into pretzels, the ends pressed onto the loop.","Bring 1 litre of water to a simmer and stir in the baking soda. This bath is the stand-in for lye; it browns the crust and gives the pretzel taste. Do not skip it.","Dip each pretzel for 20 seconds, drain, and set on a tray. Sprinkle with the rest of the salt.","Bake at 220°C until deep brown, about 15 minutes.","The crust should be shiny and dark, the inside soft and chewy. Eat warm."]},
"german|Rouladen": {"time":"About 2 hours","steps":["Pound the steaks thin. Salt and pepper them and spread each with mustard.","Lay on a strip of bacon, a stick of pickle and a slice of onion. Roll tightly and tie with string.","Brown the rolls in the oil on all sides. Browning builds the sauce; pale rouladen taste boiled.","Add the rest of the onion and the stock. Cover and simmer until the beef is tender, about 1 hour 30 minutes. Turn them once.","Lift the rolls out. Reduce the sauce until it coats a spoon, and taste the salt.","Remove the string and serve the rolls with the sauce. The centre should show pickle, bacon and onion in a spiral."]},
"german|Sauerbraten": {"time":"About 3 hours, plus marinating","steps":["Put the beef in a bowl with the sliced onion and carrot, vinegar, juniper, bay, salt and enough water to cover. Refrigerate for 2 days, turning daily. The sour marinade is the dish.","Lift the meat out and pat it dry. Strain the marinade and keep the vegetables and the liquid apart.","Brown the beef in the oil. Add the vegetables and brown them. Pour in enough marinade to come halfway up the meat.","Cover and simmer until the beef is tender, about 2 hours.","Lift the meat out. Crumble the gingerbread into the sauce with the sugar and simmer until the sauce thickens. The gingerbread sweetens and thickens without flour.","Slice the beef and nap it with the sauce. It should be sour, slightly sweet, and tender."]},
"german|Sauerkraut": {"time":"About 40 minutes","steps":["If the sauerkraut is very salty or sharp, rinse it and squeeze it dry. Taste it first; good kraut needs only a rinse of the brine, not a wash that takes all the life out.","Slice the onion and the apple.","Melt the butter and cook the onion until soft. Add the apple, juniper and caraway.","Add the sauerkraut and a splash of water. Cover and cook on low for 30 minutes, until it is tender and smells sweet from the apple and caraway.","Taste the salt. Add only if it is flat.","Serve hot. It should be tangy, not raw and crunchy, and not cooked to grey mush."]},
"german|Schnitzel": {"time":"About 30 minutes","steps":["Pound the pork cutlets thin between plastic so they do not tear. Nick the edges.","Dust with flour, dip in the beaten egg, and press into the breadcrumbs. Shake off the excess.","Heat the butter in a wide pan until it foams. Fry one or two schnitzels at a time so the pan stays hot, until both sides are gold and the crust is bubbling, about 2 minutes a side.","Drain on paper. The crust should stand off the meat, not stick to it.","Serve at once with the lemon. Butter browns quickly; if it darkens past nut-brown, wipe the pan and start the next batch with fresh butter."]},
"german|Spätzle": {"time":"About 30 minutes","steps":["Beat the flour, eggs, milk and salt to a thick, sticky batter that slowly drops from a spoon. Rest 15 minutes. Too thin and the dumplings dissolve; too stiff and they are heavy.","Bring a pot of water to a boil with a pinch of the salt.","Press the batter through a spätzle maker or off a board with a wet knife, in small ribbons, into the water.","When they float, give them 1 minute more, then lift them out.","Toss them in the butter in a hot pan until the edges turn gold in spots.","Spätzle should be tender and a little chewy, buttery, and able to hold a sauce."]},
"greek|Avgolemono": {"time":"About 40 minutes","steps":["Cover the chicken with water, add the salt, and simmer until tender, about 30 minutes. Lift the chicken out and shred the meat. Keep 1 litre of broth.","Add the rice to the broth and simmer until the rice is soft, about 15 minutes.","Whisk the eggs with the lemon juice. This is the thickening. Take the pot off the heat.","Ladle hot broth slowly into the eggs, whisking, so they warm without scrambling. Then pour the egg mixture back into the pot, whisking.","Return the shredded chicken. Warm the soup gently. If it boils, the egg curdles and the soup breaks.","Finish with dill. Avgolemono should be silky, lemon-sour and pale, the rice swollen in the broth."]},
"greek|Baklava": {"time":"About 1 hour 15 minutes","steps":["Chop the walnuts and pistachios with the cinnamon until coarse, not a powder. A powder makes a paste; a coarse nut stays crisp.","Melt the butter. Brush a tin and layer half the phyllo, brushing each sheet. Spread the nuts. Layer the rest of the phyllo, brushing each sheet, including the top.","Cut into diamonds before baking, or the baked phyllo shatters when you cut it. Bake at 170°C until deep gold, about 40 minutes.","While it bakes, boil the sugar with 120 ml of water and a strip of lemon peel for 6 minutes. Cool the syrup slightly.","Pour the warm syrup over the hot baklava. It should hiss. Leave it at least 2 hours so the syrup soaks through.","The top must stay crisp, the centre sticky with syrup and nut. Do not refrigerate or the phyllo turns leathery."]},
"greek|Dolmades": {"time":"About 1 hour 20 minutes","steps":["Rinse the grape leaves and blanch them until they bend. Cut off the stems.","Mix the rice with grated onion, dill, mint, half the olive oil and the salt. The rice stays raw; it cooks in the parcel.","Roll tight slim parcels, seam down.","Pack them in a pot. Add the rest of the oil, the lemon juice and water to cover. Weight with a plate.","Simmer 40 minutes, until the rice is tender and the liquid is almost gone.","Cool to warm. Dolmades should be lemony and herby, the leaf silky, the rice fully cooked."]},
"greek|Gyros": {"time":"About 30 minutes, plus marinating","steps":["Slice the pork thin. Mix it with crushed garlic, oregano, lemon juice, olive oil and salt. Rest at least 1 hour.","Heat a pan until very hot. Fry the pork in batches so it browns instead of steaming, about 2 minutes a side.","Stack the cooked slices and let them rest 3 minutes, then chop them coarse.","Warm the pita. Slice the tomato and onion.","Fill the bread with pork, tomato and onion. The juices of the meat should hit the bread.","Gyros made in a pan will not have the vertical spit crust, but the meat should still be seasoned, browned and juicy, not boiled grey."]},
"greek|Horiatiki": {"time":"About 15 minutes","steps":["Cut the tomato into wedges, the cucumber into thick half-moons, the onion into thin slices and the pepper into rings. Do not dice them small. Horiatiki is a chunky village salad.","Put them in a bowl with the olives. Salt the vegetables, not the cheese.","Lay the feta on top in one slab. Do not crumble it through.","Pour the olive oil over the feta and vegetables. Crush the oregano over the cheese.","Do not toss it into a mess before it reaches the table. The oil and tomato juice become the dressing as you eat.","Serve at once. There is no lettuce in this salad."]},
"greek|Moussaka": {"time":"About 2 hours","steps":["Slice the eggplant and leave the slices 20 minutes. Pat dry and brush with olive oil. Roast or fry until soft and golden.","Brown the ground lamb. Add the chopped onion and cook until soft. Stir in the cinnamon and tomato sauce. Simmer until thick, 20 minutes.","Slice and parboil the potato, or roast it with a little oil, until just tender.","Warm the béchamel and stir in most of the parmesan.","Layer potato, eggplant and meat sauce in a dish. Repeat. Pour the béchamel over and scatter the rest of the parmesan.","Bake at 180°C for about 40 minutes, until the top is brown and bubbling. Rest 20 minutes before cutting."]},
"greek|Pastitsio": {"time":"About 1 hour 20 minutes","steps":["Fry the onion in the olive oil, add the beef and cook until brown. Add the tomato, cinnamon and half the salt. Simmer until thick, about 20 minutes. The cinnamon should be a warm note, not a dessert.","Boil the pasta in salted water until just shy of done. Drain.","Melt the butter, stir in the flour, and cook 1 minute. Whisk in the milk and the rest of the salt. Simmer until thick. Take it off the heat and stir in the parmesan and egg.","In a baking dish, layer half the pasta, all the meat, then the rest of the pasta. Pour the sauce over.","Bake at 180°C until the top is spotted brown and set, about 30 minutes.","Rest 15 minutes before cutting. Pastitsio should slice into squares, the sauce set, the meat cinnamon-scented."]},
"greek|Souvlaki": {"time":"About 30 minutes, plus marinating","steps":["Cut the pork shoulder into cubes. Mix with the olive oil, lemon juice, garlic and oregano. Marinate at least 1 hour.","Thread onto skewers. Grill over high heat, turning, until browned and just cooked through. Shoulder stays juicier than loin.","Warm the pita.","Slide the pork onto the bread with tomato and a spoon of tzatziki.","Squeeze more lemon over the meat. Eat while the pita is warm."]},
"greek|Spanakopita": {"time":"About 1 hour","steps":["Chop the spinach and salt it. Leave 10 minutes, then squeeze it fiercely dry. Wet spinach makes a soggy pie. Chop the onion and dill.","Mix the spinach, onion, dill, crumbled feta and egg. Taste before you add more salt; feta is already salty.","Brush a tin with olive oil. Layer half the phyllo, brushing each sheet with oil. Let the edges hang over.","Spread the filling. Fold the edges in and layer the rest of the phyllo on top, brushing each sheet.","Score the top into squares so it cuts cleanly later. Bake at 180°C until deep gold and crisp, about 40 minutes.","Rest 10 minutes. The phyllo should shatter, the filling moist and green, not watery."]},
"greek|Tzatziki": {"time":"About 20 minutes, plus draining","steps":["If the yogurt is thin, drain it in a cloth for 1 hour until it is thick. Tzatziki made with watery yogurt splits.","Grate the cucumber, salt it, and leave 10 minutes. Squeeze it dry in your fist. This water is the enemy of the sauce.","Crush the garlic with the rest of the salt.","Stir the cucumber, garlic, olive oil, lemon juice and chopped dill into the yogurt.","Taste. It should be thick, garlicky and fresh, not sour-harsh. Rest 20 minutes so the garlic blooms.","Serve cold. Do not blend it smooth; the cucumber should still be in shreds."]},
"indian|Biryani": {"time":"About 2 hours","steps":["Wash the basmati rice and soak it 30 minutes. Cut the goat meat into pieces on the bone.","Slice the onion and fry it slowly in half the ghee until deep brown. Lift half of it out for the layers.","In the same ghee, brown the goat with the biryani masala. Stir in the yogurt and cook until it clings to the meat. Add hot water to barely cover and simmer until the goat is tender, about 50 to 70 minutes. The gravy must be thick.","Boil the soaked rice in plenty of water until it is three-quarters done. Drain.","Warm the saffron in a spoon of hot water or a little of the meat gravy.","In a heavy pot, layer meat and gravy, then rice, then the fried onion and torn mint. Repeat, finishing with rice. Pour over the remaining ghee and the saffron.","Seal the lid and cook on high for 4 minutes, then on the lowest heat for 25 minutes. Rest 10 minutes before opening."]},
"indian|Butter Chicken": {"time":"About 1 hour 15 minutes","steps":["Cut the skinless chicken thighs into bite-sized pieces. Mix them with the yogurt, a squeeze of the lemon or lime, half the garlic paste, half the ginger paste, half the garam masala, the Kashmiri chilli powder, turmeric and salt. Marinate 30 minutes, or overnight.","Heat the vegetable oil with a spoon of the butter. Fry the chicken in batches until just coloured, not cooked through. Set it aside.","In the same pan melt the rest of the butter. Add the remaining ginger and garlic pastes and the slit green chilli. Cook 1 minute. Stir in the roasted cumin powder and the rest of the garam masala.","Add the tomato paste and a splash of water. Simmer until the sauce darkens and the butter separates, about 10 minutes.","Pour in the heavy cream. Return the chicken and any juices. Simmer gently 12 to 15 minutes until the chicken is cooked and the sauce is thick enough to coat it.","If you are using the kasoori methi, crush it between your palms and stir it in off the heat. Finish with a last squeeze of lemon and the fresh coriander. Serve with naan or rice."]},
"indian|Dal Makhani": {"time":"About 1 hour 45 minutes, plus soaking","steps":["Rinse the whole black gram and the kidney beans together and soak them in plenty of water overnight. In the morning they should split easily under a fingernail. Drain them.","Cover the soaked dal and beans with fresh water to sit 4 cm above them. Bring to a boil, skim the foam, then simmer until both are completely soft and the black gram starts to break, about 45 to 60 minutes. Mash about a third of them against the side of the pot so the gravy turns creamy but the rest of the beans stay whole.","Slice the onion. Melt half the butter and cook the onion slowly until it is deep gold, not merely soft. Stir in the ginger paste and garlic paste and cook until the raw smell goes, about 2 minutes.","Add the chopped tomato and Kashmiri chilli powder. Cook, stirring, until the tomato collapses and the butter shows at the edge, about 8 minutes. This is the base; if it is watery the dal will taste thin.","Tip the cooked dal and beans, with their cooking liquid, into the tomato base. Add the salt. Simmer uncovered on low for 25 minutes, stirring so the bottom does not catch. The dal should thicken until a spoon dragged through it leaves a trail.","Stir in the cream and the rest of the butter. Crush the kasuri methi between your palms and add it with the garam masala off a hard boil. Rest 10 minutes. Dal makhani should be dark, glossy and mild, with whole beans in a sauce you can stand a spoon in."]},
"indian|Dosa": {"time":"About 30 minutes, plus overnight fermenting","steps":["Wash the idli rice and urad dal separately. Soak the rice for 5 hours and the dal with the fenugreek seeds for 4 hours.","Grind the dal to a fluffy batter, then the rice to a slightly grainy one. Mix them. The batter should be thick enough to coat a spoon.","Cover and leave in a warm place overnight, until it has risen and smells faintly sour. That sourness is the dosa.","For the filling, boil and crush the potato. Heat a dry pan, pop the mustard seeds, add the curry leaves, then the potato. Mash it into a dry spiced filling.","Heat a tawa until a drop of water dances. Pour a ladle of batter in the centre and spiral it thin with the back of the ladle.","When the underside is golden and the top looks dry, lay potato along the centre and fold. Serve at once."]},
"indian|Gulab Jamun": {"time":"About 1 hour","steps":["Warm the sugar with 400 ml of water and the cardamom pods until the sugar dissolves. Simmer 5 minutes to a light syrup that coats a spoon. Crumble in the saffron and add the rose water. Keep the syrup warm. A cold syrup pulls the surface of the jamun shut.","Rub the ghee into the milk powder and flour until it feels like damp sand. Sprinkle on the milk a spoon at a time and bring it into a soft dough. Do not knead it hard or the jamuns turn dense. Rest 10 minutes, covered.","Oil your palms and roll the dough into smooth balls about the size of a large marble. There must be no crack. A crack splits in the syrup and the ball falls apart.","Heat the frying oil on medium, not smoking. A scrap of dough should rise slowly, not instantly. Fry the balls a few at a time, turning constantly, until they are an even deep gold, about 5 to 7 minutes. Pale jamuns are raw in the middle; dark ones taste bitter.","Lift them out and drop them straight into the warm syrup. They should sink, then swell. If they float and stay small, the syrup is too thick: loosen it with a spoon of hot water.","Soak at least 1 hour, turning once, until they are spongy all the way through. Serve warm or at room temperature, with a little of the syrup. Gulab jamun should yield to a spoon, not bounce."]},
"indian|Naan": {"time":"About 2 hours","steps":["Stir the yeast into a little warm water with a pinch of the flour and wait 10 minutes until it foams.","Mix the rest of the flour with the yogurt and the yeast mixture. Knead 8 minutes to a soft dough. It should be slightly sticky.","Cover and leave in a warm place until doubled, about 1 hour.","Divide into balls. Roll each into a teardrop, thicker in the middle.","Cook on a very hot tawa or under a grill. When bubbles rise, flip it. Brush the hot bread with butter and scatter chopped garlic and cilantro.","Stack in a cloth and serve at once. Naan stiffens as it cools."]},
"indian|Palak Paneer": {"time":"About 40 minutes","steps":["Wash the spinach and wilt it in a covered pan with the water that clings to the leaves, about 3 minutes. Cool, then blend to a smooth puree. A few stems left in keep it from tasting flat.","Cut the paneer into cubes. If it is very fresh and soft, leave it as it is; if it is firm, soak the cubes in warm water for 10 minutes.","Chop the onion. Cook it in a dry pan with a splash of water until soft and sweet. Add the ginger-garlic paste and cook until the raw smell goes.","Add the chopped tomato. Cook until it breaks down. Stir in the spinach puree and simmer 5 minutes. The sauce should be thick, not watery.","Fold in the paneer and the cream. Simmer 3 minutes so the paneer warms but does not turn rubbery. Rest 2 minutes and serve with roti or rice."]},
"indian|Rogan Josh": {"time":"About 1 hour 45 minutes","steps":["Cut the lamb shoulder into large pieces, fat left on. Rogan josh is a red oil gravy; lean meat will taste dry.","Heat the mustard oil until it smokes, then cool it slightly. Brown the lamb in batches and set it aside.","In the same oil fry the sliced onion slowly until brown. Add the Kashmiri chilli, fennel powder and the seeds from the cardamom. Fry 1 minute with a splash of water so the chilli does not scorch.","Return the lamb. Stir in the yogurt a spoon at a time so it does not split. Add enough hot water to come halfway up the meat.","Cover and simmer gently until the lamb is tender, about 1 hour to 1 hour 15 minutes. Uncover at the end and reduce until a red oil floats on a thick gravy. That oil is the rogan.","Rest 10 minutes. Serve with rice."]},
"indian|Samosa": {"time":"About 1 hour 20 minutes","steps":["Rub two spoons of the frying oil into the flour. Add cold water and knead a firm dough. Rest it, covered, for 20 minutes.","Boil the potatoes until just tender, peel and crush them into chunks, not mash. Heat a spoon of oil, add the cumin seeds until they pop, then the chopped green chilli, peas and garam masala. Stir in the potato. Cool the filling.","Divide the dough. Roll ovals, cut each in half, and form a cone. Wet the edge, fill with potato, and pinch the seam shut. A leak will explode in the oil.","Heat the oil for frying to medium — a piece of dough should bubble steadily, not violently. Fry the samosas a few at a time, turning, until deep gold and crisp, about 8 minutes. Pale samosas are raw inside; rushed ones blister.","Drain on paper. Serve hot with chutney."]},
"indian|Tikka Masala": {"time":"About 50 minutes","steps":["Cut the chicken breast into large chunks. Stir it through the yogurt and leave it 20 minutes. This list is a simple home version: yogurt, tomato sauce and cream do the work.","Heat a wide pan. Sear the chicken in batches until the edges colour. The yogurt will brown in spots; that is the flavour. Do not cook it through yet.","Pour in the tomato sauce and scrape up the browned bits. Simmer 10 minutes until the sauce thickens and turns a deeper red.","Stir in the heavy cream and return all the chicken. Simmer gently, not at a hard boil, for 10 minutes until the chicken is just cooked and the sauce is velvety.","Rest 5 minutes so the sauce clings. Serve with rice. If you want it hotter, that heat has to come from what is already in the pot — do not add spices that are not on this list."]},
"indonesian|Bakso": {"time":"About 1 hour","steps":["Simmer the beef bones in water with half the garlic for 45 minutes. Skim. This clear broth is the soup. Keep it hot.","Beat the minced beef with the egg whites, tapioca flour, the rest of the garlic, salt and pepper until the paste is sticky and smooth. A coarse paste makes a crumbly ball. Slap it against the bowl for 2 minutes.","With wet hands, or a spoon dipped in water, shape small balls. Drop them into simmering water. They are done when they float and feel springy, about 5 minutes.","Cook the noodles and divide them between bowls.","Add the bakso and ladle over the boiling bone broth. Finish with scallion and a pinch of the pepper.","The balls should bounce back when pressed and taste of beef, not flour. The soup stays clear."]},
"indonesian|Gado-Gado": {"time":"About 45 minutes","steps":["Boil the potato until tender. Blanch the cabbage, bean sprouts and long beans separately so each stays bright. Drain well.","Warm or fry the tofu and tempeh until the surfaces colour.","Halve the hard-boiled egg.","Warm the peanut sauce with a splash of water until it is thick enough to coat a spoon.","Arrange the vegetables, tofu, tempeh and egg on a plate and spoon the peanut sauce over, or serve the sauce in a bowl to pour at the table. Everything should still have its own texture under the sauce."]},
"indonesian|Gudeg": {"time":"About 2 hours","steps":["If the jackfruit is canned in brine, rinse it well and drain. Tear it into chunks along the grain. Hard boiled the eggs and peel them.","Pound the shallot, garlic and galangal. Put the jackfruit in a heavy pot with the paste, coconut milk, palm sugar, bay leaves, salt and the teak leaves if you have them. Bay leaves alone will not turn it brown; the long cook and palm sugar do most of that colour.","Bring to a simmer, then cook on the lowest heat, covered, for 1 hour 30 minutes, stirring now and then. The jackfruit should drink the coconut and turn soft and dark.","Add the peeled eggs for the last 20 minutes so they stain.","Uncover and cook until the sauce is thick and almost dry, clinging to the jackfruit. Gudeg is not a soup.","Rest 15 minutes. It should be sweet, coconut-rich and savoury, the jackfruit tender enough to cut with a spoon."]},
"indonesian|Mie Goreng": {"time":"About 25 minutes","steps":["Cook the egg noodles until just tender, rinse, and drain. Slice the chicken thin and shred the cabbage. Chop the chilli.","Heat the oil and fry the garlic and chilli for 20 seconds. Add the chicken and stir-fry until just cooked.","Add the cabbage and toss until it wilts but stays bright.","Add the noodles, sweet soy sauce and salt. Toss until the noodles are dark and glossy and a few strands char.","Push them aside, scramble the eggs, and fold them through with the scallion.","Mie goreng should taste sweet-salty from the sweet soy, with heat from the chilli, and be dry enough to eat with a fork."]},
"indonesian|Nasi Goreng": {"time":"About 20 minutes","steps":["Use cold day-old rice. Break up the clumps.","Pound the shallot, garlic and shrimp paste together, or fry them together until the terasi smells toasted, not raw. Terasi is strong; a little is enough.","Push them aside and fry the egg, then chop it, or fry an egg separately to lay on top.","Add the rice and the sweet soy sauce. Toss on high heat until every grain is coated and a little crisp at the edges.","Serve with the prawn crackers and the fried egg. The kecap manis is the seasoning; it should taste sweet-salty and smoky."]},
"indonesian|Pempek": {"time":"About 1 hour","steps":["Chop the fish very fine, or pulse it, with the garlic and salt. It should be a paste, not chunks. Work in the tapioca flour and enough of the water to make a soft, smooth dough that holds a shape.","For the cuko sauce, simmer the palm sugar, tamarind, chopped chilli and the rest of the water until the sugar dissolves and the sauce is sour-sweet and thin. Keep it warm.","Shape the dough into short sausages or rounds. A wet knife helps.","Boil the pieces in simmering water until they float and feel firm, about 8 minutes. Drain and cool.","Heat the oil and fry the boiled pempek until the outside is gold and blistered, about 3 minutes. Boiling first sets the inside; frying only colours it.","Serve hot with the cuko poured over. Pempek is chewy, the sauce dark, sour and sweet. The centre should be soft, not raw and white."]},
"indonesian|Rawon": {"time":"About 1 hour 40 minutes","steps":["Crack the keluak and scoop out the black paste inside. If they are very hard, soak the nuts in warm water for 20 minutes first. Keluak is what turns rawon black and gives it the earthy taste. Do not leave it out.","Pound the keluak paste with the shallot, garlic, galangal, lemongrass and turmeric.","Brown the beef in a heavy pot. Add the paste and fry 4 minutes until fragrant. Cover with water, add the salt, and simmer until the beef is tender, about 1 hour 15 minutes.","The broth should be very dark, almost black, and clear enough to taste the beef under the keluak.","Slice or leave the beef in large pieces. Taste the salt.","Serve with bean sprouts and lime. Rawon is a black beef soup, savoury and a little bitter, not a red curry."]},
"indonesian|Rendang": {"time":"About 3 hours","steps":["Pound the lemongrass, galangal, shallot, red chilli, ginger and candlenut to a paste. Candlenut makes the sauce thicken and turn glossy; do not leave it out.","Fry the paste until fragrant and the coconut fat separates.","Add the beef and coat it. Pour in the coconut milk, the torn kaffir lime leaves.","Cook uncovered at a gentle bubble, stirring often, until the liquid is gone and the beef is frying in a dark paste, 2 to 3 hours.","The meat should be tender and the colour deep brown. Rest before serving with rice."]},
"indonesian|Sate Ayam": {"time":"About 35 minutes, plus marinating","steps":["Cut the chicken into small cubes. Mix half the sweet soy with the crushed garlic, the juice of half the lime and the salt. Coat the chicken and rest 1 hour.","Thread onto skewers, pieces touching.","Warm the peanut butter with the rest of the sweet soy, the palm sugar, chopped chilli and 80 ml of water. Simmer until thick. Finish with the rest of the lime.","Grill or pan-fry the skewers, brushing with the oil, until charred at the edges and cooked through, about 8 minutes.","Brush a little of the peanut sauce on in the last minute so it lacquers, and serve the rest beside.","The chicken should be sweet at the crust and still juicy. Do not boil it in the sauce."]},
"indonesian|Soto Ayam": {"time":"About 1 hour","steps":["Pound the shallot, garlic, galangal, turmeric and lemongrass to a coarse paste. This spice paste is the colour and smell of soto.","Brown the paste in a dry pot for 3 minutes, then add the chicken and salt. Cover with water by 4 cm. Simmer until the chicken is tender, about 35 minutes. Lift it out and shred the meat. Keep the broth and skim the fat.","Cook the rice and keep it warm.","Shred the cabbage. The broth should be golden and clear enough to see the bottom of a spoonful. If it is cloudy, you boiled it too hard.","Put rice in each bowl, then chicken and cabbage. Ladle over the boiling broth.","Finish with scallion and lime. Soto ayam is a light chicken soup, turmeric-yellow, eaten with the rice in the bowl."]},
"italian|Cacio e Pepe": {"time":"About 25 minutes","steps":["Toast the black pepper in a dry pan until it smells fragrant. Crack it coarse. This toasting is the difference between peppery pasta and raw heat.","Boil the spaghetti in water salted with the salt until it is a minute from done. Save two cups of the water before you drain.","Grate the pecorino very fine. Mix it with a ladle of the hot pasta water off the heat until you have a smooth cream. If you boil the cheese it turns grainy strings.","Put the drained pasta in the pepper pan with a splash of pasta water. Toss.","Take the pan off the heat and add the pecorino cream, tossing fast, adding pasta water until the sauce clings to every strand and looks glossy.","Serve at once with more pepper. There is no other ingredient. The sauce should be creamy from the cheese and the starch of the water, not from cream."]},
"italian|Gelato": {"time":"About 40 minutes, plus freezing","steps":["Split the vanilla bean and scrape the seeds. Heat the milk, cream, half the sugar and the vanilla pod and seeds until steaming.","Whisk the yolks with the rest of the sugar until pale. Pour in the hot milk slowly, whisking, so the yolks do not scramble.","Return the custard to the pan and cook on low, stirring, until it coats the back of a spoon, about 8 minutes. Do not boil it.","Strain, discarding the pod. Chill the custard completely. Warm gelato base makes ice crystals.","Churn in an ice-cream maker until thick, or freeze in a tray, stirring every 30 minutes, until set but still soft.","Gelato should be dense and silky, not icy, and taste of vanilla and milk rather than of air. Serve slightly soft."]},
"italian|Lasagna": {"time":"About 2 hours","steps":["Start with the ground beef and ground pork in a wide pan until browned. Pour off excess fat. Add the red wine and let it reduce. Add the tomato sauce and simmer 30 minutes.","Warm the béchamel. If it is very thick, loosen it with a spoon of milk. Season it.","If the lasagna sheets are dried, boil them until flexible. Fresh sheets can go in as they are if the sauce is loose.","In a baking dish, spread a thin layer of meat sauce, then sheets, béchamel, mozzarella and parmesan. Repeat, finishing with béchamel and parmesan.","Bake at 180°C for about 40 minutes, until bubbling and brown on top. Rest 15 minutes before cutting so the slices hold."]},
"italian|Minestrone": {"time":"About 50 minutes","steps":["Dice the onion, carrot, celery and zucchini small. Heat the olive oil and cook the onion, carrot and celery until soft, about 8 minutes. Add the garlic for 1 minute.","Add the tomato and salt and cook until it softens. Pour in water to make a soup, about 1 litre.","Add the cannellini beans and zucchini. Simmer 15 minutes.","Add the pasta and cook until it is just tender. The soup will thicken as the pasta releases starch. Add water if it becomes a stew.","Taste the salt. Stir in the basil off the heat.","Serve with grated parmesan. Minestrone should be thick with vegetables and beans, the pasta cooked in the soup, not boiled apart and dumped in."]},
"italian|Osso Buco": {"time":"About 2 hours","steps":["Tie each veal shank so the meat stays on the bone. Salt them. Brown them in the olive oil until deep gold on both sides. Lift them out. The marrow bone is the point of the dish; do not lose it.","Dice the onion, carrot and celery. Cook them in the same pan until soft, about 8 minutes.","Add the wine and let it reduce by half. Add the chopped tomato and the stock.","Return the shanks. The liquid should come halfway up. Cover and simmer very gently for 1 hour 30 minutes, until the meat is falling from the bone. Turn them once.","Chop the parsley, garlic and the zest of the lemon together. This gremolata goes on at the end, raw.","Taste the sauce and reduce it if it is thin. Spoon it over the shanks and scatter the gremolata. The marrow should be soft enough to eat with a spoon."]},
"italian|Pasta Carbonara": {"time":"About 25 minutes","steps":["Put a pot of water on to boil.","Cut the guanciale into strips. Cook it in a cold pan over medium heat until the fat renders and the pieces are crisp. Turn off the heat. There must be no other fat.","Beat the egg yolks with the grated pecorino and a lot of black pepper. No cream.","Boil the spaghetti until just shy of done. Lift it into the guanciale pan with a splash of the pasta water. Toss.","Take the pan off the heat. Stir in the egg and cheese, adding pasta water until you have a glossy sauce that coats every strand. The heat of the pasta cooks the egg; a hot burner scrambles it.","Serve at once with more pecorino and pepper."]},
"italian|Pesto Pasta": {"time":"About 25 minutes","steps":["Toast the pine nuts in a dry pan until pale gold. They burn suddenly.","Pound or blend the basil, pine nuts, garlic and a pinch of the salt with the olive oil to a coarse, bright green paste. Do not over-blend or the basil turns dark and bitter. Stir in the grated parmesan by hand.","Boil the trofie in salted water until just tender. Save a cup of the water.","Toss the drained pasta with the pesto off the heat, adding pasta water until the sauce coats every piece and looks creamy, not oily and split.","Taste the salt. Pesto should taste of basil first, then pine nut and cheese.","Serve at once. Do not reheat pesto on a burner or the basil blackens."]},
"italian|Pizza Margherita": {"time":"About 1 hour 30 minutes, plus rising","steps":["Dissolve the yeast in 250 ml of warm water. Mix with the flour and salt and knead 10 minutes until the dough is smooth and springs back. Rise, covered, until doubled, about 1 hour.","Crush the tomato with a pinch of the salt and a spoon of the olive oil. Do not cook it. Margherita sauce is raw tomato, so the tomatoes must be ripe and sweet.","Heat the oven as high as it will go, with a tray inside. Divide the dough in two and stretch each into a thin round, leaving a rim. Do not use a rolling pin if you can stretch it; a pin knocks out the air.","Spread a thin layer of tomato, leaving the rim bare. Tear the mozzarella over it. A thick layer of cheese makes a wet pizza.","Bake until the rim is blistered and spotted and the cheese has melted, about 8 to 10 minutes. Drizzle the rest of the oil and tear on the basil the moment it comes out.","The centre should be tender, the rim chewy, the basil still bright. Eat at once."]},
"italian|Risotto": {"time":"About 40 minutes","steps":["Heat the chicken stock and keep it simmering.","Melt half the butter and cook the finely chopped onion until soft but not brown.","Add the arborio rice and stir until the edges of the grains turn translucent, about 2 minutes.","Pour in the white wine and stir until it has evaporated.","Add the hot stock a ladle at a time, stirring, letting each ladle be absorbed before the next. Keep going for about 18 minutes, until the rice is tender with a slight bite and the sauce is loose enough to ripple.","Take it off the heat. Beat in the rest of the butter, the parmesan and the saffron bloomed in a spoon of stock. Rest 2 minutes under a lid. Serve at once."]},
"italian|Tiramisu": {"time":"About 30 minutes, plus at least 6 hours chilling","steps":["Beat the egg yolks with the sugar until thick and pale.","Beat in the mascarpone until smooth.","Mix the espresso with the marsala and let it cool.","Dip the ladyfingers in the espresso for a second on each side. A long soak makes them collapse. Lay them in a dish.","Spread half the mascarpone over them. Repeat with another layer of dipped biscuits and the rest of the cream.","Dust with the cocoa powder. Chill at least 6 hours, overnight is better, so the biscuits soften into the cream. Dust again before serving."]},
"jamaican|Ackee and Saltfish": {"time":"About 30 minutes","steps":["Soak the salt cod in several changes of water, or boil it twice, until it is salty but edible. Flake it and pick out bones. If you skip this, the dish is inedibly salty.","If the ackee is canned, drain it gently. Fresh ackee must be fully ripe and boiled until tender. Do not stir it hard or it turns to mush.","Slice the onion and tomato. Heat the oil and cook the onion, thyme and a little of the scotch bonnet until the onion is soft. Leave the rest of the pepper aside if you want less heat.","Add the tomato and the saltfish. Fry 3 minutes.","Fold in the ackee and warm it through, about 2 minutes. Season with pepper. Taste before adding any salt.","Finish with scallion. Ackee and saltfish should look like scrambled eggs, the ackee in soft curds, the fish savoury, the pepper a warmth not a burn."]},
"jamaican|Bammy": {"time":"About 30 minutes","steps":["Peel and grate the cassava. Squeeze it hard in a cloth. The juice is bitter and watery; the dry meal is what sets. Salt the meal.","Press the meal into thin rounds.","Cook the rounds in a dry pan on medium until both sides are set and pale gold, about 4 minutes a side. They can be stored at this stage.","Soak each round in coconut milk for 2 minutes so it softens.","Fry in the oil until the outside is crisp and the inside is tender, about 2 minutes a side.","Bammy should taste of cassava and coconut, crisp at the edge, not raw and bitter. Eat hot."]},
"jamaican|Beef Patty": {"time":"About 1 hour","steps":["Rub the butter into the flour with the turmeric and a pinch of salt. Add cold water to make a dough. The turmeric turns the pastry yellow. Chill 30 minutes.","Fry the onion, then the beef, thyme, a little chopped scotch bonnet, curry powder and the rest of the salt until the meat is brown and dry. Stir in the breadcrumbs so the filling holds. Cool it.","Roll the dough and cut rounds. Fill one half, fold, and crimp the edge with a fork.","Brush with beaten egg. Bake at 190°C until the pastry is gold and crisp, about 25 minutes.","Rest 5 minutes. The crust should flake, the filling juicy and spiced, not wet enough to sog the base.","Eat warm."]},
"jamaican|Callaloo": {"time":"About 25 minutes","steps":["Soak the salt cod briefly and flake it. Wash the callaloo and strip any tough stems. Chop the leaves.","Heat the oil and soften the onion, garlic, thyme and a little scotch bonnet.","Add the tomato and saltfish and cook 2 minutes.","Add the callaloo with a splash of water. Cover and cook until the leaves collapse and are tender, about 8 minutes.","Uncover and cook off extra water. Taste before salting; the cod is salty.","Callaloo should be soft and savoury, a greens stew, not a raw salad and not a watery soup."]},
"jamaican|Curry Goat": {"time":"About 1 hour 45 minutes","steps":["Cut the goat into pieces. Mix it with the curry powder, crushed garlic and ginger, salt and a spoon of the oil. Rest 30 minutes.","Heat the rest of the oil and fry the curry-coated goat until the spices smell toasted and the meat browns, about 8 minutes. Burning the curry powder makes it bitter, so add a spoon of water if it darkens too fast.","Add the sliced onion, thyme and the whole scotch bonnet. Fry 3 minutes.","Add water to barely cover. Simmer until the goat is tender, about 1 hour 15 minutes. Add the potato in chunks for the last 20 minutes.","The gravy should be thick and yellow-brown, clinging to the meat. Fish out the whole pepper.","Rest 10 minutes. Curry goat is hot, aromatic, and better when the meat has given in."]},
"jamaican|Escovitch Fish": {"time":"About 30 minutes","steps":["Score the snapper and salt it. Pat it dry.","Heat the oil and fry the fish until the skin is crisp and the flesh is just cooked, about 4 minutes a side. Drain it onto a platter.","Slice the onion and carrot thin. In a spoon of the frying oil, cook them with the thyme, allspice and scotch bonnet until the onion softens but the carrot still has crunch, about 3 minutes.","Add the vinegar and sugar and simmer 2 minutes. The pickle should be sharp and lightly sweet.","Pour the hot vinegar vegetables over the fried fish. The heat of the pickle meets the crisp fish.","Rest 10 minutes, or serve the next day cold. Escovitch is fried fish under a sharp pickled dressing."]},
"jamaican|Festival": {"time":"About 35 minutes","steps":["Mix the flour, cornmeal, sugar, baking powder and salt. Rub in the butter.","Add water a spoon at a time until you have a soft dough that is not sticky. Knead briefly.","Roll finger-length dumplings, slightly tapered.","Heat the oil to medium. Fry until deep gold and cooked through, about 4 minutes, turning. A raw centre means the oil was too hot and coloured the outside first.","Drain.","Festival should be lightly sweet, crisp outside and soft inside, eaten hot beside fried fish."]},
"jamaican|Jerk Chicken": {"time":"About 1 hour, plus marinating","steps":["Roughly chop the scotch bonnet, allspice, thyme, green onion, garlic and ginger. Blend or pound them with the soy sauce and brown sugar into a thick paste. Taste a dab; the heat should be serious.","Cut slashes in the chicken legs and rub the paste in, including under the skin. Marinate at least 4 hours, overnight if you can.","Grill over moderate coals, or roast at 200°C, turning, until the skin is dark and lacquered and the juices at the bone run clear. Scotch bonnet burns, so keep the heat from flaring onto the paste.","Rest 10 minutes.","Serve with rice or festival. The allspice and thyme should be as clear as the chilli."]},
"jamaican|Oxtail": {"time":"About 2 hours 30 minutes","steps":["Season the oxtail with salt. Brown it in the oil. Browning the outside gives the gravy its colour.","Add the onion, garlic, thyme and whole scotch bonnet. Fry 3 minutes. Add the tomato and soy sauce.","Cover with water. Simmer until the meat is falling off the bone, about 2 hours. Skim the fat now and then.","Add the butter beans for the last 30 minutes so they are tender but not dissolved.","The gravy should be thick and glossy. Remove the whole pepper. Taste the salt; the soy may be enough.","Rest 15 minutes. Oxtail stew is rich, the meat gelatinous, the beans whole."]},
"jamaican|Rice and Peas": {"time":"About 50 minutes","steps":["Soak the kidney beans overnight and boil them until just tender. Keep the cooking liquid.","Put the beans in a pot with the coconut milk, enough bean liquid to cook the rice, the scallion, thyme, whole scotch bonnet, garlic, allspice and salt. The pepper stays whole so it perfumes without bursting heat through every grain.","Add the rinsed rice. The liquid should sit about 2 cm above the rice.","Bring to a boil, then cover and cook on low until the rice is tender and the liquid is gone, about 20 minutes.","Rest 10 minutes. Remove the whole pepper and thyme stems. Fluff.","The rice should be separate, coconut-rich, with whole beans. Do not stir the pepper so it splits."]},
"japanese|Gyoza": {"time":"About 1 hour","steps":["Chop the cabbage fine, salt it, and leave it 10 minutes. Squeeze it dry in a cloth. Wet cabbage makes a gyoza that bursts.","Mix the pork with the cabbage, grated garlic and ginger, chopped scallion, soy sauce and sesame oil. Stir until the filling is sticky and holds a ball.","Put a spoon of filling in each wrapper. Wet the rim, fold into a half-moon, and pleat the top edge so the bottom stays flat. The flat bottom is what fries.","Heat a non-stick pan with half the oil. Set the gyoza flat side down, close but not touching. Fry until the bottoms are deep gold, about 3 minutes.","Pour in 60 ml of water, cover at once, and steam until the water is gone and the wrappers look translucent, about 4 minutes. Uncover and let the bottoms crisp again for 1 minute.","Turn them out browned side up. The pleats should be tender and the base shatter-crisp, the pork cooked through."]},
"japanese|Miso Soup": {"time":"About 20 minutes","steps":["Soak the wakame in cold water for 5 minutes until it opens, then drain and cut away any hard rib.","Bring the dashi just to a simmer. Do not let it boil hard or the soup tastes flat and the miso turns grainy later.","Cut the silken tofu into small cubes and slide them in with the wakame. Warm them for 2 minutes. They should not be stirred hard or the tofu breaks.","Ladle a cup of the hot dashi into a bowl and whisk in the miso until it dissolves. Pour that back into the pot. Never dump miso straight into a rolling boil.","Take the pot off the heat at once. Slice the scallion thin and add it.","Serve immediately in small bowls. Miso soup should taste of the sea from the dashi, with the miso round and not salty-harsh, and the tofu still in soft cubes."]},
"japanese|Okonomiyaki": {"time":"About 40 minutes","steps":["Shred the cabbage fine. Mix the flour with the egg, dashi stock to a thick batter.","Fold the cabbage through the batter. It should look like more cabbage than batter.","Heat a lightly oiled griddle. Spread a round of batter. Lay strips of pork belly on top. Cook until the underside is golden and the top looks set, about 5 minutes.","Flip it. Cook until the pork is done and the pancake is cooked through, another 5 minutes. Press gently.","Brush with okonomiyaki sauce and cover with a drift of bonito flakes. They should curl and move in the heat. Eat with a spatula from the griddle."]},
"japanese|Ramen": {"time":"About 6 hours for the broth","steps":["Blanch the pork bones and chicken carcass, rinse them, and cover with fresh water. Simmer, never a hard boil, for 4 to 6 hours, skimming at the start. The broth should turn milky if you let it bubble gently near the end.","Mix soy sauce and mirin for a tare. In each bowl, put a spoon of tare, then the hot broth.","Roll and tie the pork belly. Braise it in a little of the soy and mirin with water until a skewer slides in, about 2 hours. Cool in the liquid, then slice.","Boil the eggs for 7 minutes, chill them, peel, and soak in the pork braising liquid if you have enough.","Cook the ramen noodles until just tender. Put them in the bowls, then the chashu, halved egg, green onion, nori and menma. Serve at once; noodles overcook in the broth."]},
"japanese|Sukiyaki": {"time":"About 40 minutes","steps":["Slice the beef paper-thin, across the grain, while it is still cold. Cut the tofu into cubes, the napa into wide pieces, the negi on a diagonal, and the shiitake in half. Rinse the shirataki and boil them 1 minute, then drain. They smell of the packet until you do this.","Arrange everything on a platter, raw. Sukiyaki is cooked at the table, but one heavy pan on the stove works the same way.","Mix the soy sauce, mirin and sugar with 100 ml of water. This is the warishita. Taste it: sweet first, then salty.","Heat a wide pan. Lay in a few slices of beef and pour over a little of the warishita. When the beef just changes colour, push it aside and add tofu, napa, mushroom, negi and noodles. Add more sauce as the pan dries.","Cook only until the vegetables soften and the beef is still tender. Crowding and long boiling makes the beef grey.","Beat the eggs in small bowls, one each. Dip the hot beef and tofu into the raw egg as you eat. The egg cooks on the food. Keep the pan simmering and add the rest of the platter as you go."]},
"japanese|Sushi": {"time":"About 1 hour, plus cooling the rice","steps":["Rinse the short-grain sushi rice until the water is almost clear. Cook it with a little less water than for plain rice. When it is done, rest it covered for 10 minutes.","Fold the rice vinegar through the hot rice with a cutting motion. Do not mash. Fan it as you fold so it shines and cools. Seasoned rice that is still hot will cook the fish.","Slice the salmon and tuna sashimi against the grain, in strokes, not sawing. Keep it cold.","For nigiri, wet your hands, take a small oval of rice, and press a slice of fish on with a dab of wasabi.","For rolls, lay nori shiny side down, spread rice thinly, add cucumber, avocado and fish, and roll tight in a mat. Cut with a wet knife.","Serve with soy sauce, more wasabi and pickled ginger. The ginger is between pieces, to clear the palate, not piled on the fish."]},
"japanese|Tempura": {"time":"About 40 minutes","steps":["Peel the sweet potato and slice it. Cut the eggplant. Pat the shrimp dry and nick the belly so they do not curl.","Heat the oil for frying to about 180°C. It must be hot before the batter is mixed. Batter that waits turns gluey.","Make the batter at the last moment: beat the egg with ice-cold water, then stir in the cake flour in a few strokes. Lumps are correct. A smooth batter makes heavy tempura.","Dip shrimp and vegetables, let the excess drip, and fry until the crust is pale gold and crisp, not brown. Do not crowd the pot.","Drain on a rack. Serve at once with the tentsuyu. Tempura is a few minutes old at most."]},
"japanese|Teriyaki": {"time":"About 25 minutes","steps":["Pat the chicken thigh dry. Sear it skin-side down in a pan until the skin is golden and the fat has rendered. Turn and cook until nearly done.","Mix the soy sauce, mirin, sake and sugar. Pour it over the chicken.","Simmer, turning the chicken, until the sauce reduces to a thick glaze that coats the meat. If it threatens to burn, add a spoon of water.","Rest 3 minutes. Slice.","Spoon the glaze over the slices and scatter the sesame seeds. Serve with rice."]},
"japanese|Tonkatsu": {"time":"About 35 minutes","steps":["Nick the fat around the pork loin cutlets so they stay flat. Pound them lightly to an even thickness.","Set out the flour, the beaten egg, and the panko in a line. Dust with flour, dip in egg, then press into panko. Let them sit 5 minutes so the crust sticks.","Heat the oil for frying to medium. Fry until the crust is deep gold and the pork is just cooked through, about 5 minutes, turning once. Thick cutlets need a minute of rest in the oil off the heat to finish.","Drain, rest 2 minutes, and slice. Serve on the shredded cabbage with tonkatsu sauce. Squeeze the lemon over the pork, not the sauce."]},
"japanese|Yakitori": {"time":"About 30 minutes","steps":["Cut the chicken thigh into bite-sized pieces and the negi into similar lengths. Thread them alternately on the bamboo skewers. Soak the skewers first if you are grilling over flame, so they do not burn.","Simmer the soy sauce, mirin and sake until slightly syrupy. This is the tare.","Grill the skewers over high heat, turning, until the chicken is just cooked and starting to char.","Brush with tare in the last minute, then again as they come off the grill. A last brush on the hot meat glazes without burning.","Serve at once. The negi should be soft and sweet, the chicken still juicy."]},
"kenyan|Chapati": {"time":"About 40 minutes","steps":["Mix the flour and salt with water to a soft dough. Knead 8 minutes until smooth. Rest 20 minutes.","Divide into 8 balls. Roll each into a thin round, brush with oil, roll it into a rope, then coil the rope into a disc. Rest 10 minutes.","Roll each coil into a round about 15 cm across. The coil is what makes the flaky layers.","Cook on a hot pan, brushing with oil, until both sides are blistered and cooked through, about 2 minutes a side.","Press gently with a cloth so they puff where they can.","Stack them so they steam and stay soft. Kenyan chapati should be flaky, soft and lightly rich with oil."]},
"kenyan|Githeri": {"time":"About 50 minutes","steps":["Soak the maize and beans overnight. Boil them together until both are tender, about 40 minutes. The maize stays a little chewy; that is right.","Dice the potato and boil it until just tender. Drain.","Heat the oil and cook the sliced onion until soft. Add the tomato and salt and cook until the tomato breaks down.","Add the maize, beans and potato with a splash of the cooking water. Simmer 10 minutes so the stew thickens and the flavours meet.","Stir in the coriander.","Githeri should be a thick maize and bean stew, the potato soft, the tomato savoury, not a soup."]},
"kenyan|Mahamri": {"time":"About 40 minutes, plus rising","steps":["Mix the flour, sugar, yeast, cardamom and salt with the coconut milk to a soft dough. Knead 6 minutes.","Rise until doubled, about 1 hour.","Divide into 12, roll into balls, and flatten to about 1 cm.","Fry in medium oil until both sides are deep gold and the centre is cooked, about 3 minutes.","Drain. They should be puffed, cardamom-scented and tender.","Mahamri is the Swahili coconut doughnut, eaten with tea, best the day it is fried."]},
"kenyan|Mandazi": {"time":"About 40 minutes, plus rising","steps":["Mix the flour, sugar, yeast, cardamom and salt. Add the coconut milk and a splash of warm water if needed to make a soft dough. Knead 6 minutes.","Rise until doubled, about 45 minutes.","Roll to 1 cm and cut triangles.","Heat the oil to medium. Fry until both sides are gold and the centre is cooked, about 3 minutes. If they brown before they cook through, the oil is too hot.","Drain.","Mandazi should be lightly sweet, cardamom-scented and soft, not heavy with oil. Eat the same day."]},
"kenyan|Mukimo": {"time":"About 35 minutes","steps":["Boil the potato, peas and maize until the potato is very soft. If the maize is dried, boil it first until tender.","Chop the pumpkin leaves and the onion and add them for the last 5 minutes so the leaves wilt.","Drain, keeping a spoon of the water. Mash everything together with the butter and salt. It should be coarse, the maize and peas still visible, not a smooth puree.","If it is dry, mash in a splash of the cooking water.","Taste the salt.","Mukimo is a green-flecked mash, mild and filling, eaten hot beside a stew."]},
"kenyan|Nyama Choma": {"time":"About 1 hour 30 minutes","steps":["Cut the goat meat into large pieces, or leave chops whole. Rub with a little of the lemon.","Grill slowly over coals, not a fierce flame, turning often, until the outside is deeply browned and the inside is cooked through but still juicy. Goat punishes a rushed high heat by going tough.","Rest 10 minutes. Chop it, bones and all, the way it is served.","Chop the kachumbari: tomato and onion, with the rest of the lemon.","Serve the meat with the salsa and ugali. The lemon cuts the char."]},
"kenyan|Pilau": {"time":"About 50 minutes","steps":["Rinse the rice until the water runs clearer and soak it 20 minutes.","Cut the beef into cubes. Heat the oil and brown it. Add the sliced onion and cook until deep gold, about 10 minutes. The browned onion is the colour of the pilau.","Add the garlic, ginger, pilau masala and tomato. Fry until the spices smell toasted and the tomato thickens.","Add the drained rice and salt and stir so every grain is coated. Add 550 ml of water. Bring to a boil, then cover on the lowest heat for 18 minutes.","Do not lift the lid. Rest 10 minutes and fluff.","The rice should be separate, the beef tender, the whole pot fragrant with pilau spice and sweet onion."]},
"kenyan|Sukuma Wiki": {"time":"About 20 minutes","steps":["Strip the collard greens off the tough stems and slice the leaves thin.","Heat the oil. Fry the chopped onion until soft.","Add the chopped tomato and cook until the tomato breaks down into a sauce.","Add the greens in handfuls, letting each wilt before the next. They collapse a long way.","Cover and cook 5 to 8 minutes until tender but still green. There should be almost no liquid. Serve with ugali."]},
"kenyan|Tilapia Fry": {"time":"About 30 minutes","steps":["Score the fish on both sides. Rub with the garlic, lemon, salt, turmeric and chilli. Rest 20 minutes.","Pat the surface fairly dry so it fries rather than steams.","Heat the oil to medium-hot. Fry the fish until the skin is crisp and the flesh flakes at the bone, about 5 minutes a side for a whole fish.","Do not move it until the skin releases from the pan.","Drain.","The flesh should be moist, the spice on the skin, the lemon bright. Eat hot."]},
"kenyan|Ugali": {"time":"About 30 minutes","steps":["Boil water in a heavy pot. A wide pot is easier to stir.","Rain in the white cornmeal, whisking, until it is a thick porridge.","Turn the heat down. Stir hard with a wooden spoon, then press and fold, until the ugali pulls away from the sides of the pot and holds a shape, about 10 minutes. Wet it and it is porridge; cooked out, it is ugali.","Taste a pinch. It should be cooked through, not grainy-raw, and plain.","Turn it out onto a plate. Tear off pieces with your right hand to scoop stew. It sets as it cools."]},
"korean|Bibimbap": {"time":"About 1 hour","steps":["Cook the short-grain rice. While it rests, season each vegetable on its own: spinach, bean sprouts, carrot, zucchini and shiitake, each blanched or stir-fried with a few drops of sesame oil. They stay separate until the bowl.","Cook the beef bulgogi in a hot pan until the edges caramelise.","Fry the egg so the white is set and the yolk is still runny.","In a bowl, put the rice, then arrange the vegetables and beef in sections on top. Lay the egg in the centre and add a spoon of gochujang.","The person eating mixes it all, breaking the yolk through the rice. Serve extra sesame oil at the table."]},
"korean|Bulgogi": {"time":"About 30 minutes, plus marinating","steps":["If the beef sirloin is not already thin, freeze it 30 minutes and slice it paper thin across the grain.","Grate the Asian pear. Mix it with the soy sauce, sesame oil, sugar, garlic and ginger. Fold through the beef. Marinate at least 30 minutes, up to overnight.","Cook in a very hot pan in batches. The sugar and pear should caramelise. Do not stew it in its own juice; pour off liquid if it pools, then let it fry.","Pile onto lettuce leaves with a little of the pan sauce.","Eat wrapped, while it is still hot."]},
"korean|Galbi": {"time":"About 30 minutes, plus marinating","steps":["If the beef short ribs are in long strips, cut them into portions. Score the meat so the marinade gets in.","Grate the Asian pear. Mix it with the soy sauce, brown sugar, sesame oil, garlic and ginger. Coat the ribs and marinate at least 4 hours, overnight is better.","Grill over high heat or under a hot grill, turning once, until caramelised and cooked through. The sugar burns quickly, so do not walk away.","Rest 3 minutes.","Slice and serve with rice and lettuce."]},
"korean|Japchae": {"time":"About 45 minutes","steps":["Soak the sweet potato noodles in warm water until they bend, then boil until just transparent and chewy, about 6 minutes. Drain and toss with a spoon of the sesame oil so they do not glue into a block.","Slice the beef thin. Mix it with a spoon of soy sauce, a little garlic and a pinch of sugar. Cut the carrot into matchsticks and slice the onion.","Blanch the spinach in boiling water for 20 seconds, squeeze it dry, and season with a pinch of salt and a few drops of sesame oil.","Fry the beef until just cooked and set it aside. In the same pan fry the carrot and onion until the onion is sweet and the carrot still has bite.","Add the noodles, the rest of the soy sauce, the sugar, the remaining garlic and sesame oil. Toss on the heat until every strand is brown and glossy and the sauce has been absorbed, about 3 minutes.","Fold in the beef, spinach and chopped scallion off the heat. Japchae is eaten warm or at room temperature, the noodles chewy, not soft, and lightly sweet."]},
"korean|Jjajangmyeon": {"time":"About 40 minutes","steps":["Boil the fresh noodles until just tender. Toss so they do not stick.","Dice the pork belly, onion, zucchini and potato the same small size. Fry the pork until the fat renders and the meat browns.","Add the vegetables and fry until the onion is soft and the potato is half cooked.","Stir in the chunjang and fry it in the pork fat for 1 minute so it loses its raw bean smell. Add water to make a thick sauce and simmer until the potato is soft and the sauce is glossy and dark, about 10 minutes.","Put noodles in bowls and ladle the black sauce over them. Mix at the table."]},
"korean|Kimchi": {"time":"About 1 hour, plus 2 to 5 days fermenting","steps":["Cut the napa cabbage into quarters through the core. Salt it generously with the coarse sea salt, getting between the leaves. Leave 2 hours, turning once, until the leaves bend without breaking. Rinse and drain well.","Make a paste of gochugaru, fish sauce, garlic, ginger and a little water. Fold in the Korean radish, cut into matchsticks, and the green onion.","Work the paste between every cabbage leaf. Pack into a jar, pressing out air pockets. Leave a little space at the top.","Leave at cool room temperature for 1 to 2 days, then taste. When it is lively and sour, move it to the fridge.","It keeps for weeks and gets sourer. Use clean utensils."]},
"korean|Kimchi Jjigae": {"time":"About 40 minutes","steps":["Use kimchi that is already sour. Fresh sweet kimchi makes a dull stew. Chop it, and keep the brine.","Slice the pork belly and the onion. Heat the sesame oil and fry the pork until the fat renders and the edges brown.","Add the onion, garlic and gochugaru. Fry 1 minute until the oil turns red. Add the kimchi and fry it until the edges soften, about 5 minutes. Frying the kimchi before adding water is what makes the stew deep rather than watery.","Pour in the kimchi brine and enough water to cover, about 500 ml. Add a little salt only if the brine is mild. Simmer 15 minutes.","Cut the tofu into thick slices and lay them on top. Simmer 8 minutes more, until the tofu has drunk the broth and the pork is tender.","Finish with the scallion. The stew should be red, sour and bubbling, thick enough that the tofu sits proud of the liquid."]},
"korean|Korean BBQ": {"time":"About 30 minutes at the table","steps":["If you can, freeze the pork belly and beef short ribs briefly and slice the belly thick and the galbi thin across the bone, or ask the butcher to do it.","Set a grill or a hot pan in the centre. Cook the meat in batches over high heat. Pork belly is done when the fat renders and the edges crisp; galbi when it caramelises and is just cooked.","Wrap a piece in lettuce with a slice of raw garlic, a dab of ssamjang and a bit of kimchi.","Do not sauce the meat on the grill or the ssamjang burns. The wrap is the seasoning.","Eat as it comes off the heat."]},
"korean|Samgyetang": {"time":"About 1 hour 20 minutes","steps":["Soak the glutinous rice for 1 hour, then drain. A dry filling stays hard inside the chicken.","Trim the chicken and pat it dry. Stuff the cavity with the rice, several garlic cloves, the ginseng and half the jujubes. Cross the legs and tie them, or sew the cavity shut with a skewer, so the rice stays in.","Put the chicken in a pot just big enough to hold it. Add the remaining garlic and jujubes. Cover with water by 3 cm.","Bring to a boil, skim the foam, then cover and simmer very gently for 50 to 60 minutes. The broth should tremble, not roll. A hard boil makes a cloudy, stringy soup.","The chicken is done when a skewer in the thigh runs clear and the rice inside has swollen and split the skin a little.","Lift the chicken into a deep bowl and ladle the clear broth around it. Serve the salt and chopped scallion on the side so each person seasons their own bowl. The ginseng and jujube are eaten with the chicken, and the rice is spooned from the cavity."]},
"korean|Tteokbokki": {"time":"About 25 minutes","steps":["If the rice cakes are refrigerated, soak them in warm water for 10 minutes so they soften.","Simmer the anchovy broth. Stir in the gochujang and gochugaru until the broth turns a deep red and starts to thicken.","Add the rice cakes and fish cakes. Cook, stirring often, until the sauce clings and the rice cakes are chewy all the way through, about 8 to 10 minutes. Add a splash of broth if it gets pasty.","Toss in the green onion for the last minute.","Serve bubbling. The sauce should stain the spoon."]},
"laotian|Jeow Bong": {"time":"About 30 minutes","steps":["Dry-roast the dried chilli in a pan until it darkens and smells toasted, not burnt. Soak it in a little warm water for 10 minutes, then drain.","Roast the unpeeled garlic and shallot and the galangal in a dry pan until the skins blacken in spots and the insides are soft. Peel the garlic and shallot.","Pound the chilli, garlic, shallot and galangal to a coarse paste. Jeow is a relish, not a smooth sauce. Leave some texture.","Heat the oil and fry the paste for 4 minutes until it darkens and the raw smell goes. Add the fish sauce, sugar and salt.","Cook, stirring, until the sugar melts and the paste turns glossy and thick, about 3 minutes. It should be hot, salty, a little sweet, and smoky.","Cool. Jeow bong keeps and is eaten in small spoons with sticky rice. A little is enough."]},
"laotian|Khao Piak Sen": {"time":"About 1 hour","steps":["Cover the chicken with water, add the ginger and salt, and simmer until the meat is tender, about 40 minutes. Lift the chicken out, shred it, and keep the broth. You need about 1.2 litres of broth; add water if it has reduced too far.","Mix the rice flour and tapioca flour with 120 ml of the hot broth, stirred in slowly, to a soft dough. Knead until smooth. The tapioca is what makes the noodles chewy rather than brittle.","Rest the dough 15 minutes. Roll it into ropes and cut short noodles, or pinch small pieces. Dust them so they do not stick.","Bring the broth to a boil. Drop in the noodles and cook until they float and turn translucent, about 4 minutes.","Add the shredded chicken, fish sauce and chopped garlic. Simmer 2 minutes.","Serve with scallion and lime. Khao piak sen is a thick chicken noodle soup, the noodles soft and slippery, the broth clean."]},
"laotian|Khao Poon": {"time":"About 45 minutes","steps":["Simmer the chicken in water until cooked. Shred the meat and keep the broth.","Fry the red curry paste in the thick part of the coconut milk until fragrant. Add the shredded chicken.","Pour in the coconut milk and enough chicken broth to make a thin, aromatic soup. Simmer 10 minutes. It should be soupy, not a thick curry.","Cook the rice vermicelli, rinse, and divide into bowls.","Ladle the coconut broth and chicken over the noodles. Serve hot."]},
"laotian|Larb": {"time":"About 25 minutes","steps":["Toast the sticky rice powder in a dry pan until pale gold and nutty if it is not already toasted.","Cook the minced chicken with a splash of water, breaking it up, until just done. Do not brown it hard.","Off the heat, season with lime juice and fish sauce until it tastes sour and salty.","Fold in the sliced shallot, mint and the toasted rice powder.","Serve warm with raw vegetables and sticky rice. The rice powder should still crunch."]},
"laotian|Mok Pa": {"time":"About 40 minutes","steps":["Cut the fish into thick pieces. Slice the lemongrass very fine and shred the lime leaves.","Mix the fish with the coconut milk, lemongrass, lime leaves, chilli, dill, fish sauce and salt. The mixture should be moist, not swimming.","Soften the banana leaves over a flame or in hot water until they bend. Lay a portion of fish in each and fold into a tight parcel. Tie it.","Steam the parcels for 18 to 20 minutes, until the fish is just opaque and the coconut has set around it.","Open one to check. The fish should flake, and the leaf should smell green, not scorched.","Serve in the leaf. Mok pa is a steamed fish custard, softer than a curry, fragrant with dill and lemongrass."]},
"laotian|Or Lam": {"time":"About 1 hour 15 minutes","steps":["Cut the brisket into small pieces. Bruise the lemongrass. Toast the sticky rice in a dry pan until deep gold, then pound it to a coarse powder. Toasted rice thickens or lam and gives it a nutty smell.","Cover the beef with water, add the lemongrass and salt, and simmer until the beef is tender, about 50 minutes.","Pound the garlic and chilli to a coarse paste and stir it into the pot with the fish sauce.","Add the eggplant, cut in chunks, and the yardlong beans, cut short. Simmer until the eggplant collapses and thickens the stew, about 12 minutes.","Stir in the toasted rice powder. Cook 3 minutes. The stew should be thick enough to eat with sticky rice, not a thin soup.","Finish with the dill off the heat so it stays fragrant. Or lam tastes herbal, a little bitter from the eggplant, and smoky from the toasted rice."]},
"laotian|Ping Gai": {"time":"About 40 minutes, plus marinating","steps":["Cut the chicken into bite-sized pieces, bone in. Pound the lemongrass, garlic and chilli to a coarse paste.","Mix the paste with the fish sauce, the juice of the lime, salt and oil. Coat the chicken and rest it at least 1 hour.","Thread the chicken on skewers, or lay it in one layer if you have no skewers. Do not crowd it.","Grill or fry on a hot pan, turning, until the skin is blotched with char and the meat is just cooked, about 8 to 10 minutes. The lemongrass should smell toasted, not burnt black.","Rest 5 minutes. The lime in the marinade cooks on the chicken; do not add a wet sauce at the end or you lose the char.","Ping gai is eaten with sticky rice, pinched into balls. The chicken should be salty, sour and fragrant, with a dry grilled surface."]},
"laotian|Sai Oua": {"time":"About 1 hour","steps":["Chop the lemongrass, galangal, kaffir lime leaves and shallot very fine, or pound them to a coarse paste. The flavour has to be through the meat, not in chunks.","Mix the paste into the ground pork until the mixture is sticky.","Rinse the sausage casings and slide them onto a funnel. Stuff firmly but not so tight that they split, twisting into links.","Prick any air bubbles. Rest 15 minutes.","Grill over medium heat, turning, until browned and cooked through, about 15 minutes. Rest 5 minutes before cutting. The inside should be juicy and smell of lemongrass."]},
"laotian|Sticky Rice": {"time":"About 40 minutes, plus soaking","steps":["Rinse the glutinous rice until the water is no longer milky. Soak it for at least 4 hours, or overnight. Unsoaked sticky rice stays hard in the middle.","Drain it well. Line a steamer basket with muslin or a piece of banana leaf with holes, so the grains do not fall through.","Spread the rice in an even layer. Steam over boiling water for 20 to 25 minutes. Flip the cake of rice halfway so the top gets the steam.","It is done when the grains are translucent and chewy, stuck together but not gummy.","Keep it covered in the basket. Eat with your hands, pinching off balls to pick up larb or papaya salad. It hardens as it cools."]},
"laotian|Tam Som": {"time":"About 20 minutes","steps":["Shred the green papaya. It must be crisp and unripe.","Bruise the cherry tomato in a mortar.","Add lime juice and fish sauce and mix. Taste before the papaya goes in: sour and salty, not sweet.","Toss in the papaya and pound lightly so it softens at the edges and stays crunchy.","Fold in the peanuts. Serve at once with sticky rice."]},
"lebanese|Baba Ghanoush": {"time":"About 50 minutes","steps":["Prick the eggplant and char it over a flame or under a very hot grill, turning, until the skin is black and the inside has collapsed. This smoke is the flavour.","Cool it in a bowl, then peel. Scrape out the flesh and discard long strips of seed if they are bitter. Drain the flesh for 10 minutes; watery eggplant makes watery dip.","Mash it with the tahini, lemon juice and garlic. It should be smoky, lemony and a little coarse, not a puree from a blender if you can help it.","Spread it out, pour on the olive oil, and scatter the pomegranate seeds.","Serve with bread."]},
"lebanese|Fattoush": {"time":"About 25 minutes","steps":["Tear the pita and toast or bake it until it is completely crisp. Fattoush bread must shatter. Soft bread turns to mush in the dressing.","Chop the tomato, cucumber, lettuce, radish and scallion into bite-sized pieces. Dry them if they are wet.","Whisk the lemon juice, olive oil, sumac and salt. The sumac is the sour red note; do not leave it out or this is only a chopped salad.","Toss the vegetables with the dressing and the mint.","Break the pita into the salad just before you eat, so it stays crisp for a few minutes and then softens at the edges.","Fattoush should taste lemony and sumac-sour, with crunch from the radish and the bread."]},
"lebanese|Hummus": {"time":"About 20 minutes, plus soaking if the chickpeas are dried","steps":["If the chickpeas are dried, soak them overnight and boil until they are completely soft, almost falling apart. Save some of the cooking water. Peel them if you want the hummus especially smooth.","Blend the warm chickpeas with the tahini, lemon juice and garlic. Add cooking water until it is silky, not pasty.","Taste: lemon and tahini should both be clear, with garlic behind them.","Spread on a plate, make a well, and pour in the olive oil.","Serve with the pita, warm."]},
"lebanese|Kibbeh": {"time":"About 1 hour 15 minutes","steps":["Soak the bulgur in cold water for 15 minutes, then squeeze it very dry.","For the filling, fry half the onion in the olive oil until gold. Add half the lamb, the pine nuts, half the spices and half the salt. Cook until the meat is brown and dry. Cool it. A wet filling bursts the shell.","For the shell, pound or process the rest of the lamb with the rest of the onion, spices and salt until it is a smooth paste. Knead in the bulgur until you have a dough you can shape. Wet your hands.","Form oval shells: make a ball, poke a hole, hollow it, fill with a spoon of the cooked lamb, and seal. Smooth every crack.","Heat the frying oil to medium-hot. Fry the kibbeh until deep brown and crisp, about 4 minutes, turning once. Do not crowd them.","Drain. The shell should be crunchy, the inside moist and spiced. Eat hot."]},
"lebanese|Manakish": {"time":"About 1 hour 10 minutes","steps":["Knead the flour with the yeast, salt and 170 ml of warm water for 8 minutes. The dough should be soft. Rise, covered, 45 minutes.","Stir the za'atar with the olive oil into a spreadable paste. It should be loose enough to smear, not a dry dust.","Divide the dough into 6 balls. Roll each into a thin round.","Dimple the surface with your fingers and spread the za'atar paste to the edge.","Bake at 240°C until the bread puffs in spots and the rim is gold, about 7 minutes. The za'atar should look glossy, not burnt.","Eat warm. Manakish is a breakfast bread, soft in the middle, fragrant with thyme and sesame from the za'atar, and slick with olive oil."]},
"lebanese|Mujadara": {"time":"About 50 minutes","steps":["Rinse the lentils and simmer them in water until they are just tender but still hold their shape, about 15 minutes. Drain and keep the cooking liquid.","Slice the onion thin. Fry it in the olive oil, slowly, until it is deep brown and crisp, about 20 minutes. This dark onion is half the dish. Take half of it out to scatter at the end.","Add the cumin to the onion in the pan and fry 30 seconds. Add the rice and stir until the grains look glossy.","Add the lentils, salt, and enough of the lentil water to sit 1 cm above the rice. Cover and cook on low until the rice is done and the water is gone, about 15 minutes.","Rest 10 minutes. Fluff gently.","Pile the crisp onions on top. Mujadara should be earthy, the rice and lentils separate, the onion both sweet in the pot and shatter-crisp on top."]},
"lebanese|Shawarma": {"time":"About 40 minutes, plus marinating","steps":["Slice the chicken thighs. Mix them with the yogurt, garlic and cumin. Marinate at least 2 hours.","Spread on a hot pan or grill and cook until browned and just cooked through. Do not pile it so deep that it steams.","Warm the pita.","Fill with chicken, pickled turnip and tomato.","Spoon a little of the garlicky yogurt over. Eat while the bread is soft and the chicken is hot."]},
"lebanese|Shish Taouk": {"time":"About 30 minutes, plus marinating","steps":["Cut the chicken into cubes. Crush the garlic with the salt and mix it with the yogurt, lemon juice, tomato paste, paprika and olive oil.","Coat the chicken and rest it at least 2 hours. The yogurt tenderises; a short marinade only flavours the surface.","Thread onto skewers, pieces close together.","Grill or pan-fry on high, turning, until the edges char and the centre is just cooked, about 8 minutes. Do not cook until the cubes are dry.","Rest 5 minutes. Warm the pita.","Slide the chicken into the bread. Shish taouk should be tangy, a little smoky, and still juicy, the paprika red on the crust."]},
"lebanese|Tabbouleh": {"time":"About 30 minutes","steps":["Rinse the bulgur and soak it in warm water for 15 minutes, until it is tender but still has a bite. Drain it and squeeze it dry. Tabbouleh is a parsley salad with a little grain, not a grain salad with a little parsley.","Wash the parsley and dry it completely. Wet parsley turns the salad into juice. Chop it very fine with a sharp knife, not a machine, which blackens it.","Dice the tomato small and chop the scallion and mint.","Toss the parsley, bulgur, tomato, scallion and mint with the salt.","Dress with the lemon juice and olive oil just before serving. If you dress it early, the tomato weeps and the parsley wilts.","Taste. It should be sharply lemony, green, and only lightly oiled. Serve soon."]},
"lebanese|Warak Enab": {"time":"About 1 hour 30 minutes","steps":["Rinse the grape leaves and blanch them until pliable. Cut off the stems.","Mix the rice with the grated onion, chopped tomato, parsley, mint, half the olive oil and the salt. Do not cook the rice first. It finishes in the roll.","Place a thin line of filling on each leaf and roll tightly, tucking the sides. The rolls must be slim.","Pack them seam down in a pot. Pour over the rest of the olive oil, the lemon juice and enough water to cover. Weight with a plate.","Simmer gently for 45 minutes, until the rice is tender and the leaves are silky. The liquid should be almost gone.","Cool to warm. Warak enab should be lemony, herby and glossy, the rice fully cooked but the roll still firm enough to pick up."]},
"malaysian|Apam Balik": {"time":"About 35 minutes","steps":["Whisk the flour, sugar, baking soda and salt. Add the egg and coconut milk and whisk to a smooth, pourable batter, a little thicker than crepe batter. Rest 15 minutes.","Chop the peanuts and mix them with the sweet corn and a pinch of the sugar.","Heat a small pan and grease it with a little of the butter. Pour a ladle of batter and tilt it into a round.","Cook on medium until the top is full of bubbles and looks set, and the underside is gold, about 2 minutes.","Scatter corn and peanuts over one half, fold the pancake over, and press for 20 seconds so the filling warms.","Eat hot. Apam balik should be soft and coconut-sweet, with a crisp underside and a juicy corn filling."]},
"malaysian|Asam Pedas": {"time":"About 35 minutes","steps":["Soak the dried chilli until soft. Blend or pound it with the shallot, garlic, ginger and turmeric to a paste.","Heat the oil and fry the paste until it darkens and smells sweet, about 6 minutes. Add a spoon of water if it sticks.","Stir in the tamarind and the water. Simmer 5 minutes. Taste: asam pedas must be sour first, then hot. Add salt.","Cut the mackerel into steaks. Slide them into the simmering gravy and cook gently until just done, about 8 minutes. Do not boil hard or the fish breaks.","The gravy should be thin and red, enough to spoon over rice, not a dry fry.","Rest 5 minutes so the fish firms. The sourness deepens as it sits."]},
"malaysian|Cendol": {"time":"About 40 minutes, plus chilling","steps":["Blend or bruise the pandan with 300 ml of water and strain the green liquid. Whisk it into the rice flour until smooth.","Cook the flour mixture on low, stirring, until it turns translucent and thick, like a paste, about 8 minutes.","Press the paste through a coarse strainer or a cendol frame into a bowl of ice water. The drops should set into short green worms. Drain them.","Simmer the palm sugar with 80 ml of water until it dissolves into a dark syrup. Cool it.","Stir the salt into the coconut milk. Salt is what makes cendol taste finished rather than only sweet.","In each glass put cendol, shaved ice, coconut milk and a spoon of palm syrup. Do not stir it into one colour before you serve it; the green, white and dark syrup should meet in the spoon."]},
"malaysian|Char Kway Teow": {"time":"About 25 minutes","steps":["Separate the flat rice noodles with your hands so they do not go into the wok as a block. Slice the sausage. Have every ingredient next to the stove. This fry takes about 3 minutes and cannot wait.","Heat a wok until it smokes. Add the oil, garlic and chilli paste and fry 20 seconds until the oil turns red.","Add the sausage and shrimp. Fry until the shrimp just turn pink.","Add the noodles, soy sauce and salt. Toss hard so every strand is stained and a few edges char against the wok. That char is the smell of char kway teow.","Push the noodles aside, crack in the eggs, scramble them, then toss everything together with the bean sprouts for 30 seconds. The sprouts should stay crisp.","Serve at once, in the wok if you can. If the noodles clump, the wok was not hot enough; fry the next batch smaller."]},
"malaysian|Laksa": {"time":"About 45 minutes","steps":["Cook the rice noodles, rinse them cold, and drain. Slice the cucumber. This soup is assembled, so the noodles should be ready before the broth boils.","Heat the oil and fry the laksa paste until it darkens and the oil separates, about 5 minutes. Frying the paste is the flavour; raw paste tastes harsh.","Add the shrimp and toss until they turn pink. Pour in the coconut milk and 300 ml of water. Simmer gently 8 minutes. Do not boil hard or the coconut splits.","Stir in the fish sauce. Taste: it should be rich, spicy and a little sour already from the paste. Add lime off the heat.","Put noodles and bean sprouts in bowls. Ladle over the shrimp and broth.","Top with cucumber and mint. Laksa is eaten hot, the noodles slippery, the broth creamy and hot with chilli."]},
"malaysian|Mee Goreng": {"time":"About 25 minutes","steps":["Boil the potato until just tender, then dice it. Cook the egg noodles until barely done, rinse, and drain.","Heat the oil and fry the garlic and chilli paste until fragrant. Add the shrimp and potato and fry until the shrimp turn pink.","Add the chopped tomato and cook until it softens and sweetens, about 3 minutes.","Add the noodles, soy sauce and salt. Toss until the noodles are stained and a few edges catch.","Push them aside, scramble the eggs in the wok, and fold them through.","Mee goreng should be savoury, a little hot, and dry enough that the noodles do not sit in sauce. Serve at once."]},
"malaysian|Nasi Lemak": {"time":"About 1 hour","steps":["Rinse the jasmine rice. Cook it with the coconut milk and the pandan leaves, knotted. Use a little water if the coconut milk alone is too rich to cook the rice through. When it is done, fluff it and remove the pandan.","Dry-fry the anchovies until crisp. Roast or fry the peanuts until they colour.","Warm the sambal. Boil the eggs for 8 minutes if they are not already hard, cool, and halve them.","Slice the cucumber.","On a plate: coconut rice, a spoon of sambal, anchovies, peanuts, egg and cucumber. Every part is eaten together."]},
"malaysian|Rendang": {"time":"About 3 hours","steps":["Pound or blend the lemongrass, galangal, shallot, garlic and soaked dried chilli to a paste.","Fry the paste until it is darker and the coconut fat separates, about 10 minutes. This is where rendang gets its depth.","Add the beef chuck, cut large, and stir until it is coated. Pour in the coconut milk.","Add the kaffir lime leaves. Simmer uncovered, stirring more often as it thickens, for 2 to 2 and a half hours. The coconut milk must reduce until it is a dry, dark paste clinging to the beef, not a curry.","Stir in the toasted coconut in the last 20 minutes. The beef should be tender enough to part with a spoon, and the paste should fry in its own fat at the end. Rest 15 minutes."]},
"malaysian|Roti Canai": {"time":"About 2 hours, plus resting","steps":["Mix the flour with a spoon of the ghee, the condensed milk and water to a soft, oily dough. Knead until smooth.","Divide into balls, coat each generously in ghee, and rest at least 1 hour, longer if you can. The rest is what lets it stretch.","Lightly flour the table with a pinch of the dough. Flatten a ball and stretch it with damp hands until it is a thin, almost transparent sheet. Fold the edges in, trapping air, into a square.","Cook on a hot griddle with a little ghee until both sides are flaky and spotted, about 2 minutes a side. Clap the hot roti between your hands to shatter the layers.","Serve with the dhal curry for dipping."]},
"malaysian|Satay": {"time":"About 40 minutes, plus marinating","steps":["Cut the chicken into strips. Pound the lemongrass with the turmeric, cumin, coriander seeds, half the sugar, the salt and half the oil. Coat the chicken and rest it at least 1 hour.","Thread the chicken on skewers, pressing the pieces close so they stay juicy.","For the sauce, warm the peanut butter with the tamarind, the chopped chilli, the rest of the sugar and 100 ml of water. Simmer, stirring, until it is thick enough to coat a spoon, about 5 minutes. Add a pinch of the salt.","Grill or pan-fry the skewers, brushing with the rest of the oil, until the edges char and the chicken is just cooked, about 3 minutes a side.","Rest 3 minutes. The turmeric crust should be fragrant, not burnt.","Serve the skewers with the warm peanut sauce. The sauce is savoury and sour, not a sweet dip."]},
"mexican|Chiles Rellenos": {"time":"About 50 minutes","steps":["Char the poblanos over a flame or under a grill until the skins blacken. Bag them for 10 minutes, then peel. Slit each one and remove the seeds, keeping the pepper whole.","Stuff each with cheese and close the slit. Dust lightly with flour so the batter holds.","Separate the eggs. Whip the whites until stiff, then fold in the yolks and a pinch of salt. This batter should be airy.","Heat the oil. Dip each pepper in the batter and fry until the coating is puffed and gold, about 3 minutes, turning once.","Fry the onion and garlic, add the tomato and salt, and cook to a light sauce.","Serve the peppers with the sauce. The crust should be crisp and airy, the cheese molten, the chilli still a little hot."]},
"mexican|Cochinita Pibil": {"time":"About 3 hours","steps":["Cut the pork into large chunks. Mix the achiote with the orange juice, half the vinegar, crushed garlic, oregano and salt. Coat the pork and rest it at least 2 hours. Achiote stains and seasons; the pork should look deep red.","Pack the pork into a snug pot or a banana-leaf-lined dish if you have leaves. Pour the marinade over. Cover tightly.","Cook at 160°C until the pork shreds easily, about 2 hours 30 minutes. Uncover for the last 20 minutes if you want the edges to brown.","Slice the onion and cover it with the rest of the vinegar and a pinch of salt. Add the sliced habanero. This pickle is the sharp contrast.","Shred the pork in its juices. It should be sour, earthy and red, moist, not dry.","Serve with the pickled onion. The habanero stays on the side for those who want the heat."]},
"mexican|Elote": {"time":"About 20 minutes","steps":["Husk the corn, or leave a few leaves as a handle. Grill or boil it until the kernels are tender and, if grilled, spotted with char, about 10 minutes.","Brush each cob with mayonnaise while it is hot so the fat melts into the kernels.","Roll or sprinkle with crumbled cotija, chilli powder and salt.","Squeeze lime over each cob.","Eat at once, off the cob. The cheese should cling, the chilli be visible, the corn sweet.","If you have no grill, a hot dry pan will blister the kernels well enough."]},
"mexican|Enchiladas": {"time":"About 45 minutes","steps":["Simmer the chicken until tender, then shred it. Toast and soak the chilli. Blend it with the tomato, half the onion, the garlic, salt and a ladle of the chicken broth.","Fry this sauce in a spoon of the oil for 5 minutes until it thickens. Stir half of it into the shredded chicken.","Soften each tortilla in a little hot oil for a few seconds so it bends without cracking. Dip it in the remaining sauce.","Fill with chicken, roll, and pack the rolls in a dish. Pour the rest of the sauce over.","Scatter the cheese and the rest of the chopped onion. Bake at 180°C until the cheese melts, about 12 minutes.","The tortillas should be tender, not fried hard, the sauce red and clinging. Serve hot."]},
"mexican|Guacamole": {"time":"About 15 minutes","steps":["Halve the ripe avocado, lift out the pit, and scoop the flesh. It should give under a gentle press. Hard avocado will not mash into a cream.","Mash it with a fork, leaving some lumps. Stir in the lime juice at once so it stays green.","Fold in the finely chopped red onion, jalapeño, cilantro and tomato.","Taste. It needs enough lime to taste bright, not flat.","Serve with the tortilla chips, the pit set in the bowl if you are not eating it all at once."]},
"mexican|Mole Poblano": {"time":"About 1 hour 30 minutes","steps":["Toast the dried chilli in a dry pan until fragrant, then soak it until soft. Discard the driest stems.","Toast the almonds and sesame seeds until gold. Fry the onion, garlic and tomato until soft.","Blend the chilli, tomato mixture, almonds, half the sesame, cinnamon and a ladle of water to a smooth paste.","Fry the paste in the oil, stirring, until it darkens and thickens, about 10 minutes. Add water to make a sauce that coats a spoon. Stir in the chocolate until it melts. Add salt. The chocolate rounds the chilli; it should not taste like dessert.","Simmer the chicken in water until just tender, about 25 minutes. Add it to the mole and simmer 15 minutes so the sauce clings.","Scatter the rest of the sesame. Mole should be dark, complex, a little sweet and hot, thick enough to nap the chicken."]},
"mexican|Pozole": {"time":"About 2 hours","steps":["Cover the pork with water, add half the onion and the garlic, and simmer until the meat is tender, about 1 hour 15 minutes. Skim.","Toast and soak the chilli, then blend it with a ladle of broth. Stir it into the pot with the oregano and salt. The broth should turn red and taste of chilli, not only of pork.","Add the hominy and simmer 30 minutes, until the hominy is tender and has opened. If it is canned, rinse it and add it for the last 15 minutes.","Shred the pork and return it.","Shred the cabbage, slice the radish and cut the lime.","Serve the soup with those on the side so each bowl gets crunch and acid. Pozole is a hominy and pork soup, the toppings essential."]},
"mexican|Quesadillas": {"time":"About 20 minutes","steps":["Char, peel and slice the poblanos. Slice the onion and cook both in a spoon of the oil until the onion is soft. Salt them.","Warm a dry pan. Lay a tortilla in it, scatter cheese and a spoon of the pepper and onion, and fold it.","Cook until the tortilla is spotted and the cheese stretches, about 2 minutes a side. A dry pan keeps the tortilla soft; a lot of oil makes it a cracker.","Repeat. Do not stack them covered or they steam and the cheese sets hard.","The cheese should be fully melted and stringy, the poblano still a little hot.","Eat immediately."]},
"mexican|Tacos": {"time":"About 40 minutes, plus marinating","steps":["Slice the skirt steak. Rub it with the cumin and a squeeze of the lime. Rest 20 minutes.","Chop the white onion and cilantro. Halve or slice the avocado. Warm the salsa verde.","Grill or sear the steak over very high heat, a minute or two a side for medium-rare. Rest 5 minutes, then chop it against the grain.","Warm the corn tortillas on a dry pan until they are soft and speckled.","Fill each tortilla with steak, onion, cilantro, avocado and salsa. Finish with lime. Eat standing up if you can; they are not meant to wait."]},
"mexican|Tamales": {"time":"About 3 hours","steps":["Soak the corn husks in hot water until they bend.","Beat the lard until it is fluffy. Mix in the masa harina, enough warm chicken broth to make a soft, spreadable dough. A spoon of it should float in cold water when it is aerated enough.","Shred or chop the cooked pork shoulder and warm it in the red chilli sauce until it is moist, not swimming.","Spread masa on the smooth side of a husk, leaving a border. Put a spoon of pork down the centre. Fold the husk over the filling and fold up the bottom.","Stand the tamales in a steamer, open end up. Steam for 1 hour to 1 hour 30 minutes, until the masa pulls cleanly away from the husk.","Rest 10 minutes. Unwrap at the table."]},
"moroccan|Baghrir": {"time":"About 30 minutes","steps":["Blend or whisk the semolina, flour, yeast, sugar and salt with 400 ml of warm water to a thin, smooth batter. Rest 30 minutes, until bubbly.","Heat a non-stick pan on medium and wipe it lightly. Pour a small ladle and let it spread.","Cook on one side only until the surface is covered in holes and looks dry, about 2 minutes. Do not flip. The holes are the point; they drink the butter.","If there are no holes, the batter is too thick or the pan too hot. Loosen with a spoon of water.","Stack the baghrir. Melt the butter with the honey.","Serve warm, the honey-butter poured into the holes. They should be spongy and tender."]},
"moroccan|Bissara": {"time":"About 40 minutes","steps":["Soak the fava beans overnight and peel them if the skins are tough. Simmer with the garlic and salt until completely soft, about 30 minutes.","Blend or mash with enough of the cooking water to make a smooth, thick puree. It should hold a spoon.","Stir in half the olive oil and half the cumin.","Warm it through. If it is stiff, add a splash of the cooking water.","Serve in bowls, with the rest of the olive oil, cumin and paprika on top, and the lemon.","Bissara is a fava puree, earthy and smooth, the oil pooling on top."]},
"moroccan|Couscous": {"time":"About 1 hour 45 minutes","steps":["Brown the lamb. Add the sliced onion, ras el hanout, and saffron, and cover with water. Simmer 45 minutes.","Add the carrot, turnip, zucchini, tomato and chickpeas in the order of how long they take, carrot and turnip first. Cook until the vegetables are tender and the broth is fragrant.","Steam the couscous, or cover it with boiling water or a ladle of the broth, cover, and let it swell. Fluff it with a fork and repeat once so the grains are separate.","Pile the couscous on a platter, make a well, and fill it with lamb and vegetables. Moisten with broth.","Serve the remaining broth in a bowl to pour over."]},
"moroccan|Harira": {"time":"About 1 hour","steps":["Soak the chickpeas overnight. Dice the lamb and onion.","Heat the olive oil and brown the lamb with the onion, ginger and cinnamon. Add the tomato and cook until it softens.","Add the chickpeas, lentils, celery, salt and water to make a soup. Simmer until the legumes and lamb are tender, about 40 minutes.","Whisk the flour with a ladle of broth and stir it back in. Simmer 5 minutes until the soup thickens slightly.","Finish with chopped coriander. Harira should be hearty, tomato-red, scented with cinnamon and ginger.","It is a soup, spoonable, with the chickpeas whole and the lentils soft."]},
"moroccan|Mechoui": {"time":"About 2 hours 30 minutes","steps":["Cut deep slits in the lamb. Crush the garlic with the salt, cumin and paprika and push the paste into the slits and over the surface. Rest 1 hour.","Set the lamb on a rack. Roast at 160°C, basting with the butter, until the meat is very tender and the outside is browned, about 2 hours.","If the surface darkens too fast, cover it loosely.","Rest 15 minutes. The cumin crust should be fragrant, the inside moist.","Pull or slice the meat. Spoon the buttery juices over it.","Mechoui is slow-roasted lamb, seasoned boldly with cumin and salt, eaten with the fingers."]},
"moroccan|Msemen": {"time":"About 45 minutes","steps":["Mix the flour, semolina and salt with water to a soft dough. Knead 8 minutes. Rest 15 minutes.","Divide into 8 balls. Oil your hands and the board.","Flatten each ball and stretch it very thin. Brush with oil and a little soft butter, fold into a square, then flatten again gently so the layers stay.","Cook on a hot pan, brushing with butter, until both sides are blotched and gold and the inside is cooked, about 3 minutes a side.","The folds should separate a little at the edge.","Eat hot. Msemen is a flaky pan bread, rich with oil and butter, soft in the middle."]},
"moroccan|Pastilla": {"time":"About 1 hour 30 minutes","steps":["Simmer the chicken with the onion, ginger, saffron, half the cinnamon and the salt until tender. Shred the meat. Reduce the broth to a cup.","Whisk the eggs into the hot broth off a hard boil and stir until they set in soft curds. Mix with the chicken and parsley.","Toast the almonds and chop them with the sugar and the rest of the cinnamon.","Brush phyllo with melted butter and line a tin, letting the sheets hang over. Add the chicken, then the almonds, and fold the phyllo over the top. Brush with butter.","Bake at 180°C until the pastry is deep gold, about 30 minutes.","Pastilla should be crisp, the filling savoury and sweet with cinnamon almond, the chicken moist. Rest 10 minutes before cutting."]},
"moroccan|Tagine": {"time":"About 2 hours 15 minutes","steps":["Heat the olive oil in a heavy pot. Brown the lamb shoulder. Add the sliced onion and cook until soft.","Stir in the cinnamon, cumin, ginger and the saffron bloomed in warm water.","Tuck in the chickpeas, soaked and drained if they were dried, and the preserved lemon, chopped. Pour in enough water to come halfway up the meat.","Cover and simmer gently until the lamb is nearly tender, about 1 hour 15 minutes.","Add the dried apricots and the honey. Cook uncovered until the sauce is syrupy and the lamb is soft, about 20 minutes.","Scatter the almonds, toasted. Rest 10 minutes. The sauce should be sweet, salty from the lemon, and spiced, not like a dessert."]},
"moroccan|Tanjia": {"time":"About 3 hours","steps":["Cut the lamb into large pieces. Crush the garlic with the cumin, saffron, salt and olive oil. Toss with the lamb and the chopped preserved lemon.","Pack it into a tight oven pot. Add 100 ml of water. Seal the lid.","Cook at 150°C for 3 hours, until the lamb is falling apart and sitting in its own fat and juices. Do not open the pot early.","There should be very little liquid left, just rich juices.","Taste the salt and the preserved lemon. It should be salty-sour and deeply cumin-scented.","Tanjia is a Marrakesh pot of slow lamb, eaten with bread to scoop the meat."]},
"moroccan|Zaalouk": {"time":"About 35 minutes","steps":["Cube the eggplant. Cook it in a covered pan with half the olive oil, a splash of water and the salt until very soft, about 15 minutes.","Chop the tomato and add it with the garlic, cumin and paprika. Cook uncovered, stirring and mashing, until the mixture is thick and the oil shows, about 12 minutes.","Add the rest of the olive oil and the lemon juice.","Mash to a coarse puree. Zaalouk should have some texture, not be a smooth soup.","Stir in the coriander off the heat.","Serve warm or at room temperature. It should taste of cumin, lemon and olive oil, the eggplant fully sweet."]},
"nepali|Aloo Tama": {"time":"About 40 minutes","steps":["Soak the black-eyed peas for 4 hours, then boil them until just tender. Drain. If the bamboo shoots are sour from the jar, rinse them and boil 5 minutes, then drain and cut into short pieces. Cube the potato.","Heat the mustard oil until it just smokes, then cool it a moment so it loses the raw edge. Add the cumin seeds.","Add the sliced onion and cook until gold. Add the chopped garlic, turmeric and red chilli powder with a spoon of water so the spices do not burn.","Add the tomato and salt and cook until the tomato breaks down. Add the potato and fry 3 minutes.","Add the bamboo shoots, the peas and enough hot water to barely cover. Simmer until the potato is soft and the gravy is thick and a little sour from the bamboo, about 15 minutes.","The peas should still hold their shape. Rest 5 minutes. Aloo tama is a sour, turmeric-yellow curry, not a dry fry."]},
"nepali|Chatamari": {"time":"About 40 minutes","steps":["Whisk the rice flour with the eggs, half the salt and enough water to make a batter thinner than pancake batter, about 300 ml. It should pour and spread. Rest 10 minutes.","Chop the onion, garlic, ginger, tomato and green chilli very fine. Mix them with the minced chicken and the rest of the salt. The topping must be fine or it will not cook on the crepe.","Heat a non-stick pan and wipe it with a little of the oil. Pour a ladle of batter and swirl it thin, like a dosa.","As soon as the top looks set but still moist, spread a thin layer of the chicken mixture over it. Cover the pan.","Cook on medium until the chicken is cooked through and the underside is spotted and crisp, about 4 minutes. Do not flip it. The steam from the cover finishes the meat.","Slide it out, cut into wedges, and repeat. Chatamari is a Newari rice crepe: crisp underneath, savoury on top, eaten at once."]},
"nepali|Chow Mein": {"time":"About 25 minutes","steps":["Boil the egg noodles until just tender. Rinse under cold water and toss so they do not stick.","Heat a wok. Stir-fry the cabbage, carrot and capsicum, cut thin, until they are bright and still crisp, about 3 minutes.","Add the noodles and the soy sauce. Toss on high heat until every strand is coated and a few edges catch.","Taste it. The vegetables should still have bite.","Serve at once. Chow mein goes limp if it waits in the pan."]},
"nepali|Dal Bhat": {"time":"About 50 minutes","steps":["Rinse the rice and cook it in salted water until the grains are separate and just soft. Spread it on a plate for a minute so steam escapes and it does not clump. This is the bhat.","Rinse the red lentils. Cover with water to sit 3 cm above, add turmeric and half the salt, and simmer until they break down, about 18 minutes. Whisk to a pourable dal, not a paste. Add a splash of hot water if it is stiff.","Slice the onion, chop the tomato, and crush the garlic and ginger. Heat the ghee, add the cumin seeds, and when they pop add the onion. Cook until gold.","Add garlic, ginger and the slit green chilli. Cook 1 minute. Add the tomato and cook until it softens.","Pour this tempering into the dal with the rest of the salt. Simmer 5 minutes. The dal should be thin enough to spoon over rice but thick enough to coat it.","Finish with coriander. Serve the rice and dal together in one plate, the dal in a pool beside the rice, not stirred in until you eat."]},
"nepali|Dum Aloo": {"time":"About 45 minutes","steps":["Boil or fry the baby potatoes until the skins blister and they are half cooked. Pierce each one so the gravy can get in.","Fry the sliced onion until golden. Add the chopped tomato and cook to a paste.","Stir in the yogurt a spoon at a time, off a fierce heat so it does not split. Simmer until the sauce is thick.","Add the potatoes and a splash of water. Cover and cook on low until they are soft and have drunk the sauce, about 15 minutes.","Rest 5 minutes. The gravy should cling, not pool."]},
"nepali|Gundruk": {"time":"About 25 minutes","steps":["Rinse the fermented mustard greens quickly and squeeze them. Do not soak them or you wash away the sourness that makes gundruk.","Heat the mustard oil until hot. Fry the chopped onion until soft.","Add the chopped tomato and cook until the tomato breaks down.","Stir in the gundruk and a splash of water. Cover and cook 8 to 10 minutes until it is soft and the sour smell has mellowed into the tomato.","Taste. It should be sour, salty and a little bitter. Serve with rice and dal."]},
"nepali|Momo": {"time":"About 1 hour 30 minutes","steps":["Mix the flour and warm water to a firm dough. Knead 5 minutes, cover, and rest 20 minutes.","Mix the minced buffalo meat with very finely chopped onion, ginger, garlic and cilantro. Knead it until sticky. Raw onion water will make the filling leak, so chop the onion fine rather than grating it.","Roll the dough into thin circles. Place a spoon of filling in each, pleat the edge into a half-moon or a round purse, and pinch it shut. There must be no gaps.","Set the momo in a greased steamer, leaving space between them. Steam over boiling water for 12 to 15 minutes, until the dough looks shiny and the meat is cooked.","Serve at once with a chilli-tomato dip. The dough toughens if they wait."]},
"nepali|Sekuwa": {"time":"About 40 minutes, plus marinating","steps":["Cut the goat meat into bite-sized pieces. Mix it with the grated or finely chopped onion, ginger-garlic paste and lemon juice. Marinate at least 1 hour.","Thread the meat onto skewers, shaking off excess onion so it does not burn.","Grill over hot coals or a very hot grill, turning, until browned and just cooked through. Goat dries out if you leave it until it is grey all the way through; a little pink at the bone is right if the outside is well seared.","Squeeze more lemon over the hot meat.","Serve with beaten rice or salad. The char is the point."]},
"nepali|Sel Roti": {"time":"About 45 minutes","steps":["Soak the rice for 4 hours, then drain it very well. Grind it with a spoon or two of water to a thick, slightly grainy batter. A silky batter makes a heavy sel; a little grit is right.","Stir in the sugar, yogurt, ghee, salt and the seeds from the cardamom. The batter should fall from a spoon in a thick ribbon. Rest 20 minutes.","Heat the oil on medium. It is ready when a drop of batter rises slowly and swells.","Pour the batter in a circle into the oil, joining the ends so you have a ring. If the stream breaks, the batter is too thick: loosen it with a spoon of water.","Fry until the underside is deep gold, then flip and fry the other side. The ring should be crisp at the edge and soft inside, about 2 minutes a side. Do not crowd the pan.","Drain. Sel roti is eaten warm the day it is fried. It softens if it is stacked while hot, so lay the rings in a single layer first."]},
"nepali|Thukpa": {"time":"About 50 minutes","steps":["If the wheat noodles are dried, soak or boil them until just tender, then rinse so they do not clump. Keep them aside.","In a pot, fry the chicken pieces until they colour. Add the chopped tomato and cook until soft.","Add the carrot and cabbage, cut thin, and enough water to make a light soup. Simmer until the chicken is cooked and the vegetables are tender but not collapsed, about 15 minutes.","Taste the broth. It should be savoury and clean, not thick.","Put noodles in each bowl and ladle the soup, chicken and vegetables over them. Serve very hot."]},
"nigerian|Akara": {"time":"About 30 minutes, plus soaking","steps":["Soak the peas and peel off the skins. The skins must go or the fritters are heavy.","Blend the peas with the onion and a little scotch bonnet, using as little water as possible, to a thick paste.","Beat the paste with a spoon for 2 minutes until it lightens and looks aerated. Salt it.","Heat the oil to medium. Drop spoons of paste and fry until deep gold and cooked through, about 4 minutes.","Drain. The outside should be crisp, the inside soft and beany, not wet and raw.","Eat hot. Akara is a savoury bean fritter, pepper-warm."]},
"nigerian|Efo Riro": {"time":"About 40 minutes","steps":["Simmer the beef with half the onion and the salt until tender. Keep a cup of broth. Chop the spinach.","Blend the tomato, pepper and a little scotch bonnet.","Heat the palm oil and fry the rest of the onion, then the blended pepper, until the sauce thickens and the oil rises, about 12 minutes.","Add the beef, crayfish and a splash of broth. Simmer 5 minutes.","Fold in the spinach and cook until it wilts and the stew is thick, about 4 minutes. Efo riro is not a watery greens soup.","Taste the salt. Rest 5 minutes. It should be red from the pepper and palm oil, the spinach soft."]},
"nigerian|Egusi Soup": {"time":"About 50 minutes","steps":["Grind the melon seeds to a coarse meal. Cut the beef into pieces and simmer it in water with half the onion and the salt until nearly tender, about 30 minutes. Keep the broth.","Heat the palm oil and fry the rest of the onion, the tomato and a little scotch bonnet until the tomato breaks down.","Stir in the ground egusi and fry 3 minutes. Add the beef broth, whisking, so the seeds swell into a thick soup rather than clumping.","Add the beef, crayfish and the rest of the salt. Simmer 10 minutes. The soup should be thick enough to mound.","Fold in the spinach and cook until it wilts, about 3 minutes.","Egusi is rich, slightly bitter-nutty, and hot. Rest 5 minutes so it thickens further."]},
"nigerian|Fried Plantain": {"time":"About 15 minutes","steps":["The plantain must be ripe, yellow with black patches. Green plantain fries starchy and hard. Peel and slice on a diagonal.","Salt the slices lightly.","Heat the oil to medium. Fry in batches until deep gold on both sides, about 2 minutes a side.","Do not crowd the pan or the temperature drops and they drink oil.","Drain. They should be sweet, soft in the centre and crisp at the edge.","Eat hot. They soften as they cool but stay good the same day."]},
"nigerian|Jollof Rice": {"time":"About 1 hour","steps":["Blend the tomato, red bell pepper, scotch bonnet and half the onion to a smooth puree.","Fry the remaining chopped onion in the vegetable oil until soft. Add the tomato paste and fry it until it darkens.","Pour in the puree and cook, stirring, until the sauce is thick and the raw taste has gone, about 20 minutes. The oil should show at the edges.","Add the thyme, curry powder and the chicken stock. Stir in the parboiled rice, well rinsed. The liquid should just cover the rice.","Cover and cook on low until the rice is tender and has absorbed the sauce, about 25 minutes. If it dries too soon, add a splash of stock, not a flood. A little crust on the bottom is welcome.","Rest 10 minutes, then fluff. The grains should be red all the way through, separate, and smoky."]},
"nigerian|Moi Moi": {"time":"About 1 hour, plus soaking","steps":["Soak the peas and rub off the skins. Rinse the skins away. Blend the peeled peas with the onion, pepper and a little scotch bonnet, adding water only as needed, to a thick smooth batter.","Stir in the palm oil, salt and beaten egg. The batter should be pourable but thick, like a custard.","Taste a dab for salt.","Ladle into small bowls or leaf parcels, leaving room to swell. Cover them.","Steam over boiling water for 40 minutes, until a skewer comes out clean and the moi moi has set.","Rest 5 minutes and turn them out. They should be smooth, savoury and lightly spicy, firm enough to slice."]},
"nigerian|Pepper Soup": {"time":"About 40 minutes","steps":["Cut the goat into pieces. Cover with water, add the salt, and bring to a boil. Skim.","Add the sliced onion, ginger and pepper soup spice. The spice mix is the fragrance of the soup; do not replace it with only chilli.","Simmer until the goat is tender, about 35 minutes.","Add the whole or pierced scotch bonnet for the last 10 minutes so the heat perfumes the broth. Remove it if you do not want it to burst.","The broth should be thin, spicy and aromatic, not a thick stew.","Finish with scent leaf. Serve hot, the meat tender, the broth loud with spice."]},
"nigerian|Pounded Yam": {"time":"About 30 minutes","steps":["Peel the yam and cut it into chunks. Boil it in water until a knife meets no resistance, about 20 minutes. Undercooked yam will not pound smooth.","Drain it. While it is hot, pound or beat it with a little of the boiling water and the salt until it is smooth, stretchy and free of lumps.","Dip the pestle or spoon in water so the yam does not stick. Keep going until it pulls in one mass.","If it is stiff, work in a spoon more hot water. If it is sloppy, you added too much; beat it over low heat to dry it a little.","Shape it into a smooth mound.","Pounded yam should be white, elastic and mild. It is the swallow beside a soup, eaten hot. It firms as it cools."]},
"nigerian|Puff Puff": {"time":"About 45 minutes, plus rising","steps":["Dissolve the yeast and sugar in the warm water. Stir in the flour, salt and nutmeg to a thick batter, looser than bread dough. It should drop from a spoon.","Cover and rise until doubled and bubbly, about 45 minutes.","Heat the oil to medium. A spoon of batter should swell and colour slowly. If it browns at once, the oil is too hot and the centre stays raw.","Drop spoons of batter into the oil. Fry until deep gold all over, about 4 minutes, turning.","Drain. They should be light, sweet and nutmeg-scented, not greasy.","Eat warm. Puff puff does not keep its softness for long."]},
"nigerian|Suya": {"time":"About 30 minutes, plus marinating","steps":["Slice the beef sirloin thin. Mix the ground peanut with the yaji spice, ginger and garlic. Press this onto the meat. It should be a thick coat, not a dusting.","Thread onto the skewers.","Grill over hot coals or a hot grill, turning, until the peanut crust is toasted and the beef is just cooked. The spice burns if the flame licks it, so use coals, not a blaze.","Chop the onion.","Serve the suya hot, with the raw onion on the side."]},
"pakistani|Aloo Keema": {"time":"About 50 minutes","steps":["Dice the potato into small cubes and slice the onion and tomato. Heat the oil and fry the cumin seeds until they crackle.","Add the onion and cook until deep gold. Stir in the ginger paste and garlic paste and cook 1 minute. Add the minced beef and salt and fry, breaking the meat up, until it is brown and the liquid has cooked off.","Add turmeric, red chilli powder and the tomato. Cook until the tomato melts into the oil, about 6 minutes.","Add the potato and a cup of hot water. Cover and simmer until the potato is nearly tender, about 12 minutes. Stir once so the mince does not clump.","Add the peas and garam masala. Cook uncovered 5 minutes until the peas are bright and the gravy is thick enough to hold the mince, not a soup.","Finish with the coriander. Rest 5 minutes. Aloo keema should taste of browned mince and soft potato, with the peas still a little sweet."]},
"pakistani|Biryani": {"time":"About 1 hour 45 minutes","steps":["Soak the basmati rice 30 minutes. Joint the chicken.","Fry the sliced onion in the ghee until brown. Set half aside.","Brown the chicken in the same ghee. Add the yogurt and cook until the chicken is nearly done and the gravy is thick, about 25 minutes.","Parboil the rice in plenty of water until three-quarters cooked. Drain.","Steep the saffron in two spoons of warm water.","Layer chicken and rice in a heavy pot. Scatter the fried onion, the rest of the ghee and the saffron. Seal and cook on low for 20 minutes. Rest 10 minutes, then open."]},
"pakistani|Chapli Kebab": {"time":"About 45 minutes","steps":["Chop the tomato, onion, coriander and green chilli very fine. Squeeze the tomato and onion in a cloth so the mix is not wet. Wet mince will not hold a flat kebab.","Crush the cumin, coriander seeds and dried pomegranate seeds coarsely. The pomegranate is the sour note of a Peshawari chapli; do not grind it to dust.","Mix the minced beef with the chopped vegetables, the crushed spices, cornmeal, egg and salt. Knead for 2 minutes until it feels sticky. The cornmeal drinks the last of the moisture and gives the crust.","Divide into 8 portions. With wet hands, press each into a thin, wide patty, thinner in the middle than at the rim. A thick chapli steams instead of frying.","Heat the oil in a wide pan until a crumb of the mix sizzles at once. Fry the kebabs on medium, two at a time, without moving them until a crust forms, about 3 minutes. Flip once and cook through.","Drain on a rack, not stacked, so the crust stays crisp. The centre should be cooked and juicy, the edge lacy and brown. Eat hot."]},
"pakistani|Chicken Handi": {"time":"About 50 minutes","steps":["Joint the chicken, keeping the bone in. Whisk the yogurt with half the salt, the ginger paste and garlic paste. Coat the chicken and rest it 20 minutes.","Chop the onion and tomato. Melt the butter in a wide handi or heavy pan. Cook the onion until soft and pale gold. Add the chicken with its marinade and fry until the yogurt dries onto the meat and the pieces take a little colour, about 10 minutes.","Add the tomato and the slit green chillies. Cook on a lively flame, stirring, until the tomato breaks down and the butter separates, about 8 minutes. Do not add water yet.","Add a small cup of hot water, cover, and simmer until the chicken is just tender, about 15 minutes. The gravy should cling, not swim.","Stir in the cream and garam masala off a hard boil so the cream does not split. Taste the salt.","Rest 5 minutes. Scatter the chopped coriander. A handi is eaten from the pot, with the sauce thick enough to coat the chicken."]},
"pakistani|Gajar ka Halwa": {"time":"About 1 hour 15 minutes","steps":["Grate the carrots on the coarse side of a box grater. Fine shreds turn to mush; coarse ones keep a little chew.","Put the carrot and milk in a wide heavy pan. Bring to a simmer and cook, stirring often, until the milk has been drunk by the carrot and the mixture looks thick, about 35 to 40 minutes. Do not walk away near the end or it catches.","Add the sugar. It will loosen the halwa. Keep cooking until it thickens again and looks glossy, about 10 minutes.","Stir in the ghee and the seeds from the cardamom. Fry the halwa in the ghee for 5 minutes until it smells toasted and pulls from the side of the pan.","Crumble in the khoya and cook 3 minutes more. The khoya makes it rich without making it wet.","Chop the almonds and fold most of them through. Scatter the rest on top. Serve warm. Gajar ka halwa firms as it cools and is better the next day, warmed in a spoon of the ghee."]},
"pakistani|Haleem": {"time":"About 3 hours","steps":["Soak the cracked wheat, chana dal and masoor dal for 1 hour. Cut the beef small.","Brown the beef in the ghee. Cover with water and the drained grains. Simmer, stirring often, until the meat falls apart and the wheat has melted into a thick porridge, about 2 hours. Add hot water when it gets too stiff.","Mash or beat the haleem until it is almost smooth, with only a little texture left.","Fry the onions in a little more ghee until dark brown.","Serve the haleem in bowls with the fried onions piled on top."]},
"pakistani|Karahi": {"time":"About 45 minutes","steps":["Joint the chicken. Heat a wide karahi or pan until very hot. Add the chicken with no extra water and cook, stirring, until it releases its juices and they cook off.","Add the chopped tomato and the green chilli. Cook on high, mashing the tomato, until it becomes a thick paste and the chicken fat shows.","Add the chopped ginger and garlic. Fry until the raw smell is gone and the chicken is cooked through, about 10 minutes. A karahi should be dry enough to eat with naan, not a swimming curry.","Finish with more ginger cut into matchsticks and a last handful of green chilli. Serve straight from the pan."]},
"pakistani|Nihari": {"time":"About 4 to 5 hours","steps":["Cut the beef shank into pieces and keep the bone marrow with it. The marrow is served in the gravy.","Heat the ghee. Brown the shank well. Stir in the nihari masala and the chopped ginger and cook until the spices smell toasted, not raw.","Add hot water to cover by several centimetres. Bring to a boil, skim, then cook on the lowest heat for 3 to 4 hours until the meat leaves the bone.","Whisk the wheat flour with cold water until smooth. Pour it in while the gravy simmers and stir for 15 minutes until the nihari is glossy and thick.","Taste it. Serve with extra ginger on top, the softened marrow, and naan to swipe the bowl clean."]},
"pakistani|Pakora": {"time":"About 40 minutes","steps":["Slice the onion, potato and spinach. Squeeze the sliced onion so it is not watery.","Mix the besan and cold water to a thick batter that clings to a slice and slowly drops from a spoon. Too thin and the pakora is all crust.","Fold the vegetables through the batter.","Heat the oil for frying to medium. Drop spoonfuls in and do not crowd the pan. Fry until deep gold and crisp, turning once, about 4 minutes.","Drain. Serve at once with chutney. They soften if they sit."]},
"pakistani|Seekh Kebab": {"time":"About 45 minutes","steps":["Mince the onion very fine and squeeze out the water in your fist. Wet onion makes the seekh fall off the skewer.","Mix the minced lamb with the onion, ginger-garlic paste, chopped green chilli, cilantro and cumin. Knead the mixture against the bowl for 3 minutes until it turns sticky. That stickiness holds it on the skewer.","Rest 15 minutes. Wet your hands and press the meat in a sausage shape along metal skewers.","Grill over hot coals or under a very hot grill, turning often, until browned and just cooked through, about 8 to 10 minutes. Do not press them or the juices run out.","Slide off the skewers. Serve with onion, more cilantro, and naan."]},
"palestinian|Fattet Hummus": {"time":"About 40 minutes","steps":["Soak the chickpeas overnight and boil them until very soft, about 40 minutes. Keep some of the cooking water.","Toast or bake the pita until crisp, then break it into pieces.","Crush the garlic with the salt. Blend half the chickpeas with the tahini, lemon juice, garlic and enough cooking water to make a thick hummus.","Stir the rest of the garlic into the yogurt.","Fry the pine nuts in the olive oil until gold.","In a dish, lay the crisp bread, spoon over hot chickpeas and a little of their water so the bread softens but does not dissolve, then the hummus and the yogurt. Finish with pine nuts and parsley. Eat at once, while the bread still has some chew under the sauce."]},
"palestinian|Ka'ak": {"time":"About 1 hour 30 minutes","steps":["Mix the flour, yeast, sugar and salt. Add the olive oil, milk and about 180 ml of warm water. Knead 10 minutes to a smooth dough. Rise, covered, 1 hour.","Divide into 8 pieces. Roll each into a rope and join the ends to make an oval ring, fatter at the bottom edge.","Brush with a little water and press the top edge into sesame seeds.","Bake at 200°C until the rings are gold and the sesame smells toasted, about 18 minutes.","Cool on a rack. Ka'ak should be chewy, a little sweet-salty, with a firm sesame crust.","Eat the day it is baked, or split and warm it. It dries if it sits uncovered."]},
"palestinian|Knafeh": {"time":"About 1 hour","steps":["Shred the knafeh dough if it is in nests, and work the softened butter through it until every strand is coated.","Press half into a buttered tin. Crumble the Nabulsi cheese over it in an even layer. If the cheese is very salty, soak it in water first and drain. Cover with the rest of the dough and press flat.","Bake at 190°C until the top is deep gold, about 30 to 40 minutes.","Meanwhile boil the sugar with water and the rose water to a light syrup and cool it.","Pour the cool syrup over the hot knafeh. Rest 10 minutes. Invert or cut in the tin, and scatter the pistachios. The cheese should stretch, the pastry should crunch."]},
"palestinian|Maftoul": {"time":"About 1 hour","steps":["Soak the chickpeas overnight and boil them until tender.","Brown the chicken in the olive oil with the sliced onion. Add the chopped tomato, baharat, salt and water to cover. Simmer 30 minutes. Add the chickpeas for the last 10 minutes.","Lift the chicken out. In a steamer, or a colander over the simmering broth, steam the maftoul, fluffing it once, until the pearls are tender, about 20 minutes. Sprinkle a ladle of broth over them halfway so they do not dry.","The broth underneath should reduce to a thick tomato gravy.","Serve the maftoul with the chicken, chickpeas and gravy.","Maftoul is a pearl of wheat, larger than couscous, and should be separate and fragrant, not mush."]},
"palestinian|Maqluba": {"time":"About 1 hour 30 minutes","steps":["Soak the rice 20 minutes. Slice the eggplant and break the cauliflower into florets. Fry both in the oil until golden. Frying them first keeps them from turning to mush under the rice.","Brown the chicken with the sliced onion. Add the chopped tomato, baharat, salt and water to cover. Simmer 25 minutes. Lift the chicken out and reserve 700 ml of broth.","In a heavy pot, layer fried eggplant and cauliflower, then the chicken, then the drained rice. Pour the broth over. It should just cover the rice.","Bring to a boil, then cover and cook on low for 25 minutes. Rest 10 minutes off the heat.","Put a platter over the pot and flip it in one confident turn. Tap the pot and lift it. If the rice sticks, it needed another 5 minutes of rest.","Maqluba means upside-down: the vegetables and chicken should crown the rice, the grains separate and spiced."]},
"palestinian|Mujaddara": {"time":"About 50 minutes","steps":["Simmer the lentils until just tender. Drain and save the liquid.","Slice the onion and fry it in the olive oil until deep brown and crisp. Remove half for the top.","Add the cumin and rice to the onion left in the pan. Stir until the rice is glossy.","Add the lentils, salt and enough lentil water to sit 1 cm above the grains. Cover and cook on low for 15 minutes.","Rest 10 minutes and fluff.","Cover with the crisp onions. Mujaddara is lentil and rice, the onion both melted into the pot and shatter-crisp on top."]},
"palestinian|Musakhan": {"time":"About 1 hour 30 minutes","steps":["Bake or simmer the chicken until just cooked. Tear or joint it.","Fry the sliced onion slowly in the olive oil with the sumac until the onion is very soft, sweet and dark pink from the sumac, about 25 minutes. Do not rush this.","Heat the taboon bread. Lay it on a tray, soak it with the onion oil, and spread the onions over it.","Nestle the chicken on the onions. Scatter the pine nuts, toasted in a little of the olive oil.","Bake at 200°C for 10 minutes so the bread crisps at the edge and the chicken warms. Serve hot, with more sumac."]},
"palestinian|Qidreh": {"time":"About 2 hours","steps":["Soak the chickpeas overnight. Soak the rice 20 minutes.","Brown the lamb in the oil with the sliced onion. Add the whole garlic cloves, baharat, salt, chickpeas and water to cover. Simmer until the lamb and chickpeas are tender, about 1 hour 15 minutes.","Lift the lamb out. Measure enough broth to cook the rice, about 700 ml.","Put the drained rice and chickpeas in the pot with the broth and the garlic. Nestle the lamb on top. Cover and cook on low for 25 minutes.","Rest 10 minutes. The garlic should be soft enough to crush into the rice.","Qidreh is a Hebron lamb and rice pot, the chickpeas whole, the rice fragrant with baharat and garlic, not saucy."]},
"palestinian|Rummaniyya": {"time":"About 1 hour","steps":["Rinse and simmer the lentils until just soft, about 20 minutes. Keep the liquid.","Cube the eggplant, salt it for 10 minutes, pat dry, and fry in the olive oil until golden.","Fry the sliced onion in a spoon of the oil until soft. Add the garlic and cumin for 1 minute. Stir in the flour.","Add the lentils, their liquid, the pomegranate molasses and salt. Simmer until thick, about 10 minutes.","Fold in the eggplant gently so the cubes stay in pieces. The stew should be sour from the molasses, earthy from the lentils.","Rest 10 minutes. Rummaniyya is eaten warm, glossy, and distinctly sour-sweet, the eggplant soft."]},
"palestinian|Waraq Dawali": {"time":"About 1 hour 30 minutes","steps":["Rinse and blanch the grape leaves. Trim the stems.","Mix the uncooked rice with grated onion, chopped tomato, parsley, half the olive oil and the salt.","Roll small tight parcels, seam down.","Pack them in a pot. Add the rest of the oil, the lemon juice and water to cover. Weight with a plate.","Simmer 45 minutes, until the rice is cooked and the leaves are tender. Most of the water should cook away.","Cool until just warm. They should taste of lemon and olive oil, the rice plump inside the leaf."]},
"persian|Ash Reshteh": {"time":"About 1 hour 20 minutes","steps":["Soak the chickpeas and kidney beans overnight. Boil them with the lentils until everything is soft, about 40 minutes.","Slice the onion and fry half of it until deep brown. Set that aside for the top. Fry the rest until soft.","Chop the spinach, parsley and coriander. Add them to the soft onion and cook until the spinach wilts and the herbs darken a little, about 8 minutes.","Add the beans, lentils and their liquid, plus water to make a thick soup. Add the salt. Simmer 15 minutes.","Break in the noodles and cook until they are tender, about 10 minutes. The ash should be thick enough that a spoon stands for a moment. Stir in half the kashk.","Warm the rest of the kashk with the dried mint and a spoon of oil. Swirl it on each bowl and top with the fried onion. Ash reshteh is herbal, sour from the kashk, and heavy with noodles and beans."]},
"persian|Fesenjan": {"time":"About 1 hour 30 minutes","steps":["Toast the walnuts in a dry pan until they smell nutty, then grind them fine.","Cook the sliced onion in a dry pan until golden. Add the chicken thighs and brown them lightly.","Stir in the walnuts, the pomegranate molasses and enough water to make a sauce. Add the saffron, bloomed in a spoon of warm water.","Cover and simmer gently, stirring often so the walnuts do not catch, until the chicken is tender and the sauce is thick, dark and oily, about 1 hour.","Taste. It should be sour from the pomegranate and rich from the walnut, not sweet. Serve with rice."]},
"persian|Ghormeh Sabzi": {"time":"About 2 hours","steps":["Soak the kidney beans overnight. Chop the parsley, coriander and fenugreek very fine. The herbs are the body of this stew, not a garnish.","Heat half the oil and fry the herbs on medium, stirring often, until they darken and smell sweet, about 15 minutes. Pale herbs make a grassy stew. Set them aside.","In the rest of the oil, fry the sliced onion until gold. Add the turmeric and the lamb and brown the meat.","Add the beans, the fried herbs, the pierced dried limes and salt. Cover with water. Simmer until the lamb and beans are tender, about 1 hour 30 minutes.","The dried limes should be soft. Press them against the pot so their sourness goes into the gravy. The stew should be thick, dark green, and sour-savoury.","Rest 15 minutes. Ghormeh sabzi tastes better after sitting, when the herbs and lime have married. Do not let it go dry; it is a stew, with enough sauce to spoon."]},
"persian|Kebab Koobideh": {"time":"About 45 minutes","steps":["Grate the onion and squeeze it dry. Mix it with the ground lamb, ground beef, a pinch of the saffron bloomed in warm water.","Knead the meat for several minutes until it is pasty and sticks to your hand. Rest it 15 minutes.","Wet your hands and mould the meat around wide flat skewers in long, ridged shapes. If it slips, the mixture needs more kneading, not more onion.","Grill over very hot coals, turning carefully, until browned and just cooked.","Slide onto the flatbread. Dust with sumac. Serve at once."]},
"persian|Khoresh Bademjan": {"time":"About 1 hour 15 minutes","steps":["Cut the eggplant into thick wedges. Salt them for 15 minutes, pat dry, and fry in the oil until golden and soft. Drain. Fried eggplant holds its shape in the stew; raw eggplant drinks all the oil and collapses.","Slice the onion and fry it in a spoon of the oil until gold. Add the turmeric and lamb and brown the meat. Add the chopped tomato and salt and cook until the tomato breaks down.","Add water to barely cover and simmer until the lamb is nearly tender, about 40 minutes.","Lay the eggplant on top. Simmer gently 15 minutes so it drinks the sauce without breaking apart. Do not stir hard.","Add the lemon juice. The stew should be sour-savoury, with enough sauce to spoon, the eggplant silky.","Rest 10 minutes. Khoresh bademjan is an eggplant and lamb stew, the eggplant the equal of the meat, not a side."]},
"persian|Salad Shirazi": {"time":"About 15 minutes","steps":["Dice the cucumber, tomato and onion small and even, about the size of a corn kernel. Shirazi is a fine dice, not a chunky salad.","If the onion is harsh, rinse it under cold water and squeeze it dry.","Toss with the salt and leave 5 minutes. Pour off any watery juice so the dressing is not diluted.","Add the lemon juice, olive oil and crushed dried mint.","Taste. It should be bright, oniony and minty, with the vegetables still crisp.","Serve soon, cold. It wilts if it waits more than an hour."]},
"persian|Sholeh Zard": {"time":"About 1 hour, plus chilling","steps":["Rinse the rice and simmer it in 1 litre of water until the grains burst and the mixture is a loose porridge, about 35 minutes. Stir so it does not catch.","Bloom the saffron in 2 spoons of warm water.","Add the sugar and butter and cook, stirring, until the sugar dissolves and the porridge thickens enough to hold a soft shape, about 10 minutes.","Stir in the saffron and rose water. The colour should be deep gold. If it looks pale, the saffron was old.","Pour into a dish. Chop the almonds and scatter them with the cinnamon.","Cool, then chill. Sholeh zard is a saffron rice pudding, eaten cold, fragrant with rose, the rice soft but not gluey."]},
"persian|Tahchin": {"time":"About 1 hour 30 minutes","steps":["Soak the rice 30 minutes. Bloom the saffron in 3 spoons of warm water.","Fry the sliced onion in the oil, add the chicken and half the salt, and cook with a splash of water until the chicken is just tender. Shred the meat.","Parboil the rice in salted water for 6 minutes, until half cooked. Drain.","Whisk the yogurt with the eggs, the saffron water and the rest of the salt. Fold this through half the rice. This yellow rice becomes the crust.","Melt the butter in an oven dish. Press the yellow rice into an even layer on the bottom and up the sides. Mix the chicken with the remaining rice and fill the centre. Cover.","Bake at 190°C for 45 minutes, then uncover for 10 minutes. Rest 10 minutes and invert. The bottom crust, the tahdig, should be gold and crisp, the inside saffron-yellow and studded with chicken."]},
"persian|Tahdig": {"time":"About 1 hour 15 minutes","steps":["Wash and soak the basmati rice for 30 minutes. Parboil it in plenty of water for 5 to 6 minutes, until the outside of the grain is soft and the inside is still firm. Drain.","Bloom the saffron in two spoons of warm water.","Mix a few spoons of the rice with the yogurt, a little of the melted butter and most of the saffron.","Heat the rest of the butter in a heavy pot. Spread the yogurt-rice in an even layer. This becomes the tahdig, the crust. Pile the rest of the rice on top in a cone. Poke a few holes with the handle of a spoon.","Cover the lid with a cloth, put it on, and cook on medium for 8 minutes, then on the lowest heat for 35 to 40 minutes.","Rest 5 minutes. Scoop out the loose rice, then loosen the crust with a spatula and lift it out in shards. Serve the crust on top. It should be gold and crisp."]},
"persian|Zereshk Polo": {"time":"About 1 hour","steps":["Soak the rice 30 minutes. Soak the barberries in warm water for 10 minutes and drain. They are tart; a quick soak plumps them without washing the colour away.","Slice the onion and fry it in the oil until gold. Add the chicken and half the salt and brown it. Add a cup of water, cover, and simmer until the chicken is just tender, about 25 minutes. Keep the juices.","Bloom the saffron in 2 spoons of warm water.","Parboil the rice in plenty of water with the rest of the salt until the outside of the grain is soft and the inside still firm, about 6 minutes. Drain.","Melt the butter in a pot, add the rice, and drizzle with the saffron water and a few spoons of the chicken juices. Cover with a cloth under the lid and steam on low for 20 minutes.","Fry the barberries in a spoon of the butter with the sugar for 1 minute, until they shine. Serve the rice with the chicken and the barberries scattered on top. The berries should pop, sour against the saffron rice."]},
"peruvian|Aji de Gallina": {"time":"About 50 minutes","steps":["Simmer the chicken until tender, then shred it. Keep a cup of broth. Soak the bread in the evaporated milk.","Slice the onion and cook it in the oil until soft and sweet, about 10 minutes. Add the garlic and aji amarillo and fry 2 minutes. The aji is the yellow heat of the dish.","Blend the onion mixture with the soaked bread, walnuts and a splash of broth until smooth.","Return the sauce to the pan, add the shredded chicken and salt, and simmer until thick enough to coat the meat, about 10 minutes. Stir in the parmesan.","Boil the eggs and halve them.","Serve the chicken in its yellow sauce with the eggs. It should be creamy, gently hot, and nutty, not a thin soup."]},
"peruvian|Anticuchos": {"time":"About 30 minutes, plus marinating","steps":["Trim the heart of sinew and cut it into cubes. Heart is lean; large cubes dry out, so keep them bite-sized.","Mix the aji panca, crushed garlic, cumin, vinegar, salt and oil. Coat the meat and rest it at least 2 hours.","Thread onto skewers.","Grill or pan-fry on high, turning, until charred outside and still rosy inside, about 3 minutes a side. Well-done heart is tough.","Brush once with the leftover marinade in the last minute, boiled first if it sat on raw meat.","Rest 3 minutes. Anticuchos should be spicy, tangy and smoky, the meat tender."]},
"peruvian|Arroz con Pollo": {"time":"About 50 minutes","steps":["Blend the coriander with a splash of water to a green liquid. This colours the rice.","Brown the chicken in the oil. Add the sliced onion and garlic and cook until soft. Stir in the aji amarillo.","Add the beer and let it reduce by half. Add the coriander liquid, salt and the rice. Stir until the rice looks green and glossy.","Add water to sit just above the rice. Cover and cook on low until the rice is done and the chicken is tender, about 20 minutes.","Fold in the peas for the last 5 minutes.","Rest 10 minutes. The rice should be green, separate and savoury, the chicken moist, the aji a gentle heat."]},
"peruvian|Causa": {"time":"About 40 minutes","steps":["Boil the potato until soft, drain, and mash it while hot until smooth. Cool slightly.","Beat in the aji amarillo, the juice of the lime, the oil and salt. The potato should be yellow, seasoned and stiff enough to mould. Taste it. Causa is the potato, not only the filling.","Mix the tuna with the mayonnaise and a pinch of salt. Slice the avocado. Boil and slice the eggs.","Press a layer of potato into a mould or onto a plate, add tuna and avocado, then another layer of potato.","Unmould and lay the egg on top.","Serve cold or just cool. The potato should hold its shape and taste of lime and aji."]},
"peruvian|Ceviche": {"time":"About 20 minutes, plus chilling the fish","steps":["Cut the sea bass into bite-sized pieces, removing any bloodline. Keep it ice cold. Ceviche is only as good as the freshness of the fish.","Toss it with the lime juice and the thinly sliced red onion and aji limo. The lime should just cover the fish.","Leave it for a few minutes only, until the outside turns opaque and the centre is still tender. Longer and it turns chalky.","Fold in the cilantro. Taste for lime.","Serve at once with the sweet potato and choclo, both cooked and cooled. Spoon over some of the lime juice, the leche de tigre."]},
"peruvian|Lomo Saltado": {"time":"About 30 minutes","steps":["Cut the beef sirloin into strips. Cut the red onion into thick wedges and the tomato into chunks. Fry the French fries and keep them crisp.","Heat a wok or pan until it smokes. Sear the beef in batches so it browns, not stews. Set it aside.","In the same pan, char the onion. Add the tomato and aji amarillo and toss for a minute. The tomato should soften at the edge and stay in pieces.","Return the beef. Splash in the soy sauce and toss. Fold in the fries and cilantro at the last moment so the fries stay crisp.","Serve at once, often with rice beside it. The soy and the chilli should both be clear."]},
"peruvian|Papa a la Huancaina": {"time":"About 35 minutes","steps":["Boil the potato in its skin until tender. Peel and halve it while warm. Boil the eggs.","Blend the aji amarillo, cheese, evaporated milk, cracker, garlic, oil and salt until the sauce is smooth and thick enough to coat a spoon. If it is stiff, add a spoon more milk.","The sauce should be salty, cheesy and hot. Fry it in a pan for 3 minutes if you want it silkier, stirring.","Lay the warm potato on a plate and spoon the cold or warm sauce over it so it drapes.","Add halved eggs and olives.","Huancaína is a potato in cheese-chilli sauce, rich and yellow, eaten soon so the potato is still soft."]},
"peruvian|Picarones": {"time":"About 45 minutes","steps":["Boil the sweet potato and pumpkin until soft. Mash them together and cool until warm.","Dissolve the yeast in a spoon of warm water. Mix it into the mash with the flour and salt to a soft, sticky dough. Rise 30 minutes.","Boil the sugar with 80 ml of water, the cinnamon and orange peel until it is a light syrup. Keep it warm.","Heat the oil to medium. With wet hands, shape rings of dough and fry until puffed and deep gold, about 3 minutes, turning once.","Drain. The inside should be soft and sweet from the squash, the outside crisp.","Serve at once, dipped in the warm syrup. They soften if they wait."]},
"peruvian|Pollo a la Brasa": {"time":"About 1 hour 15 minutes, plus marinating","steps":["Spatchcock the chicken or leave it whole. Crush the garlic with the salt and mix with soy sauce, cumin, paprika, pepper, lime juice and oil.","Rub the chicken under the skin as well as over it. Rest at least 4 hours.","Roast at 200°C, breast up, until the skin is deep brown and the thigh juices run clear, about 50 minutes. Baste once with the drippings.","Rest 10 minutes. The soy and spices should have made a dark, savoury crust, not a burnt one.","Chop into pieces, keeping the skin on.","Pollo a la brasa is rotisserie chicken; in an oven the skin still needs to be crisp and the meat juicy."]},
"peruvian|Rocoto Relleno": {"time":"About 1 hour","steps":["Cut the tops off the rocotos and scrape out the seeds and veins, which hold much of the heat. Boil the peppers in salted water for 5 minutes, drain, and repeat once if they are fiercely hot. Save the tops.","Fry the onion and garlic in the oil, add the beef and salt, and cook until brown and dry. Chop the peanuts and stir them in. Cool slightly, then mix in one egg.","Stuff the peppers. Put a slice of cheese on the filling and replace the tops.","Set them in a snug dish. Beat the remaining egg and pour it around them.","Bake at 180°C until the egg is set and the cheese has melted, about 25 minutes.","The pepper should be tender, the filling moist, the heat still present. Rocoto is hotter than a bell pepper; the boiling tames it without making it mild."]},
"russian|Beef Stroganoff": {"time":"About 35 minutes","steps":["Slice the beef into strips across the grain. Pat it dry and salt it. Wet beef will not brown.","Heat half the butter until it foams and sear the beef in batches, about 1 minute. It should stay pink inside. Set it aside.","Add the rest of the butter and cook the sliced onion and mushroom until the mushroom has given up its water and started to brown, about 8 minutes.","Stir in the mustard and sour cream off a hard boil so the cream does not split. Add pepper and taste the salt.","Return the beef and its juices just to warm through, 1 minute. Longer and the beef turns grey and tough.","The sauce should be glossy and loose enough to coat the meat, tasting of mushroom and sour cream, not of flour."]},
"russian|Blini": {"time":"About 40 minutes","steps":["Whisk the flour, eggs, sugar and salt. Add the milk until the batter is thin, like cream. Rest 20 minutes.","Stir in half the melted butter.","Heat a small pan and wipe it with butter. Pour a small ladle and swirl a thin round.","Cook until the top looks dry and the underside is spotted, then flip for a few seconds. Stack them under a cloth.","Blini should be thin and tender, a little crisp at the edge, not thick pancakes.","Serve warm with the rest of the butter and the sour cream."]},
"russian|Borscht": {"time":"About 2 hours","steps":["Cover the beef with water and simmer until tender, about 1 hour 30 minutes. Lift the meat out and cut it into pieces. Keep the broth.","Cook the chopped onion and carrot in a dry pan. Add the tomato paste and the beet, cut into sticks. Stew with a splash of the broth until the beet softens and turns the fat red, about 15 minutes.","Add the beet mixture, shredded cabbage and diced potato to the broth. Simmer until the potato is tender, about 20 minutes.","Return the beef. Taste it. The soup should be a clear deep red, sweet from the beet and savoury from the beef.","Serve with a spoon of sour cream and the dill. It is better the next day."]},
"russian|Golubtsy": {"time":"About 1 hour 30 minutes","steps":["Core the cabbage and boil it until the outer leaves are pliable. Peel them off carefully. Trim the thick rib so the leaf rolls.","Half-cook the rice. Mix it with the beef, chopped onion, salt and pepper.","Place a spoon of filling on each leaf and roll, tucking the sides.","Fry the rolls lightly in the oil so the outside takes a little colour.","Pack them in a pot, pour over the tomato sauce and a splash of water, cover, and simmer 45 minutes, until the rice inside is cooked and the cabbage is silky.","The sauce should thicken around the rolls. Rest 10 minutes. Golubtsy are cabbage parcels, tender, the filling savoury and not raw in the centre."]},
"russian|Medovik": {"time":"About 1 hour 20 minutes, plus resting","steps":["Warm the honey, half the sugar and the butter until the sugar dissolves. Take it off the heat, stir in the baking soda, and watch it foam and lighten.","Beat in the eggs, then the flour, to a soft dough. Rest 20 minutes.","Divide into 8. Roll each very thin and bake at 180°C until gold, about 5 minutes a layer. They firm as they cool. Trim them to match.","Whip the sour cream with the rest of the sugar until spreadable. Do not turn it into butter.","Layer cream between the cakes and over the top. Crush the trimmings and press them onto the sides.","Rest in the fridge at least 6 hours, overnight if you can. Medovik is dry when first built and becomes tender only after the sour cream soaks the honey layers."]},
"russian|Olivier Salad": {"time":"About 40 minutes","steps":["Boil the potato and carrot until tender, and the eggs for 9 minutes. Cool them completely. Warm vegetables make the mayonnaise oily.","Dice the potato, carrot, egg, pickle and bologna small and even.","Fold in the peas.","Add the mayonnaise and salt and mix gently so the dice stay distinct.","Chill at least 1 hour. Olivier tastes better cold, when the pickle and potato have swapped flavours.","It should be creamy but not swimming in mayonnaise, salty from the pickle and bologna."]},
"russian|Pelmeni": {"time":"About 1 hour 45 minutes","steps":["Mix the flour, egg and cold water to a firm dough. Knead until smooth. Rest 30 minutes.","Mix the ground beef, ground pork, finely chopped onion and garlic. The onion can be grated; squeeze it if it is watery.","Roll the dough thin. Cut small rounds, fill with a little meat, and fold into half-moons, then bring the two corners together to make the classic shape.","Boil them in plenty of water until they float and the dough is cooked, about 6 to 8 minutes. Do not crowd the pot.","Serve with sour cream and black pepper. A spoon of the boiling broth in the bowl keeps them hot."]},
"russian|Pirozhki": {"time":"About 1 hour 30 minutes","steps":["Warm the milk and dissolve the yeast. Mix with the flour, egg, a pinch of salt and the butter. Knead 8 minutes. Rise 1 hour.","Fry the chopped onion in a spoon of butter, add the beef and the rest of the salt, and cook until the meat is brown and dry. Cool it.","Divide the dough into 12. Flatten each, fill with beef, and pinch into an oval, seam down.","Rise 20 minutes. Brush with a little milk.","Bake at 190°C until gold, about 20 minutes.","The dough should be soft, the filling moist and fully cooked. Eat warm."]},
"russian|Shchi": {"time":"About 1 hour 15 minutes","steps":["Cover the beef with water, add the salt, and simmer 40 minutes. Skim.","Dice the potato, shred the cabbage, and slice the onion and carrot.","Add the potato and bay leaf to the broth. After 10 minutes add the cabbage, onion, carrot and chopped tomato.","Simmer until the cabbage is tender and the potato is soft, about 20 minutes. The broth should taste of beef and sweet cabbage.","Taste the salt. Shchi is a cabbage soup, light, not a thick stew.","Finish with dill. Serve the beef in the bowl with the vegetables."]},
"russian|Syrniki": {"time":"About 30 minutes","steps":["If the cheese is wet, drain it. Mix it with the egg, sugar, salt and most of the flour. The dough should be soft but hold a patty. Too much flour makes a pancake, not a syrnik.","Shape thick rounds and dust them with the rest of the flour.","Melt the butter on medium. Fry the cakes until both sides are deep gold and the centre is hot, about 3 minutes a side. Low heat dries them out before they colour.","They should be creamy inside, not rubbery.","Serve hot with sour cream.","Eat them the hour they are fried. They toughen as they cool."]},
"singaporean|Bak Kut Teh": {"time":"About 1 hour 40 minutes","steps":["Blanch the pork ribs in boiling water for 3 minutes, then drain and rinse. This keeps the broth clear.","Put the ribs in a clean pot with the whole garlic cloves, cracked white pepper, star anise, cinnamon, soy sauce and salt. Cover with water by 4 cm.","Bring to a boil, skim, then cover and simmer on the lowest heat for 1 hour 20 minutes, until the meat eases off the bone and the pepper smells loud.","Taste. Bak kut teh should be peppery first, then savoury from the pork and soy, with garlic sweetness. Add pepper if it is shy.","The broth stays light brown, not a dark stew. Do not let it boil hard or it turns cloudy.","Serve the ribs in deep bowls with the broth and scallion. The garlic cloves are soft enough to eat."]},
"singaporean|Chai Tow Kway": {"time":"About 30 minutes, plus setting","steps":["Grate the radish. Whisk the rice flour with 500 ml of water and the salt. Stir in the radish.","Cook the batter, stirring, until it is a thick porridge. Pour it into a tray about 2 cm deep and steam, or chill, until it sets firm, about 20 minutes. Cool and cut into cubes. This cake is the 'carrot' of carrot cake, though it is radish.","Heat the oil until very hot. Fry the cubes in one layer until the sides are gold and crisp. Pale cubes taste floury.","Add the garlic, then the eggs, and scramble them through the cubes.","Add the sweet soy, bean sprouts and chives. Toss until the sprouts just wilt and the cubes are dark at the edges.","Serve hot. Chai tow kway should be crisp outside, soft inside, sweet-salty, and smell of garlic and the hot pan."]},
"singaporean|Chicken Rice": {"time":"About 1 hour 30 minutes","steps":["Poach the whole chicken in water with a few slices of the ginger, keeping the water just under a boil, until the juices at the thigh run clear, about 40 minutes. Lift it out and plunge it into ice water so the skin stays silky. Rub the skin with a little of the rendered chicken fat.","Skim the fat off the poaching broth. Cook the jasmine rice in that broth with the chicken fat, the rest of the ginger, the garlic and the knotted pandan leaves, using less liquid than you would with plain water because the broth is rich.","Chop the cooled chicken.","Serve the rice, the chicken, the cucumber and the chilli sauce, with a small bowl of the poaching broth on the side.","The rice should taste of chicken and ginger even before the sauce."]},
"singaporean|Chili Crab": {"time":"About 40 minutes","steps":["Clean the mud crab and crack the claws so the sauce can get in. Cut the body into pieces.","Fry the garlic and ginger until fragrant. Add the sambal and tomato ketchup and bubble until the sauce turns a deep red and the coconut fat separates.","Add the chicken stock and the crab, largest pieces first. Cover and cook until the shells are bright red and the meat is just cooked, about 8 minutes.","Beat the egg and stream it in, stirring, so it sets in soft ribbons through the sauce.","Serve with the fried mantou for soaking up the sauce. Eat with your hands."]},
"singaporean|Fish Head Curry": {"time":"About 45 minutes","steps":["Ask for the fish head cut in half so it fits the pot. Rub it with salt. Cut the okra and eggplant into chunks.","Heat the oil and fry the garlic, ginger and curry powder until the oil is fragrant and red, about 3 minutes. Add a spoon of water if the powder threatens to burn.","Add the tomato and tamarind and cook until the tomato softens. Pour in the coconut milk and 250 ml of water. Simmer 8 minutes.","Add the eggplant and okra. When they are nearly tender, slide in the fish head. Spoon the gravy over it. Simmer gently until the fish is just cooked, about 10 minutes. A hard boil breaks the cheeks.","Taste the salt and the sourness. The curry should be tangy, coconut-rich and hot, thin enough to spoon, not a dry masala.","Rest 5 minutes. The prize is the cheek and the gelatin in the head, so serve those pieces first."]},
"singaporean|Hokkien Mee": {"time":"About 30 minutes","steps":["Slice the pork belly and squid. Shell the shrimp and simmer the shells in 400 ml of water for 10 minutes. Strain. This quick stock is the gravy.","Soften both noodles in hot water if they are dried, then drain.","Heat the oil and fry the garlic and pork until the fat renders. Add the shrimp and squid and toss until just cooked. Lift them out.","Add both noodles to the wok with the soy sauce, salt and the shrimp stock. Toss until the noodles drink the stock and look glossy, about 3 minutes. There should be a little gravy, not a dry fry and not a soup.","Crack in the eggs and toss until they just set in streaks. Return the seafood and pork. Fold in the chives.","Serve at once. Hokkien mee tastes of prawn stock and pork fat, with both yellow and white noodles in the same bite."]},
"singaporean|Katong Laksa": {"time":"About 40 minutes","steps":["Cook the rice noodles a minute less than the packet says, rinse, and cut them short with scissors so they can be eaten with a spoon. Katong laksa is a spoon dish.","Slice the fish cake. Fry the laksa paste in the oil until the fat separates and the paste smells sweet, about 4 minutes.","Add the shrimp and coconut milk with 200 ml of water. Simmer gently until the shrimp are just pink, about 5 minutes. Stir in the fish sauce.","Add the fish cake to warm through. Do not boil hard.","Put noodles and bean sprouts in bowls and ladle the gravy over them. It should be thick enough to cling to the short noodles.","Top with torn laksa leaf and lime. The leaf is the perfume; without it the bowl is only coconut and chilli."]},
"singaporean|Kaya Toast": {"time":"About 25 minutes","steps":["Whisk the eggs with the coconut milk, palm sugar, salt and the torn pandan. Cook in a bowl set over simmering water, stirring, until the custard thickens enough to hold a ribbon, about 12 minutes. Do not let the water touch the bowl or the eggs scramble.","Fish out the pandan. Cool the kaya. It thickens more as it cools and should be spreadable, not runny.","Toast the bread until the edges are deep gold and the centre is still soft.","Spread cold butter on the hot toast so it melts in, then a thick layer of kaya.","Close into sandwiches and cut them. The contrast is the dish: cold salted butter, warm sweet coconut custard, crisp bread.","Eat at once. Kaya made this way keeps covered in the fridge and is better the next day, stirred once."]},
"singaporean|Popiah": {"time":"About 45 minutes","steps":["Shred the jicama and carrot. Fry the garlic in the oil, add the shrimp until pink, then the jicama and carrot. Season with salt and a spoon of sweet soy. Cover and cook until the jicama is sweet and translucent and its water has cooked off, about 15 minutes. A wet filling makes a soggy popiah. Cool it.","Make thin omelettes with the eggs and slice them into strips. Blanch the bean sprouts for 10 seconds.","Warm the wrappers so they bend without cracking.","Lay a wrapper flat, spread a thin streak of the remaining sweet soy, then a line of jicama, egg and sprouts.","Fold the sides in and roll tightly. The filling should be a firm cylinder, not a loose parcel.","Cut in half. Popiah is eaten fresh, the wrapper soft, the jicama still a little crunchy and sweet."]},
"singaporean|Satay": {"time":"About 35 minutes, plus marinating","steps":["Cut the chicken into strips. Pound the lemongrass with the turmeric, half the sugar, the salt and oil. Coat the chicken and rest it 1 hour.","Thread onto skewers.","Simmer the peanut butter with the tamarind, chopped chilli, the rest of the sugar and 100 ml of water until thick and sour-sweet.","Grill or pan-fry the skewers until charred in spots and cooked through, about 3 minutes a side.","Rest 3 minutes so the juices settle.","Serve with the warm peanut sauce. Singapore satay is eaten off the stick, the sauce thick enough to cling."]},
"south-african|Biltong": {"time":"About 30 minutes, plus 4 days drying","steps":["Cut the beef with the grain into strips about 2 cm thick. Trim excess fat if you want it to dry faster; a little fat is traditional.","Toast the coriander seeds and crush them coarsely with the pepper.","Mix the salt, sugar, coriander and pepper. Rub the vinegar over the meat, then the spice. The vinegar is the cure; do not skip it.","Layer the strips and rest them covered in the fridge for 12 hours. Turn them once.","Hang the strips in a cool, airy place out of direct sun, with space between them, until they are dry at the edge and still slightly pink and yielding in the centre, about 4 days. Thicker strips take longer.","Biltong is cured dried beef, coriander-scented, sliced thin across the grain. If it smells sour or feels slimy, it has not dried safely; discard it."]},
"south-african|Bobotie": {"time":"About 1 hour 15 minutes","steps":["Soak the bread in the milk, then mash it.","Fry the chopped onion until soft. Add the minced beef and cook until browned. Stir in the curry powder and turmeric and fry 1 minute.","Add the apricot jam, raisins, almonds, then the soaked bread. The mixture should be moist and sweet-spiced. Spread it in a baking dish.","Beat the egg with a little of the remaining milk. Pour it over the meat.","Bake at 180°C until the custard is set and golden, about 35 minutes.","Rest 10 minutes. The top should be a soft yellow custard over a curried mince."]},
"south-african|Braai": {"time":"About 1 hour","steps":["Take the meat out of the fridge early so it is not ice-cold. Pat the beef steak and lamb chops dry. Prick the boerewors once or twice so it does not burst.","Light the coals and wait until they are grey, not flaming. A braai is slow.","Put the chicken on first, skin side down, away from the hottest coals. Add the boerewors, coiled, and turn it with tongs, not a fork.","Steak and lamb chops go on last, over the hotter part, until the fat renders and the inside is the way you like it. Rest the steak.","Serve everything off the grill as it is ready, with bread or pap. The sausage should be browned and juicy, not split and dry."]},
"south-african|Bunny Chow": {"time":"About 1 hour","steps":["Cut the lamb into cubes. Heat the oil and brown it with the sliced onion until the onion is gold.","Add the garlic, ginger and curry powder and fry 1 minute, until the spice smells toasted.","Add the tomato and salt and cook until the tomato breaks down. Add the potato in chunks and water to barely cover. Simmer until the lamb and potato are tender and the gravy is thick, about 40 minutes.","The gravy must be thick enough to sit in bread without turning it to mush at once.","Cut the loaf into quarters and hollow each quarter, keeping the crumb. Fill the hollow with curry.","Set the crumb on top. Bunny chow is Durban curry served in a bread loaf, eaten with the hands."]},
"south-african|Chakalaka": {"time":"About 30 minutes","steps":["Grate the carrot and dice the onion and pepper.","Heat the oil and cook the onion until soft. Add the curry powder and chilli and fry 30 seconds.","Add the carrot, pepper and tomato. Cook until the vegetables soften and the tomato thickens, about 10 minutes.","Stir in the baked beans and salt. Simmer 5 minutes so the beans take the spice. Do not mash them.","Taste. It should be spicy, tomato-sweet and still have texture.","Chakalaka is a relish, good hot or at room temperature beside grilled meat."]},
"south-african|Koeksisters": {"time":"About 1 hour, plus chilling","steps":["Rub the butter into the flour, baking powder and salt. Add cold water, about 100 ml, to a firm dough. Rest 20 minutes.","Roll to 5 mm and cut strips. Plait three strips together and pinch the ends.","Simmer the sugar with 200 ml of water, the lemon juice and ginger until it is a syrup. Chill the syrup. Cold syrup on hot dough is what makes a koeksister soak without going soggy at once.","Fry the plaits in medium oil until deep gold and cooked through, about 4 minutes.","Dip each hot plait straight into the cold syrup for 20 seconds, then set it on a rack.","They should be crisp-edged, syrup-soaked and ginger-scented. Let the syrup set before stacking."]},
"south-african|Malva Pudding": {"time":"About 50 minutes","steps":["Cream 40 g of the butter with 80 g of the sugar. Beat in the egg and the apricot jam.","Stir the baking soda into the milk and vinegar; it will froth. Fold the flour and the milk into the batter.","Pour into a buttered dish and bake at 180°C until risen and a skewer comes out clean, about 30 minutes.","Heat the remaining butter, sugar, cream and 60 ml of water until the sugar dissolves. Pour this sauce over the hot pudding so it soaks in.","Rest 10 minutes. The centre should be spongy and soaked, the edges caramelised.","Malva is a Cape pudding, sweet with apricot, served warm in its sauce."]},
"south-african|Pap": {"time":"About 30 minutes","steps":["Bring 700 ml of water to a boil with the salt.","Rain in the maize meal while whisking so lumps do not form. Lower the heat.","Cook, stirring often, until it thickens and pulls from the sides, about 20 minutes. For a soft porridge, stop while it is still spoonable. For stiff pap, cook longer and beat it until it holds a shape.","Stir in the butter.","If it sticks and scorches, move it to a clean pot and add a splash of water; do not scrape the burnt bottom in.","Pap should taste of toasted maize, smooth if you whisked well, hot and mild."]},
"south-african|Potjiekos": {"time":"About 2 hours","steps":["Cut the beef into large pieces. Heat the oil in a heavy pot and brown the meat. Set it aside.","Cook the sliced onion in the same pot until gold. Add the garlic and thyme.","Return the beef. Add the tomato, stock and salt. Cover and simmer 1 hour, until the beef is nearly tender. Do not stir; a potjie is layered and left.","Add the carrot, then the potato on top. Cover and cook until the vegetables are tender, about 40 minutes. The steam cooks the top layer.","Taste the salt. The gravy should be rich and not watery.","Serve from the pot, the meat at the bottom, the vegetables on top, each still recognisable."]},
"south-african|Vetkoek": {"time":"About 40 minutes, plus rising","steps":["Dissolve the yeast and sugar in 180 ml of warm water. Stir in the flour and salt to a soft dough. Knead 6 minutes.","Rise until doubled, about 45 minutes.","Divide into 8 and shape into balls. Flatten slightly.","Heat the oil to medium. Fry until both sides are deep gold and the centre is cooked, about 4 minutes. A pale, doughy middle means the oil was too hot.","Drain.","Vetkoek should be puffed and tender. Split it while warm. It is a fried bread, plain and yeasted."]},
"spanish|Churros": {"time":"About 35 minutes","steps":["Bring 250 ml of water to a boil with the butter, a spoon of the sugar and the salt. Tip in the flour and stir hard until the dough comes away from the pan and looks smooth.","Cool until you can handle it. Put it in a piping bag with a star nozzle. The ridges are what turn crisp.","Heat the oil to medium. Pipe lengths of dough and snip them into the oil. Do not crowd.","Fry until deep gold and crisp outside, about 3 minutes, turning once. Pale churros are doughy.","Drain and toss at once with the rest of the sugar mixed with the cinnamon.","Eat hot. They soften as they cool and do not revive well."]},
"spanish|Crema Catalana": {"time":"About 40 minutes, plus chilling","steps":["Heat the milk with the lemon peel and cinnamon until steaming. Let it sit 10 minutes, then remove the peel and cinnamon.","Whisk the yolks with half the sugar and the cornstarch until smooth.","Pour in the hot milk, whisking. Return to the pan and cook on low, stirring, until the custard thickens and bubbles once. The cornstarch lets it boil briefly without scrambling; still do not walk away.","Pour into shallow dishes and chill at least 4 hours, until set.","Sprinkle the rest of the sugar over the surface and caramelise it with a hot iron or under a very hot grill until the sugar is a hard amber sheet.","Serve within the hour, so the crust stays brittle and the custard cold. It should taste of lemon peel and cinnamon, not of vanilla."]},
"spanish|Fabada": {"time":"About 2 hours","steps":["Soak the beans overnight. Drain. Cover with fresh water, add the onion and garlic, and simmer gently. Do not salt yet or the skins toughen.","After 45 minutes, add the pork belly and chorizo. Keep the water at a tremble so the beans do not break.","When the beans are almost tender, add the morcilla, paprika, saffron and salt. Cook 20 minutes more.","The broth should be thick from the bean starch, stained orange by the paprika and chorizo.","If it is thin, mash a few beans into the liquid.","Rest 10 minutes. Fabada is a bean pot with smoked sausage, rich and filling, the beans whole and creamy."]},
"spanish|Gambas al Ajillo": {"time":"About 15 minutes","steps":["Pat the shrimp dry and salt them. Slice the garlic thin. A wet shrimp steams and does not take the oil.","Warm the olive oil with the garlic and the dried chilli on medium until the garlic is pale gold and fragrant. Do not let it go brown or the dish turns bitter.","Add the shrimp in one layer. Cook until they just turn pink and curl, about 2 minutes, turning once.","Take the pan off the heat. The shrimp should be juicy, not tight little balls. Overcooked shrimp are the usual failure.","Scatter parsley and a squeeze of lemon.","Serve in the pan, with bread for the oil. The oil is garlic-hot and part of the dish, not something to drain away."]},
"spanish|Gazpacho": {"time":"About 20 minutes, plus chilling","steps":["Use ripe tomatoes. Chop them with the cucumber, pepper and garlic. Soak the bread in a little water and squeeze it.","Blend everything with the olive oil, vinegar and salt until completely smooth. A grainy gazpacho needs more blending, or a pass through a sieve.","Taste. It should be bright, a little sharp from the vinegar, and silky from the oil. Add salt before you chill it; cold dulls salt.","Chill at least 2 hours. Gazpacho is a cold soup, not a room-temperature puree.","If it separates, whisk it again. The bread keeps the oil in suspension.","Serve very cold. It should taste of tomato first, then cucumber and vinegar."]},
"spanish|Paella": {"time":"About 1 hour","steps":["Heat the olive oil in a wide, shallow pan. Brown the chicken thighs and the rabbit and set them aside.","In the same oil fry the chopped tomato with the paprika until the tomato darkens and the oil is red.","Return the meat. Add the green beans. Pour in the hot chicken stock and the saffron, crushed into a spoon of the stock. Simmer 15 minutes.","Scatter the bomba rice in an even layer. Do not stir after this. Add the shrimp and mussels, hinged side down.","Cook on a lively heat until the liquid is absorbed and the rice is tender, about 18 minutes. In the last 2 minutes, raise the heat so a crust, the socarrat, forms on the bottom. You will smell it toasting.","Rest 5 minutes off the heat. Serve from the pan."]},
"spanish|Pan con Tomate": {"time":"About 10 minutes","steps":["Toast the bread until the edges are hard and the centre is still a little soft. Rub one side with the cut garlic.","Halve the tomatoes and grate them on a coarse grater, skin side out, so you get pulp and leave the skin behind.","Spoon the tomato pulp thickly on the bread. Do not soak the bread until it collapses.","Add salt and a thread of olive oil.","Eat at once, while the toast still crackles under the tomato.","The tomato must be ripe. A pale tomato makes wet bread and nothing else."]},
"spanish|Patatas Bravas": {"time":"About 40 minutes","steps":["Cut the potato into chunks. Simmer them in salted water until just tender, then drain and let them steam dry. Wet potatoes will not crisp.","Heat most of the olive oil and fry the potatoes until the edges are deep gold and crisp, about 8 minutes. Drain and salt them.","In a spoon of the oil, fry the garlic, paprika and cayenne for 30 seconds. Add the chopped tomato and cook until you have a thick, sharp sauce, about 10 minutes. Add a pinch of salt.","The sauce should be hot from the cayenne and smoky from the paprika, thick enough to sit on the potato.","Spoon the sauce over the potatoes, or serve it beside so the crust stays crisp.","Eat at once. Bravas are fried potatoes with a piquant tomato sauce, not a stew."]},
"spanish|Pulpo a Feira": {"time":"About 50 minutes","steps":["Freeze the octopus overnight if it is fresh, then thaw it. This tenderises the fibres. Rinse it.","Bring a large pot of unsalted water to a boil. Dip the octopus three times, then submerge it. Simmer gently until a knife slides into the thickest tentacle, about 35 to 45 minutes. Do not boil hard or the skin tears.","Turn off the heat and leave the octopus in the water 10 minutes. Then lift it out and rest it.","Boil the potato in the octopus water until tender. Slice it.","Slice the tentacles into coins. Lay them on the potato. The potato drinks the juices.","Dress with olive oil, smoked paprika and coarse salt. Pulpo a feira is tender octopus, smoky and salty, served warm, not in a sauce."]},
"spanish|Tortilla": {"time":"About 40 minutes","steps":["Slice the potato thinly, and the onion. Cover them with the olive oil in a pan — they are confited, not deep-fried — and cook gently until the potato is soft and the onion is sweet, about 20 minutes. They should not brown hard.","Drain them, saving the oil. Beat the eggs and fold in the warm potato and onion. Leave 5 minutes.","Heat a spoon of the saved oil in a non-stick pan. Pour in the egg. Cook on medium-low until the underside is set and the top is still loose.","Invert onto a plate and slide it back to cook the other side for a minute or two. The centre should be just set, or still slightly wet if you like it that way.","Rest 5 minutes. Cut in wedges."]},
"srilankan|Ambul Thiyal": {"time":"About 40 minutes","steps":["Soak the goraka in the water for 15 minutes, then rub it with your fingers until the water is sour and brown and the pieces are soft. This sourness is the whole point of ambul thiyal. Do not substitute a lot of liquid.","Cut the tuna into thick cubes. Slice the onion and crush the garlic and black pepper together coarsely.","Put the fish, goraka and its soaking water, onion, garlic, pepper, curry leaves, torn pandan and salt into a wide pan. There should be barely enough liquid to come a third of the way up the fish.","Bring it to a simmer, then cook uncovered on medium, turning the pieces once, until the water has nearly gone and the fish is coated in a dry, dark sour paste, about 20 minutes. Do not stir hard or the tuna breaks.","If the pan dries before the fish is cooked through, add a spoon of water, not a cup. Ambul thiyal is a dry curry.","Rest 5 minutes. The pepper should be loud and the goraka sour, with no gravy left to spoon. It keeps well and tastes sharper the next day."]},
"srilankan|Deviled Chicken": {"time":"About 40 minutes","steps":["Cut the chicken into bite-sized pieces and fry or roast it until cooked and browned at the edges. Devilled dishes start with meat that is already cooked.","In the same pan fry the sliced onion until it softens and the edges catch.","Add the tomato ketchup, chilli flakes and soy sauce. Bubble it until the sauce turns sticky and dark.","Toss the chicken back through the sauce until every piece is glazed. It should taste hot, sweet and salty, with a little char.","Serve with rice or bread. The sauce thickens as it cools, so eat it hot."]},
"srilankan|Hoppers": {"time":"About 40 minutes, plus fermenting","steps":["Stir the yeast into warm coconut milk with a spoon of the rice flour. Leave it 15 minutes until foamy.","Whisk in the rest of the rice flour. Add more coconut milk or water until the batter is thinner than pancake batter and pours in a ribbon. Ferment, covered, for 3 to 6 hours until slightly sour and bubbly.","Heat a small curved hopper pan, or a small wok, and heat it well. Pour a ladle of batter and swirl so the sides are thin and a pool sits in the centre.","Cover and cook on medium until the sides are crisp and lacy and the centre is set, about 3 minutes.","For an egg hopper, crack an egg into the soft centre as soon as you swirl, then cover again until the white sets and the yolk is still soft.","Lift out and eat at once with sambol."]},
"srilankan|Kiribath": {"time":"About 35 minutes","steps":["Rinse the rice until the water is almost clear. Soak 15 minutes, then drain. Kiribath is a short-grain or samba-style rice if you have it; any white rice will set if you cook it this way.","Put the rice in a pot with the water and salt. Bring to a boil, then simmer covered until the water is absorbed and the grains are almost cooked, about 12 minutes.","Pour in the coconut milk. Stir once, gently, so you do not smash the grains. Cook on low, covered, until the milk is absorbed and the rice is soft and sticky, about 10 minutes.","Take the lid off and cook 2 minutes more, stirring, until the rice looks glossy and holds together when you press a spoonful.","Tip it into a flat dish and press it into an even slab about 2 cm thick. Let it set 10 minutes.","Cut into diamonds. Kiribath should be moist and coconut-rich, not a dry pulao and not a porridge."]},
"srilankan|Kottu Roti": {"time":"About 30 minutes","steps":["Cut the godamba roti into strips, then chop them smaller. Stale roti chops more cleanly than fresh.","Heat a wide pan or a flat griddle. Fry the chicken, already cooked or cut small, until it takes a little colour. Push it aside.","Add the cabbage and carrot and stir-fry until just tender. Beat in the egg and scramble it through the vegetables.","Add the chopped roti and a splash of water or any curry gravy you have. Chop and toss constantly, the way it is done with the metal blades on a street griddle, until the bread is hot, a little crisp at the edges, and mixed through.","Taste it. Serve at once, piled high."]},
"srilankan|Lamprais": {"time":"About 2 hours","steps":["Cook the samba rice in plenty of water until just done, then mix it with a little of the gravy from the curries so each grain is savoury.","Warm the beef curry and the chicken curry until they are thick, not watery. Lamprais parcels should be moist, not leaking.","Wilt the banana leaves over a flame so they turn glossy and flexible. A raw leaf will crack.","Lay a square of leaf, spread a cup of rice, and top with a spoon of beef curry and a spoon of chicken curry.","Fold into a tight parcel and secure it. Steam the parcels 15 minutes, or bake them wrapped in foil at 180°C for 15 minutes.","Serve in the leaf. The leaf perfumes the rice; do not unwrap it until you eat."]},
"srilankan|Mallung": {"time":"About 20 minutes","steps":["Shred the cabbage very fine, as if for a slaw. Coarse pieces stay raw and wet. Sprinkle with the salt and turmeric and leave 5 minutes, then squeeze firmly. Save none of that water.","Slice the onion thin and chop the green chilli. Flake the Maldive fish with your fingers.","Put the squeezed cabbage in a dry pan on medium heat with the onion, chilli and Maldive fish. Toss for 3 to 4 minutes until the cabbage is just wilted and still bright. Mallung is not a stew.","Take it off the heat. Fold in the grated coconut and the juice of the lime. The residual heat softens the coconut without melting it.","Taste. It should be salty from the fish, sharp from the lime, and only just warm.","Serve the same day. If it sits, the coconut weeps and the cabbage turns grey."]},
"srilankan|Parippu": {"time":"About 35 minutes","steps":["Rinse the red lentils until the water runs clearer. Cover them with water to sit 3 cm above, add the turmeric and half the salt, and simmer until they collapse into a soft mash, about 15 minutes. Whisk them so they are smooth but not glue.","Stir in the coconut milk and the rest of the salt. Simmer gently 5 minutes. Do not boil hard or the coconut splits and the dal turns oily in a dull way.","Slice the onion and chop the garlic. Heat the coconut oil until it shimmers. Add the mustard seeds and wait until they pop.","Add the dried red chilli, curry leaves, onion and garlic. Fry until the onion is soft and the leaves crackle.","Pour this tempering over the dal and cover for 2 minutes so the coconut oil perfumes the pot. Stir once.","The parippu should be thick enough to sit on rice in a soft mound, pale yellow, and gently hot from the chilli."]},
"srilankan|Pol Sambol": {"time":"About 15 minutes","steps":["Pound or pulse the dried red chilli to a coarse paste. The heat of pol sambol lives here; taste a grain before you go further.","Add the chopped red onion and pound it in, then the grated coconut. Work it until the coconut is evenly red, not a smooth puree. It should stay in moist crumbs.","Squeeze in the lime juice and mix. Taste for chilli and lime. It should be sharp enough to wake up rice.","If it looks dry, add a few drops of water. Rest 10 minutes so the onion softens.","Serve with hoppers, rice or string hoppers. It is a relish, not a curry."]},
"srilankan|Watalappan": {"time":"About 1 hour, plus chilling","steps":["Warm the coconut milk with the jaggery and the cardamom pods, stirring, until the jaggery dissolves. Do not boil it hard. Cool until it is warm, not hot, or it will scramble the egg.","Beat the eggs. Pour in the warm coconut milk, whisking. Strain to remove the cardamom pods and any egg threads.","Pour into a bowl that fits inside a steamer, or into small cups. Scatter the cashew nuts on top.","Steam over gently simmering water for 30 to 40 minutes, until a knife comes out clean and the centre only just wobbles. A rolling boil will make it porous.","Cool, then chill. Watalappan is silkier cold. Serve in the bowl it steamed in."]},
"swedish|Gravlax": {"time":"About 20 minutes, plus 2 days curing","steps":["Pat the salmon dry and feel for pin bones. Mix the salt and sugar. This cure draws water out and firms the fish. Too little salt and it spoils; do not reduce it.","Lay half the dill in a dish, put the salmon on it skin side down, cover with the salt-sugar, pepper and the rest of the dill. Wrap tightly.","Weight it and refrigerate for 48 hours, turning it once a day. Liquid will pool; leave it.","Scrape off the cure and dill. The flesh should be firm and translucent, darker at the edges.","Slice thin, on a diagonal, leaving the skin behind.","Stir the mustard with a squeeze of lemon as a sharp sauce beside the sweet-salty fish. Serve cold."]},
"swedish|Janssons Frestelse": {"time":"About 1 hour","steps":["Slice the potato into thin matchsticks and the onion thin. The potato must be raw; parboiling makes a mush.","Butter a baking dish. Layer potato, onion and anchovy, ending with potato. The anchovy is the salt, so add almost no extra salt.","Pour the cream over. It should come nearly to the top of the potato.","Dot with the rest of the butter. Bake at 190°C until the potato is tender and the top is deep gold, about 45 minutes.","Rest 10 minutes. The cream should have been drunk by the potato, leaving a soft centre and a crust.","Janssons is salty, sweet from the onion and cream, and rich. Serve hot."]},
"swedish|Kanelbullar": {"time":"About 2 hours","steps":["Warm the milk and dissolve the yeast. Mix with the flour, salt, cardamom, half the sugar and half the butter. Knead 8 minutes. Rise 45 minutes.","Mix the rest of the butter with the rest of the sugar and the cinnamon.","Roll the dough into a rectangle, spread the cinnamon butter to the edges, and roll it up from the long side.","Cut into pieces and set them cut side up in a tin, or curl them into knots. Rise 30 minutes.","Brush with beaten egg. Bake at 200°C until gold, about 12 minutes.","They should be soft, fragrant with cardamom in the dough and cinnamon in the swirl. Eat warm the day they are baked."]},
"swedish|Meatballs": {"time":"About 45 minutes","steps":["Soak the breadcrumbs in the milk.","Mix them with the ground beef, ground pork, finely chopped onion, egg and allspice. Knead lightly. Roll small balls with wet hands.","Fry in the butter until browned all over and cooked through. Lift them out.","In the same pan, loosen the browned bits with a little water and stir in the cream to make a short gravy. Return the meatballs for a minute.","Serve with the lingonberry jam. The jam is sour against the rich gravy; do not skip it."]},
"swedish|Pea Soup": {"time":"About 1 hour 15 minutes","steps":["Rinse the peas. Cover them with water, bring to a boil, and skim.","Add the whole onion, the pork belly, thyme and salt. Simmer until the peas collapse and the pork is tender, about 1 hour. Stir now and then so the bottom does not catch.","Remove the onion if it is still in a piece, or mash it in. The soup should be thick enough that a spoon stands briefly.","Lift out the pork, slice it, and return it or serve it beside.","Taste the salt. The pork may have seasoned it enough.","Serve with mustard on the side. Swedish pea soup is yellow, thick and mild, the mustard the sharp note."]},
"swedish|Princess Cake": {"time":"About 1 hour 30 minutes, plus chilling","steps":["Whip the eggs with 80 g of the sugar and the salt until thick. Fold in the flour. Bake in a tin at 170°C until just done, about 20 minutes. Cool and split into three layers.","Whip the cream with the rest of the sugar and the vanilla until it holds peaks.","Layer jam, cream and cake. The cream layer should be generous; this is a cream cake, not a jam sponge.","Mound cream on top into a dome.","Roll the marzipan thin, colour it pale green if it is not already, and drape it over the dome, smoothing out folds. Trim the base.","Chill 1 hour. Cut with a hot knife. The marzipan should be a thin coat, the cream cold, the sponge light."]},
"swedish|Pyttipanna": {"time":"About 30 minutes","steps":["Dice the potato small and even. Parboil it 5 minutes, then drain and dry it. Dice the onion, sausage and beef the same size.","Melt the butter and fry the potato until golden and crisp at the edges, about 8 minutes. If you add the meat too soon the potato steams.","Add the onion, sausage and beef. Fry until the onion is sweet and the beef is browned. Season with salt and pepper.","Everything should be in distinct cubes, browned, not a hash of mush.","Fry the eggs separately, yolks still soft.","Serve the pytt with an egg on top. The yolk dresses it. This is a leftovers pan, but the potato must be crisp."]},
"swedish|Raggmunk": {"time":"About 30 minutes","steps":["Peel and grate the potato. Squeeze out some of the liquid, but not all; the starch in that liquid binds the pancake.","Stir in the flour, milk, egg and salt. The batter should be thick with potato shreds.","Heat the butter in a pan. Spread the batter thin.","Fry until the underside is deep gold and crisp, then flip and cook the other side. Low heat makes a pale, sad cake.","The edge should be lacy, the centre tender.","Serve at once with lingonberry jam. The jam is sharp against the salty potato."]},
"swedish|Toast Skagen": {"time":"About 20 minutes","steps":["Chop the shrimp into small pieces, not a paste. Stir with the mayonnaise, chopped dill, a squeeze of lemon and the salt. Taste. It should be sweet from the shrimp and sharp from the lemon.","Chill the mixture for 10 minutes so it firms.","Toast the bread in the butter until the edges are gold and crisp.","Pile the shrimp high on the toast.","Spoon the roe on top.","Eat at once, while the toast is still crisp under the cold shrimp."]},
"swedish|Wallenbergare": {"time":"About 30 minutes","steps":["Keep the veal and cream cold. Mix the mince with the yolks and salt, then beat in the cream a little at a time until the mixture is soft and airy. Warm cream or overmixing makes it grainy.","Shape thick oval cakes with wet hands.","Coat them in breadcrumbs, pressing lightly so the crumbs stick.","Fry in butter on medium until the crust is gold and the centre is just cooked, still creamy, about 4 minutes a side. A high heat burns the crumbs before the inside sets.","Rest 3 minutes.","Wallenbergare should be a soft veal cream inside a thin crust, rich, not a dense burger."]},
"tajik|Chakka": {"time":"About 20 minutes, plus draining","steps":["Stir the salt into the yogurt.","Line a sieve with a clean cloth and pour in the yogurt. Hang or set it over a bowl in the fridge for at least 4 hours, until it is as thick as soft cheese.","The whey that drips out is not part of the dish. Discard it or drink it; do not stir it back.","Crush the garlic and chop the dill. Fold them through the thickened yogurt.","Taste. Chakka should be tangy, thick enough to stand a spoon in, and lightly garlicky.","Serve cold, as a side with bread. It is the fresh form of the dried qurut used in qurutob."]},
"tajik|Fatir": {"time":"About 1 hour 15 minutes","steps":["Mix the flour and salt with the yogurt and enough water to make a soft dough. Knead 5 minutes. Rest 20 minutes.","Divide into 6. Roll each thin, brush with soft butter, and fold or roll into a layered disc. This layering is what makes fatir flake.","Rest 15 minutes, then roll each disc out again, not so thin that the layers vanish.","Bake at 200°C until the bread is gold and the layers have opened at the edge, about 15 minutes.","Cool slightly. Fatir should pull apart in flakes and taste of butter.","Eat warm, with tea or torn into qurutob."]},
"tajik|Laghman": {"time":"About 1 hour 20 minutes","steps":["Knead a firm noodle dough with the flour, a pinch of salt and water. Rest 30 minutes, then pull into thick noodles.","Brown the beef strips in the oil. Add onion, pepper, tomato, garlic, cumin and the rest of the salt.","Add water and simmer until the beef is tender and the sauce is thick, about 40 minutes.","Boil the noodles until chewy. Drain.","Serve the noodles with the sauce ladled over.","The noodles should have a bite, the sauce savoury and full of pepper and tomato."]},
"tajik|Mantu": {"time":"About 1 hour 40 minutes","steps":["Make a firm dough with the flour, a pinch of salt and water. Rest 20 minutes.","Grate the onion and pumpkin. Mix with the lamb, pepper and the rest of the salt.","Roll thin squares, fill, and pinch into parcels.","Steam 35 minutes, until the dough is glossy and the meat is cooked.","The pumpkin should keep the filling moist. If the parcels look dry, the filling was short of onion.","Serve with yogurt. Tajik mantu are steamed dumplings, eaten hot, the dough tender."]},
"tajik|Narin": {"time":"About 1 hour 30 minutes","steps":["Cover the meat with water, add half the salt, and simmer until it is very tender, about 1 hour. Cool it in the broth, then slice it thin. Keep the broth.","Slice the onion very thin. Salt it and scald it with a ladle of the hot broth. Leave 10 minutes, then squeeze it gently. The onion turns sweet.","Toss the sliced meat with the onion, the rest of the salt and the pepper. Add a spoon of broth so it is moist, not wet.","Pack it into a bowl and let it sit 15 minutes so the onion juices season the meat.","Narin is served cool or just warm, the meat tender, the onion no longer harsh.","Taste the pepper. It should be savoury and onion-sweet, a meat salad, not a stew."]},
"tajik|Plov": {"time":"About 2 hours","steps":["Rinse the rice. Cut the lamb large, the carrot into sticks, the onion into wedges.","Brown the lamb in a hot dry pot until its fat renders. Add the onion, then the carrot, and fry until the carrot sweetens. Add the cumin.","Cover with hot water, tuck in the whole garlic and the soaked chickpeas, and simmer until the lamb is nearly done.","Spread the rice over the top. Add boiling water to just cover the rice. Cook until the water sinks, then cover and steam on low for 20 minutes without stirring.","Rest, then fold from the bottom. Serve the garlic whole."]},
"tajik|Qurutob": {"time":"About 40 minutes","steps":["Break the fatir into pieces and lay it in a wide bowl. Fatir is a flaky flatbread; if yours is soft, toast it until it has some crust.","Soak the qurut in warm water and mash it until you have a pourable sour yogurt sauce. Strain lumps. Qurut is dried yogurt; the sourness is the dish.","Slice the onion and fry it in the oil until deep gold. Add the chopped tomato and salt and cook to a thick sauce.","Pour the warm qurut over the bread so it softens but does not turn to porridge.","Spoon the onion and tomato over the bread. Slice the cucumber on top and scatter coriander.","Eat with your hand from the shared bowl. Qurutob should be sour, the bread still in pieces, the onion sweet and brown."]},
"tajik|Sambusa": {"time":"About 1 hour","steps":["Make a firm dough with the flour, a pinch of salt, a spoon of oil and water. Rest 20 minutes.","Chop the onion fine and mix with the beef, cumin and the rest of the salt.","Roll the dough thin, cut rounds, fill, and fold into half-moons. Seal the edges hard.","Fry in medium-hot oil until gold and crisp, about 4 minutes.","Drain. Break one open: the beef should be cooked and the onion soft.","Eat hot. The crust should flake, not chew like bread."]},
"tajik|Shakarob": {"time":"About 15 minutes","steps":["Slice the onion thin, rinse it, and squeeze it dry.","Dice the tomato. Slice the chilli.","Toss with the salt and leave 10 minutes so the tomato makes a juice.","Fold in the coriander.","Taste. Shakarob is a fresh onion and tomato salad, juicy and sharp.","Serve the same hour, beside plov or grilled meat."]},
"tajik|Shurbo": {"time":"About 1 hour 30 minutes","steps":["Soak the chickpeas overnight. Cut the lamb large.","Cover the lamb with water, skim the boil, and add cumin and salt. Simmer 40 minutes, adding the chickpeas partway.","Add potato in large pieces, carrot, onion wedges and tomato. Simmer until everything is tender, about 30 minutes.","Keep the broth clear by simmering, not boiling hard.","Taste the salt.","Finish with coriander. Shurbo is a lamb soup with whole vegetables, served in deep bowls."]},
"thai|Green Curry": {"time":"About 35 minutes","steps":["Shake the coconut milk. Spoon the thick part into a pan and fry the green curry paste in it until the coconut fat beads and the paste smells fragrant, about 3 minutes.","Add the chicken thigh, cut into pieces, and stir until it is sealed.","Pour in the rest of the coconut milk. Simmer gently until the chicken is cooked, about 12 minutes.","Add the Thai eggplant and bamboo shoots and cook until the eggplant is just tender.","Season with fish sauce. Stir in the Thai basil off the heat so it stays bright. Serve with rice."]},
"thai|Larb": {"time":"About 25 minutes","steps":["Toast the rice powder in a dry pan if it is not already toasted, until it smells nutty. This is what makes larb sandy and fragrant rather than just a mince.","Cook the minced pork in a dry pan with a splash of water, breaking it up, until it is just cooked and not browned hard. Pour off excess fat.","Off the heat, dress it with the lime juice and fish sauce. It should taste sour and salty while it is still warm.","Fold in the sliced shallot, mint and cilantro, then the toasted rice powder.","Serve warm, not hot, with raw vegetables or sticky rice. The herbs should still look fresh."]},
"thai|Mango Sticky Rice": {"time":"About 45 minutes, plus soaking","steps":["Soak the glutinous rice for at least 4 hours, or overnight. Drain it. Sticky rice steamed from dry grains stays hard in the middle.","Steam the rice over boiling water for 20 to 25 minutes, until the grains are translucent and tender. Flip the rice once halfway so the top cooks. Do not boil it in water or it turns porridge.","While it steams, warm the coconut milk with the sugar and salt until the sugar dissolves. Do not boil it. Taste: it should be sweet and a little salty. Salty coconut is what keeps the dessert from being flat.","Tip the hot rice into a bowl and pour over three-quarters of the coconut milk. Fold gently, cover, and let it drink the milk for 15 minutes. The rice should look glossy and swollen, not wet and soupy. If liquid remains, steam it 5 minutes more, covered, off the heat.","Simmer the remaining coconut milk a few minutes until it thickens slightly. This is the sauce.","Peel and slice the mango. Toast the sesame seeds in a dry pan until they smell nutty. Serve the rice warm or at room temperature beside the cold mango, with the coconut sauce and sesame on top. The mango must be ripe and fragrant or the dish is only sweet rice."]},
"thai|Massaman Curry": {"time":"About 1 hour 30 minutes","steps":["Cut the beef chuck into large chunks. Fry the massaman curry paste in the thick part of the coconut milk until the fat separates.","Add the beef and stir until it is coated. Pour in the rest of the coconut milk and enough water to cover. Simmer gently until the beef is nearly tender, about 1 hour.","Add the potato, cut large, and the onion wedges. Cook until the potato is soft. The onion should still have a little shape.","Stir in the tamarind paste and the roasted peanuts. Simmer 5 minutes. The sauce should be rich, a little sweet-sour, and thick.","Rest 10 minutes. Serve with rice. Massaman is milder and rounder than a green curry; do not rush the beef."]},
"thai|Pad Krapow": {"time":"About 20 minutes","steps":["Pound the garlic and Thai chilli together to a coarse paste. This goes in first so the oil takes the heat.","Mix the soy sauce, oyster sauce, fish sauce and sugar in a cup. Have it ready. Pad krapow is a one-minute stir-fry once the pan is hot; you cannot stop to measure.","Heat a wok until it smokes. Add the oil, then the garlic-chilli paste. Fry 15 seconds until fragrant, not brown.","Add the minced chicken and stir-fry, breaking it up, until it is browned and a little crisp at the edges, about 4 minutes. Pour in the sauce and toss until it glazes the meat.","Throw in the holy basil and toss until it just wilts, about 15 seconds. Take the wok off the heat. Thai basil or holy basil must stay green and smell sweet, not fried black.","In a second pan, fry the eggs in a little of the oil until the whites are lacy and the yolks are still runny. Serve the chicken with an egg on top. The yolk is the sauce."]},
"thai|Pad Thai": {"time":"About 30 minutes","steps":["Soak the flat rice noodles in warm water until they bend but are not soft. Drain. They finish in the pan.","Stir the tamarind paste, fish sauce and palm sugar together until the sugar dissolves. Taste it: sour, salty, then sweet.","Heat a wok. Fry the shrimp until just pink and set them aside. Scramble the egg in the same wok, then push it aside.","Add the noodles and the sauce. Toss on high heat until the noodles are tender and stained. Add the firm tofu and the shrimp.","Fold in the bean sprouts. Serve with roasted peanuts, more bean sprouts and a lime wedge. The lime is squeezed on at the end, not cooked in."]},
"thai|Satay": {"time":"About 40 minutes, plus marinating","steps":["Cut the chicken breast into strips. Mix half the coconut milk with the turmeric. Marinate the chicken for at least 30 minutes.","Thread the strips onto the bamboo skewers. Soak the skewers first if you are using an open flame.","Grill or pan-fry, brushing with a little more coconut milk, until just cooked and marked.","For the sauce, warm the peanut butter with the remaining coconut milk and the tamarind paste until it is thick enough to cling to the meat. Loosen with water if it seizes.","Serve the skewers with the warm sauce."]},
"thai|Som Tam": {"time":"About 20 minutes","steps":["Peel and shred the green papaya into long threads. It must be truly green and crisp. Soak it in cold water for 5 minutes and drain.","In a mortar, pound the Thai chilli, then the long bean cut into short lengths, then the cherry tomato, just until bruised, not pureed.","Add the palm sugar, fish sauce and lime juice. Pound and mix until the sugar dissolves. Taste: sour, salty, hot, with a little sweet.","Toss in the papaya and pound lightly so it wilts but stays crunchy. Fold in the roasted peanuts.","Serve at once, with sticky rice or as it is. It weeps if it sits."]},
"thai|Tom Kha Gai": {"time":"About 30 minutes","steps":["Slice the chicken thigh thin. Bruise the lemongrass and cut it into short lengths. Slice the galangal into coins. Tear the kaffir lime leaves. These three are the perfume; they are simmered, not eaten in big pieces.","Heat the thick part of the coconut milk with the galangal, lemongrass and lime leaves until it steams and the coconut fat beads, about 4 minutes. Do not brown them.","Add the chicken and the mushrooms. Pour in the rest of the coconut milk and 150 ml of water. Simmer gently until the chicken is just cooked, about 8 minutes. A hard boil makes the coconut grainy and the chicken tough.","Take the pot off the heat. Stir in the fish sauce, the juice of the lime, and the bruised Thai chilli.","Taste. Tom kha should be rich, sour and salty in that order, with heat you can see in the chilli, not a thick curry.","Scatter the coriander. Serve at once. If it sits on the heat after the lime goes in, the lime turns bitter."]},
"thai|Tom Yum": {"time":"About 30 minutes","steps":["Bruise the lemongrass and slice it. Slice the galangal. Tear the kaffir lime leaves. These go in whole and are not eaten.","Simmer them in water for 10 minutes until the broth smells of citrus. Add the straw mushroom and Thai chilli.","Add the shrimp and cook until they just turn pink, 2 minutes. Do not boil them longer.","Take the pot off the heat. Stir in the fish sauce, lime juice and coconut milk. Boiling after the lime goes in makes it taste dull and can split the coconut.","Taste: it must be sour and hot first, salty second. Serve with the herbs still in the bowl."]},
"turkish|Baklava": {"time":"About 1 hour 30 minutes","steps":["Chop the pistachios and walnuts, leaving some texture. Clarify the butter, or melt it and let the white solids sink.","Brush a tin with butter. Lay phyllo sheets one at a time, brushing each with butter. After several sheets, scatter nuts. Repeat the layers, finishing with plain buttered phyllo.","Cut into diamonds before baking, cutting all the way through.","Bake at 170°C until deep gold, about 40 to 50 minutes.","Meanwhile boil the sugar with water and the rose water to a light syrup and cool it. Pour the cool syrup over the hot baklava. Let it stand for hours so the layers drink the syrup and stay crisp."]},
"turkish|Dolma": {"time":"About 1 hour 30 minutes","steps":["Rinse the grape leaves if they are packed in brine, and blanch them 1 minute so they soften. Cut off the hard stems.","Slice the onion and cook it in the olive oil until soft and sweet. Add the rice, pine nuts, currants, salt and sugar and stir 2 minutes. Add 100 ml of water, cover, and cook 8 minutes until the rice is half done. Stir in the dill and mint. Cool.","Lay a leaf shiny side down. Place a thin line of filling at the stem end and roll tightly, tucking the sides. A loose roll falls apart; a fat roll stays hard in the middle.","Pack the rolls seam side down in a pot, close together. Tuck leftover leaves on top. Add the juice of the lemon and water to barely cover. Weight them with a plate.","Simmer gently for 40 minutes, until the rice is tender and the leaves are silky. Most of the water should be gone.","Cool in the pot. Dolma are eaten at room temperature, glossy with olive oil, the rice scented with dill and pine nut."]},
"turkish|Imam Bayildi": {"time":"About 1 hour","steps":["Halve the eggplants lengthwise. Score the flesh, salt them, and leave 20 minutes. Pat dry. Fry them in half the olive oil until the skins wrinkle and the flesh is soft. You are not looking for a dark crust all over.","Slice the onion thin. Cook it in the rest of the oil, very slowly, until it is sweet and pale gold, about 15 minutes. This onion is the soul of the dish.","Add the chopped garlic, chopped tomato, sugar and salt. Cook until the tomato melts into a thick, sweet sauce, about 10 minutes. Stir in the parsley.","Set the eggplants in a snug pan, slit them open, and fill them with the onion-tomato mixture. Pour any leftover sauce and a spoon of water around them.","Cover and cook on low for 25 minutes, until the eggplants have drunk the oil and sauce and are silky all the way through.","Cool to room temperature. Imam bayildi is better warm than hot, glossy with olive oil, the onion sweet, the eggplant collapsed and not bitter."]},
"turkish|Kebab": {"time":"About 45 minutes, plus marinating","steps":["Cut the lamb into cubes. Mix it with the grated onion, garlic, cumin and a spoon of the yogurt. Marinate at least 1 hour.","Thread onto skewers. Grill over high heat, turning, until browned and still juicy.","Warm the flatbread on the grill for a few seconds.","Chop the tomato. Dust the meat with sumac.","Serve the kebab on the bread with tomato and the rest of the yogurt."]},
"turkish|Kunefe": {"time":"About 40 minutes","steps":["Melt the butter. Pull the kataifi apart so it is fluffy, then toss it with the butter until every strand is coated.","Press half the strands into a small pan. Lay on the cheese in an even layer, leaving a small border. Cover with the rest of the strands and press firmly.","Cook on medium, covered, until the underside is deep gold, about 8 minutes. Invert onto a plate and slide it back to colour the other side, about 6 minutes. The cheese should be molten and stretching.","While it cooks, boil the sugar with 100 ml of water and a strip of lemon peel for 5 minutes to a light syrup. Take it off the heat.","Pour the warm syrup over the hot kunefe. It should hiss and soak in. Cold syrup sits on top.","Scatter chopped pistachios. Rest 3 minutes so the syrup settles, then cut. The outside must be crisp, the centre a stretch of cheese."]},
"turkish|Lahmacun": {"time":"About 1 hour 15 minutes","steps":["Mix the flour with the yeast, half the salt, a spoon of the olive oil and 170 ml of warm water. Knead 8 minutes to a soft dough. Cover and rise 45 minutes.","Chop the tomato, pepper, onion and parsley very fine. Mix with the lamb, pepper paste and the rest of the salt. The topping must be fine and spreadable, not a chunky stew. Squeeze out extra tomato water.","Divide the dough into 8 balls. Roll each very thin, about 20 cm across.","Spread a thin layer of the lamb mixture edge to edge, leaving no border. A thick topping steams the bread.","Bake on the hottest oven floor or a preheated tray at 250°C until the edges blister and the meat is just cooked, about 6 minutes.","Eat at once, rolled up. Lahmacun should be crisp at the rim, the meat juicy, and smell of pepper paste and parsley."]},
"turkish|Manti": {"time":"About 1 hour 45 minutes","steps":["Make a firm dough with the flour, egg, a pinch of salt and about 100 ml of water. Knead until smooth. Rest 20 minutes.","Grate the onion and squeeze it dry. Mix it with the lamb and half the salt.","Roll the dough very thin and cut 2 cm squares. Put a pinch of filling on each, then bring the four corners together and pinch them shut. The dumplings should be tiny. Large manti are a different dish.","Boil them in salted water until they float and the dough is cooked, about 8 minutes. Lift them gently.","Crush the garlic with the rest of the salt and stir it into the yogurt. Melt the butter with the pepper paste until the butter turns red.","Put the dumplings on a plate, spoon over the garlic yogurt, and streak with the pepper butter. Manti is eaten hot, the dumpling small enough that several fit on a spoon."]},
"turkish|Mercimek Corbasi": {"time":"About 40 minutes","steps":["Dice the onion, carrot and potato small so they disappear into the soup.","Melt the butter and cook the onion until soft. Add the pepper paste and cumin and fry 1 minute.","Add the carrot, potato and rinsed lentils. Cover with water by 4 cm. Add the salt.","Simmer until the lentils have collapsed and the vegetables are soft, about 25 minutes.","Blend until smooth, or mash with a spoon if you want a little texture. Loosen with hot water until the soup pours in a ribbon. Taste the salt.","Serve with lemon. Mercimek corbasi should be thick, warm and cumin-scented, red-gold from the paste, not a thin broth."]},
"turkish|Pide": {"time":"About 1 hour 20 minutes","steps":["Knead the flour with the yeast, half the salt, the olive oil and 200 ml of warm water for 8 minutes. Rise, covered, 45 minutes.","Chop the onion, tomato and green pepper fine. Mix with the minced beef and the rest of the salt.","Divide the dough into 4. Roll each into a long oval.","Spread the meat mixture down the centre, leaving a border. Fold the long edges up and pinch the ends into a boat so the filling stays in.","Bake at 240°C for 10 minutes. Crack an egg onto two of the boats if you like, or brush the crust with beaten egg, and bake 4 minutes more until the crust is gold and the egg is just set.","Rest 3 minutes. Pide should be soft in the middle, crisp at the pinched ends, the meat cooked through and still moist."]},
"turkish|Shakshuka": {"time":"About 30 minutes","steps":["Heat the olive oil. Fry the sliced onion and red bell pepper until soft.","Add the garlic and cumin and cook 1 minute. Add the chopped tomato and simmer until the sauce is thick, about 12 minutes.","Make wells in the sauce and crack in the eggs. Cover and cook until the whites are set and the yolks are still soft.","Crumble the feta over the top.","Serve from the pan with bread to swipe the sauce."]},
"uzbek|Achichuk": {"time":"About 15 minutes","steps":["Slice the onion thin and rinse it under cold water. Squeeze it dry so it is sweet, not harsh.","Slice the tomato into thin wedges. Slice the chilli.","Toss the onion, tomato and chilli with the salt and leave 10 minutes. The salt pulls juice from the tomato, which dresses the salad.","Taste. It should be juicy, sharp and fresh.","Scatter coriander.","Achichuk is the salad served with plov, eaten the hour it is made, before the tomato collapses."]},
"uzbek|Chuchvara": {"time":"About 1 hour 20 minutes","steps":["Make a firm dough with the flour, a pinch of salt and water. Rest 20 minutes.","Chop the onion fine and mix it with the beef, pepper and the rest of the salt.","Roll the dough thin, cut small squares, and fold each into a tiny triangle or parcel around a pinch of filling.","Boil in salted water until they float and the dough is cooked, about 6 minutes.","Crush the garlic into the yogurt with a pinch of salt.","Serve the dumplings with the garlic yogurt. Chuchvara are small; several should fit in a spoon, the filling juicy, the dough thin."]},
"uzbek|Dimlama": {"time":"About 1 hour 30 minutes","steps":["Slice everything thick: lamb, potato, carrot, cabbage, onion and tomato.","In a heavy pot, layer onion, lamb, carrot, potato, cabbage and tomato, seasoning each layer with salt, cumin and a little garlic. Do not stir. Dimlama cooks in layers.","Add 100 ml of water only. Cover tightly.","Cook on low for 1 hour 15 minutes. The vegetables and meat steam in their own juices. If it smells dry, add a spoon of water, not a cup.","It is done when the potato is soft and the lamb is tender. Do not stir before then or you break the layers.","Serve from the pot, a portion of each layer. Dimlama is a steam-stew, moist but not soupy."]},
"uzbek|Lagman": {"time":"About 1 hour 30 minutes","steps":["Make a firm dough with the flour, a pinch of salt and water. Knead until elastic. Rest 30 minutes. Roll into ropes and pull them into thick noodles. Rest them so they do not snap.","Cut the beef into strips. Heat the oil and brown the beef. Add the sliced onion, then the pepper and tomato. Add cumin, garlic and salt.","Add water to make a thick sauce and simmer until the beef is tender, about 40 minutes. The sauce should be hearty, not a thin soup.","Boil the noodles in plenty of water until chewy and cooked through, about 5 minutes. Drain.","Put noodles in bowls and ladle the beef sauce over them. Slice the radish thin and serve it raw on the side for crunch.","Lagman noodles should have a bite. If they are soft and swollen, they boiled too long."]},
"uzbek|Manti": {"time":"About 1 hour 45 minutes","steps":["Make a firm dough with the flour, salt and water. Rest 20 minutes.","Chop the onion and grate the pumpkin. Mix with the lamb, cumin, pepper and salt. The pumpkin keeps the filling moist.","Roll the dough thin and cut squares. Place a spoon of filling in the centre and pinch the four corners together, leaving a small opening or sealing them fully.","Steam over boiling water for 35 to 40 minutes. The dough should look glossy and the lamb be cooked. Do not boil them; Uzbek manti are steamed.","Lift them carefully so the skins do not tear.","Serve hot. The dumpling should be juicy when cut, the onion sweet, the dough tender."]},
"uzbek|Non": {"time":"About 2 hours","steps":["Mix the flour, yeast and salt with 230 ml of warm water and the oil. Knead 10 minutes to a smooth dough. Rise, covered, 1 hour.","Divide into 2 rounds. Flatten them, then press the centre thin and leave a fat rim, the shape of a lepyoshka.","Stamp the centre with a fork or a bread stamp. Brush with water and press on sesame and nigella.","Rise 20 minutes. Bake at 220°C until the rim is gold and the centre is cooked, about 15 minutes.","The centre should be chewy, the rim soft and thick, the seeds toasted.","Cool on a rack. Non is the everyday bread, torn, not sliced, and best the day it is baked."]},
"uzbek|Plov": {"time":"About 2 hours","steps":["Soak the devzira rice if it needs it; rinse it until the water is clear. Cut the lamb into chunks and the carrot into thick sticks, not fine shreds. Uzbek plov carrot stays in pieces.","Heat a kazan or heavy pot until it is very hot, then let the lamb fat start to render. Brown the lamb well. Add the sliced onion and cook until golden, then the carrot. Fry the carrot until it softens and sweetens, 10 minutes. Add the cumin.","Pour in hot water to cover the meat, add the garlic head whole, the chickpeas (soaked overnight and drained), barberries and raisins. Simmer until the lamb is nearly tender.","Pile the rice on top and add boiling water to sit about 1 cm above the rice. Do not stir. Cook uncovered until the water is almost gone, then cover and steam on low for 20 minutes.","Rest 10 minutes. Stir from the bottom so lamb, carrot and rice mix. The garlic is squeezed out at the table."]},
"uzbek|Samsa": {"time":"About 1 hour 15 minutes","steps":["Make a dough with the flour, a pinch of salt and water. Knead until smooth. Rest 20 minutes.","Chop the onion fine and mix it with the lamb, cumin, pepper and the rest of the salt. The onion should be plentiful; it steams the meat inside the pastry.","Roll the dough thin. Cut squares, brush with soft butter, and stack three squares, butter between them, then roll them together so the pastry will be flaky.","Cut rounds from the stack, fill with lamb, and bring the edges up into a triangle or a round purse. Seal firmly.","Bake at 200°C until deep gold, about 25 minutes. The lamb must be cooked through and the onion sweet.","Rest 5 minutes. Samsa should flake when broken, the inside juicy, not dry."]},
"uzbek|Shashlik": {"time":"About 30 minutes, plus marinating","steps":["Cut the lamb into large cubes, leaving a little fat. Slice the onion.","Toss the lamb with the onion, cumin, salt, pepper and vinegar. Rest at least 2 hours. The onion juices are the marinade.","Thread the lamb on wide skewers, not packed so tight that the inside steams. Shake off the onion or thread a little between the cubes.","Grill or broil on high, turning, until the outside is charred and the centre is still pink and juicy, about 8 to 10 minutes. Well-done shashlik is a dry mistake.","Rest 5 minutes.","Serve with the raw onion from the marinade, squeezed of its juice, for freshness against the smoke."]},
"uzbek|Shurpa": {"time":"About 1 hour 40 minutes","steps":["Soak the chickpeas overnight. Cut the lamb into large pieces.","Cover the lamb with cold water, bring to a boil, and skim. Add the cumin and salt. Simmer 45 minutes. Add the chickpeas after 15 minutes.","Add the whole or halved potato, large pieces of carrot, the onion in wedges and the tomato. Simmer until the vegetables are soft and the lamb is tender, about 30 minutes.","The broth should stay clear. A hard boil clouds it.","Taste the salt. Shurpa is a lamb soup with vegetables you can see, not a blended potage.","Finish with coriander. Serve in deep bowls, meat and potato in each one."]},
"vietnamese|Banh Cuon": {"time":"About 1 hour","steps":["Soak the wood ear until soft, then chop it fine. Fry the sliced shallot in the oil until gold. Add the pork and wood ear and cook until the pork is brown and dry. Season with fish sauce, black pepper and a pinch of salt. Cool the filling. A wet filling soaks through the sheet.","Whisk the rice flour with the salt and 500 ml of water. The batter must be thin and lump-free. Rest 15 minutes.","Heat a non-stick pan and wipe it with a drop of oil. Pour a small ladle of batter and swirl it into a skin so thin you can see the pan through it.","Cover for 30 seconds. When the sheet looks set and glossy, lift it onto an oiled plate. Do not flip it.","Spoon a line of pork filling across the sheet and roll it loosely. The sheet should be tender, almost slippery, not a firm crepe.","Repeat, wiping the pan each time. Serve the rolls with the fried shallot scattered over them. Banh cuon is eaten the hour it is made; the sheets dry and crack if they wait."]},
"vietnamese|Banh Mi": {"time":"About 30 minutes if the pork is ready","steps":["Warm the Vietnamese baguette until the crust crackles and the inside is still soft. Split it, but not all the way through.","Slice the pork belly and warm it so the fat softens. Slice the cha lua.","Spread pate on one cut side. Layer pork belly and cha lua.","Add the pickled daikon and carrot, well drained, then cucumber, cilantro and jalapeño.","Close the bread and press lightly. Eat soon; the crust dies if the pickles soak it."]},
"vietnamese|Banh Xeo": {"time":"About 45 minutes","steps":["Whisk the rice flour with the coconut milk, turmeric, salt and 150 ml of water. The batter should be thin, like crepe batter. Rest 20 minutes. Stir in the chopped scallion.","Slice the pork belly thin and the shrimp in half lengthwise. Season both with a pinch of the salt.","Heat a non-stick pan until very hot and add a spoon of oil. Lay in a few slices of pork and shrimp and fry until the pork fat renders and the shrimp turn pink.","Pour a ladle of batter over them and swirl to a thin round. Scatter bean sprouts on one half.","Cover for 1 minute, then uncover and cook until the edge is lacy and deep gold and the centre is crisp enough to crack. Fold it over the sprouts.","Slide it onto lettuce. Banh xeo should shatter, not bend. Eat at once, while the crust is still loud."]},
"vietnamese|Bun Bo Hue": {"time":"About 2 hours","steps":["Bruise the lemongrass. Put the beef shank in a pot with water to cover by 5 cm, half the lemongrass and the salt. Simmer until the shank is tender, about 1 hour 30 minutes. Skim. Lift the meat out and slice it. Keep the broth.","Slice the shallot and chop the garlic. Fry them in a spoon of the chilli oil until gold. Add the shrimp paste and the rest of the lemongrass and fry until the paste smells sweet, not raw, about 2 minutes.","Stir this into the broth with the fish sauce and the rest of the chilli oil. Simmer 15 minutes. The broth should be deep and spicy, with a savoury funk from the shrimp paste. Strain out the lemongrass.","Cook the rice noodles until just tender, rinse, and drain.","Put noodles in deep bowls, lay on the sliced shank, and ladle over the boiling broth.","Finish with scallion and a squeeze of lime. Bun bo Hue is a spicy beef noodle soup, red from the chilli oil, and the lime belongs in the bowl, not on the side only."]},
"vietnamese|Bun Cha": {"time":"About 45 minutes","steps":["Slice the pork belly. Season the ground pork with a little of the fish sauce and shape small patties.","Grill or pan-fry the belly and the patties until browned and cooked through. The char is the point.","Mix the fish sauce with the rice vinegar and water, tasting until the fish sauce and vinegar balance. This is the dipping broth, served warm.","Put the grilled pork into bowls of the broth. Serve the rice vermicelli, lettuce and mint on a separate plate.","At the table, take pork, noodle and herbs in one bite. The broth is for dipping and spooning, not for drowning the noodles."]},
"vietnamese|Caramel Pork": {"time":"About 50 minutes","steps":["Cut the pork belly into chunks, skin left on if it is there.","Put the sugar in a heavy pot with a spoon of water. Cook without stirring until it melts and turns the colour of tea, not black. If it smells bitter, it has gone too far; start again.","Add the pork and the sliced shallot. Stir until the meat is coated in the caramel.","Add the fish sauce and coconut water. Cover and simmer until the pork is tender and the sauce is a dark, sticky syrup, about 35 to 40 minutes. Uncover at the end to reduce.","Rest 5 minutes. The fat should be glossy, the sauce salty-sweet. Serve with rice."]},
"vietnamese|Cha Ca": {"time":"About 35 minutes, plus marinating","steps":["Cut the fish into bite-sized pieces. Pound the galangal with the turmeric, shrimp paste and salt to a paste. Coat the fish and rest it 30 minutes. The fish should turn gold from the turmeric.","Cook the rice noodles, rinse, and drain. Chop the peanuts. Cut the dill and scallion into short lengths.","Heat the oil in a pan until hot. Fry the fish until just cooked and the edges colour, about 3 minutes. Do not move it too soon or it sticks and breaks.","Throw in the dill and scallion and toss for 30 seconds until they wilt and smell sweet. The herbs are cooked with the fish, not sprinkled raw.","Serve the fish still in the pan if you can, with the noodles and peanuts alongside.","Each person puts noodles in a bowl, then fish, dill and peanuts. Cha ca should taste of turmeric, dill and the gentle funk of shrimp paste, the fish still moist."]},
"vietnamese|Com Tam": {"time":"About 50 minutes","steps":["Broken rice is the point of com tam: the short grains drink the sauce. Rinse it and steam or boil it until just tender, then spread it so it stays separate.","Pound the garlic with half the sugar, the soy sauce and a spoon of the fish sauce. Coat the pork chops and rest them 20 minutes.","Heat the oil and fry the chops on medium until browned and just cooked through, about 4 minutes a side. Rest them, then slice.","In the same pan, fry the eggs sunny side up.","Mix the rest of the fish sauce and sugar with 3 spoons of water and the chopped scallion. Warm it until the sugar dissolves. This nuoc cham is salty-sweet, not a curry.","Serve the rice with the sliced chop, the egg and cucumber, and the scallion sauce spooned over the rice so the broken grains glaze. The chop should be caramelised at the edge and still juicy."]},
"vietnamese|Pho": {"time":"About 6 hours","steps":["Char the onion and ginger over a flame until the skins blacken. Rinse off the ash. Blanch the beef bones, dump the water, and rinse them.","Cover the bones with fresh water. Add the charred onion and ginger, star anise and cinnamon. Simmer, barely bubbling, for 4 to 6 hours. Skim at the start. Season the broth with fish sauce only at the end.","In the last hour, add the beef brisket and simmer until tender. Slice it thin. Slice the rare steak paper thin and keep it raw.","Soak or boil the flat rice noodles until just tender. Drain.","Put noodles and raw steak in a bowl. Ladle over the boiling broth so the steak turns grey-pink. Add brisket, bean sprouts, Thai basil and lime. The lime and herbs stay raw."]},
"vietnamese|Spring Rolls": {"time":"About 40 minutes","steps":["Cook the rice vermicelli, rinse, and drain well. Slice the pork belly. Halve the shrimp if they are large.","Set out lettuce, mint, vermicelli, pork and shrimp so you can roll in a line.","Dip a rice paper wrapper in warm water for a second, until it just softens. Lay it flat. Too long in the water and it tears.","Lay lettuce, mint, a small bundle of vermicelli, pork and shrimp. Fold the sides in and roll tightly.","Serve at once with the hoisin-peanut dipping sauce. The papers stick to each other, so do not stack them while wet."]}
};
